var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#estimate-coil-sensitivities","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"Reconstruction types that reconstruct one image from the data of several coils, such as \"multiCoil\", need the sensitivity of each coil as a map. espirit from MRICoilSensitivities estimates these maps with ESPIRiT from a fully sampled region in the center of Cartesian k-space, the calibration region. This guide shows how to choose the calibration region, mask voxels without signal, use a separate calibration scan or calibration data in an array, and handle 3D and non-Cartesian data. Multi-Coil Reconstruction uses such maps in a reconstruction.","title":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Estimating-the-Maps","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"The example uses the knee data from Direct Reconstruction, which has eight coils. As in Multi-Coil Reconstruction, it is undersampled such that the 30 profiles in the center of k-space are kept:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling, MRICoilSensitivities\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nacqDataFull = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")))\nacqData = sample_kspace(acqDataFull, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30, seed = 1234)\n\nsensitivities = espirit(acqData, (6, 6), 30)\nsize(sensitivities)\n(320, 320, 1, 8)\n\nThe second argument is the size of the ESPIRiT kernel and the third one the size of the calibration region, here 30 × 30 samples. For 2D data, they default to (6, 6) and 24. The result has the dimensions x, y, z and coils, where z counts the slices of 2D data. This is the layout that params[:senseMaps] expects. The maps are complex and normalized: in each voxel that is not masked (see below), the squared magnitudes of all coils add up to one. The following figure shows the magnitude of the first four maps:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1000, 290))\nfor coil in 1:4\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, coil], title = \"Coil $coil\", aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(sensitivities[:, :, 1, coil])', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Estimating the Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Choosing-the-Calibration-Region","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"espirit takes the calibration data from the ncalib × ncalib samples in the center of k-space, where ncalib is the third argument. This region must be fully sampled and at least as large as the kernel. For undersampled data, choose it no larger than the fully sampled center, here the 30 profiles kept by calsize. With a larger value, the calibration data contains samples that were not measured, and the maps become less accurate. Without a fully sampled center, the maps are wrong. A region that is much smaller than the default also gives less accurate maps.","title":"Choosing the Calibration Region"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Masking-Voxels-without-Signal","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"For each voxel, ESPIRiT computes an eigenvalue, which is close to one in voxels that contain signal. espirit sets the maps to zero in all voxels whose eigenvalue is below the keyword eigThresh_2, which defaults to 0.95. A higher threshold masks more voxels, and eigThresh_2 = 0 keeps the maps everywhere:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (760, 290))\nfor (i, threshold) in enumerate((0, 0.95, 0.99))\n    maps = espirit(acqData, (6, 6), 30, eigThresh_2 = threshold)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = \"eigThresh_2 = $threshold\", aspect = DataAspect(),\n              yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(maps[:, :, 1, 1])', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nIn this slice, the default masks only some patches of the background, and 0.99 masks a large part of it. A threshold that is too high also masks voxels inside the object. The data does not constrain the image in masked voxels: with masked maps, the CG-SENSE reconstruction of Multi-Coil Reconstruction is zero in these voxels, whereas its reconstruction with wavelet regularization is not.","title":"Masking Voxels without Signal"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Using-a-Separate-Calibration-Scan","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"The maps can also be estimated from a separate scan with a lower resolution that covers the same field of view. Pass the size of the maps as the fourth argument, and espirit computes them on this grid instead of the grid of the calibration scan. Choose the calibration region no larger than this scan. In the example, the central 64 × 64 samples of the knee data stand in for such a scan:\n\nkspace = kDataCart(acqDataFull)\ncalibScan = AcquisitionData(kspace[129:192, 129:192, :, :, :, :])\nsensitivities = espirit(calibScan, (6, 6), 24, (320, 320))\nsize(sensitivities)\n(320, 320, 1, 8)\n\nA size that is smaller than the encoding size of the data in one of the dimensions is ignored.","title":"Using a Separate Calibration Scan"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Calibration-Data-in-an-Array","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"If the calibration data is not part of an AcquisitionData object, pass it as an array with the dimensions kx, ky and coils, or kx, ky, kz and coils, followed by the size of the maps and the kernel size. The array does not have to be square. The result has the dimensions x, y and coils, or x, y, z and coils, and a last dimension with one entry. For params[:senseMaps], remove this dimension from 3D maps with dropdims(maps, dims = 5), and exchange the last two dimensions of 2D maps:\n\ncalibData = kspace[146:175, 146:175, 1, :, 1, 1]\nmaps = espirit(calibData, (320, 320), (6, 6))\nsensitivities = permutedims(maps, (1, 2, 4, 3))\nsize(calibData), size(maps), size(sensitivities)\n((30, 30, 8), (320, 320, 8, 1), (320, 320, 1, 8))\n\nThe last dimension of maps belongs to the keyword nmaps, which requests several sets of maps. With an AcquisitionData object, nmaps = 2 returns the first set twice (#355), so use an array for this.","title":"Calibration Data in an Array"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Several-Slices,-Contrasts-and-Repetitions","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"For 2D data with several slices, espirit estimates the maps of each slice from the calibration region of that slice. It uses the first contrast and the first repetition, and the keywords echo and rep select another one. echo gives correct maps only if the selected contrast has the same sampling pattern as the first one (#356).","title":"Several Slices, Contrasts and Repetitions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#3D-Data","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"For 3D data, the kernel size has three entries and defaults to (6, 6, 6), and the calibration region is a cube that must fit into the data in all three directions, as in espirit(acqData, (6, 6, 6), 24). The result again has the dimensions x, y, z and coils. With these sizes, the estimation takes much longer than in 2D. A smaller kernel or calibration region reduces the time.","title":"3D Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Differences-between-Calls","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"By default, espirit computes the maps with power iterations, which use a random starting vector, so the maps differ slightly from call to call. With use_poweriterations = false, it computes them with an eigendecomposition, which does not use random numbers but takes longer.","title":"Differences between Calls"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/espirit/#Non-Cartesian-Data","page":"Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT","text":"espirit supports only Cartesian data. Non-Cartesian 2D data can be regridded to a Cartesian grid first with regrid, as in espirit(regrid(acqData, (128, 128))).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Non-Cartesian Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/plans/#configure-plans","page":"Configure a Reconstruction with Plans","text":"reconstruction builds every reconstruction from a reconstruction plan of AbstractImageReconstruction.jl, as described in Reconstruction Parameters. The plan itself shows all parameters of a reconstruction type, and with it, a configured reconstruction can be run on several datasets, and its structure can be saved as a new reconstruction type. This guide shows how. MRIReco re-exports the functions for plans, such as setAll! and build; the documentation of AbstractImageReconstruction.jl explains plans in general.","title":"Configure a Reconstruction with Plans"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/plans/#Configuring-a-Plan","page":"Configure a Reconstruction with Plans","text":"MRIRecoPlan loads the plan of a reconstruction type by its name. The loaded plan has the structure of the reconstruction, but none of its parameters has a value yet:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nplan = MRIRecoPlan(\"standard\")\nRecoPlan{StandardReconstruction}\n└─ parameter::RecoPlan{ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter}\n   ├─ reconSize\n   ├─ scheduler\n   ├─ arrayType\n   └─ parameter::RecoPlan{StandardIterativeParameters}\n      ├─ weightingParams::RecoPlan{DensityWeightingParameters}\n      ├─ encodingParams::RecoPlan{EncodingParameters}\n      │  ├─ method\n      │  ├─ K\n      │  ├─ K_tol\n      │  ├─ correctionMap\n      │  ├─ kernelSize\n      │  ├─ oversamplingFactor\n      │  └─ toeplitz\n      └─ solverParams::RecoPlan{LeastSquaresSolverParameter}\n         ├─ rho\n         ├─ absTol\n         ├─ normalizeReg\n         ├─ relTol\n         ├─ tolInner\n         ├─ solver\n         ├─ regParams::RecoPlan{RegularizationParameters}\n         │  ├─ reg\n         │  └─ sparsityParams::RecoPlan{SimpleSparsityParameters}\n         │     └─ sparseTrafo\n         ├─ iterationsInner\n         ├─ restart\n         ├─ verbose\n         ├─ iterationsCG\n         ├─ iterations\n         └─ vary_rho\n\n\nsetAll! sets a parameter wherever it occurs in the plan, by the name that the plan shows, and only warns about a name that the plan doesn't have; Reconstruction Parameters describes the parameters. A parameter can also be set at its place in the plan:\n\nsetAll!(plan; solver = CGNR, reg = L2Regularization(1e-3))\nplan.parameter.parameter.solverParams.iterations = 10\nplan.parameter.parameter.solverParams\nRecoPlan{LeastSquaresSolverParameter}\n├─ rho\n├─ absTol\n├─ normalizeReg\n├─ relTol\n├─ tolInner\n├─ solver = CGNR\n├─ regParams::RecoPlan{RegularizationParameters}\n│  ├─ reg = L2Regularization{Float64}(0.001)\n│  └─ sparsityParams::RecoPlan{SimpleSparsityParameters}\n│     └─ sparseTrafo\n├─ iterationsInner\n├─ restart\n├─ verbose\n├─ iterationsCG\n├─ iterations = 10\n└─ vary_rho\n\n\nMRIRecoPlan takes values as keyword arguments as well, for example MRIRecoPlan(\"standard\"; solver = CGNR). Parameters without a value take their defaults when the plan is built. A few, such as the coil sensitivities of \"multiCoil\", have no default and have to be set.","title":"Configuring a Plan"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/plans/#Running-the-Reconstruction","page":"Configure a Reconstruction with Plans","text":"build turns the plan into a reconstruction algorithm, and reconstruct applies it to data, here to simulated radial data of a phantom:\n\nN = 128\nphantom = shepp_logan(N)\nsimParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}(:simulation => \"fast\", :trajName => \"Radial\",\n                              :numProfiles => 64, :numSamplingPerProfile => 2N)\nacqData = simulation(phantom, simParams)\n\nsetAll!(plan; reconSize = (N, N))\nalgo = build(plan)\nimage = reconstruct(algo, acqData)\nsize(image)\n(128, 128, 1, 1, 1, 1)\n\nreconstruction with the same keys computes the same image:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}(:reco => \"standard\", :reconSize => (N, N), :solver => CGNR,\n                           :reg => L2Regularization(1e-3), :iterations => 10)\nimage == reconstruction(acqData, params)\ntrue\n\nThe algorithm reconstructs further data with the same settings, for example reconstruct(algo, otherData). reconstruct with the name of a reconstruction type, reconstruct(\"standard\", acqData; kwargs...), loads, configures, builds and runs a plan in one call.","title":"Running the Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/plans/#Replacing-a-Part-of-the-Reconstruction","page":"Configure a Reconstruction with Plans","text":"Some parameters of a plan are nested plans, for example the weighting of the data. Replacing such a plan changes how the reconstruction works. With a plan of UniformWeightingParameters, all samples have the same weight, as with :densityWeighting = false in reconstruction; like that key, it currently works only for data with one coil, see the limits in Reconstruction Parameters. The part is set as a plan, not as UniformWeightingParameters(), so that it stays when the plan is cleared below:\n\nsetAll!(plan; weightingParams = RecoPlan(UniformWeightingParameters))\nuniform = reconstruct(build(plan), acqData)\nparams[:densityWeighting] = false\nuniform == reconstruction(acqData, params)\ntrue","title":"Replacing a Part of the Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/plans/#Saving-a-Plan-as-a-Reconstruction-Type","page":"Configure a Reconstruction with Plans","text":"A plan without parameter values, like the plans in the folder reco_plans of MRIReco.jl, serves as the template of a reconstruction type. clear! removes the values and keeps the structure, that is, the nested plans, and savePlan writes the plan to a TOML file. After addMRIRecoPlanPath with the folder of the file, the new type can be used by the name of the file without .toml:\n\nclear!(plan)\ndir = mktempdir()\nsavePlan(joinpath(dir, \"uniformStandard.toml\"), plan)\naddMRIRecoPlanPath(dir)\nreconstruct(\"uniformStandard\", acqData; reconSize = (N, N), solver = CGNR,\n            reg = L2Regularization(1e-3), iterations = 10) == uniform\ntrue\n\nreconstruction takes the name as :reco as well, and the path of the file works without addMRIRecoPlanPath. Added folders are searched first, the last one added first, and stay added until Julia is restarted, so a plan file there can also replace a built-in type. getMRIRecoPlanList lists the plan files of all folders. A plan with parameter types from another module loads after addMRIRecoPlanModule with that module, which has to export the types. Save plans without values, as above, and pass the values when the plan is used: not all values can be saved and loaded yet, for example the solver, the regularization terms and reconSize (#397), and reconstruction replaces stored values such as :iterations with its defaults.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Saving a Plan as a Reconstruction Type"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/gpu/#gpu-acceleration","page":"GPU Acceleration","text":"An iterative reconstruction spends most of its time in applying the encoding operator and its adjoint, which consist of Fourier transforms and multiplications with coil sensitivities, and in the vector operations of the solver. These operations act on all voxels or samples at once and therefore suit the many cores of a GPU. MRIReco.jl runs reconstructions on GPUs without GPU-specific code in the reconstruction functions. This page explains how, and Run a Reconstruction on the GPU shows how to use it. The approach is presented in more detail in the talk Generic GPU-Acceleration for Medical Image Reconstruction at JuliaCon 2026, whose slides are available as well.","title":"GPU Acceleration"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/gpu/#Generic-Array-Types","page":"GPU Acceleration","text":"Julia's GPU packages, for example CUDA.jl for NVIDIA GPUs and AMDGPU.jl for AMD GPUs, provide array types whose data is stored on the GPU, such as CuArray and ROCArray. Broadcasting, reductions, linear algebra and FFTs run on the GPU when they are called with these arrays. Code that is written with these operations and doesn't assume that its arrays are Arrays therefore runs on the GPU as well.\n\nThe packages that MRIReco.jl builds on are written this way: the solvers and regularization terms of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, the general operators of LinearOperatorCollection.jl and the encoding operators of MRIOperators. None of them depends on a GPU package.","title":"Generic Array Types"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/gpu/#The-State-Follows-the-Data","page":"GPU Acceleration","text":"The reconstruction parameter arrayType decides where a reconstruction runs. MRIReco converts the measured data, the density compensation weights and the coil sensitivities to this array type. From the array type and the precision of the data, it also derives the storage type of the operators, for example a CuArray with element type ComplexF32 for single-precision data. This is why arrayType is given without parameters. The operators allocate their internal arrays with this storage type, so these arrays are on the GPU as well. The solvers create their state, such as the current estimate and the residual, with similar from the measured data, so it is stored where the data is.\n\nThe rest runs on the CPU: reading the data and estimating the coil sensitivities before the reconstruction, and computing the density compensation weights at its start, before MRIReco converts them. At the end, MRIReco copies the image back to the CPU and stores it in an AxisArray, as for a reconstruction on the CPU.","title":"The State Follows the Data"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/gpu/#GPU-Code-in-Package-Extensions","page":"GPU Acceleration","text":"Some operations can't be written efficiently with generic array operations. For these, the packages contain GPU kernels, which are written with KernelAbstractions.jl and are therefore not specific to one GPU vendor. In MRIOperators, these are the multiplication with coil sensitivities in SensitivityOp, the off-resonance correction in FieldmapNFFTOp, the explicit encoding operator ExplicitOp and the circular shutter that is applied for trajectories with circular k-space coverage, such as radial and spiral trajectories. RegularizedLeastSquares.jl and LinearOperatorCollection.jl contain kernels as well, for example for TV regularization. Non-Cartesian Fourier transforms use the GPU implementation of NFFT.jl.\n\nThis code is in package extensions: the kernels in MRIOperatorsGPUArraysExt of MRIOperators, the GPU settings of MRIReco that are described below in MRIRecoGPUArraysExt, and the kernels of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl and LinearOperatorCollection.jl and the GPU implementation of NFFT.jl in their own extensions. These extensions depend on GPUArrays.jl, KernelAbstractions.jl and Adapt.jl, which the GPU packages build on, and Julia loads them as soon as these packages are loaded. Loading CUDA.jl therefore loads the extensions as well, and without a GPU package they are not loaded at all.","title":"GPU Code in Package Extensions"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/gpu/#Parallelism-on-the-CPU-and-the-GPU","page":"GPU Acceleration","text":"The MRIReco.jl paper describes multi-threading on two levels. Within an operator, for example the multi-coil encoding operator, several Fourier transforms are computed in parallel. Above that, the reconstruction types \"multiEcho\", \"multiCoil\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" divide the data into independent problems, for example one per slice and repetition, which can be solved in parallel. On the CPU, MRIReco copies the Fourier operator for each coil, so that the copies can be applied in parallel, and solves the independent problems with the OhMyThreads.jl scheduler DynamicScheduler.\n\nOn the GPU, each operation of a single problem already runs in parallel. MRIReco therefore solves the independent problems one after another, with the SerialScheduler, and doesn't copy the Fourier operator, which saves GPU memory. The parameter scheduler changes the scheduler for the independent problems. With several Julia threads, the shared Fourier operator currently gives wrong results (#317).","title":"Parallelism on the CPU and the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/gpu/#Further-Reading","page":"GPU Acceleration","text":"The GPU guides of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl and LinearOperatorCollection.jl show how the solvers and operators are used on the GPU directly.\nJuliaGPU gives an overview of the GPU packages of Julia.","title":"Further Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/architecture/#Package-Architecture","page":"Package Architecture","text":"MRIReco.jl is a family of packages that are developed together in one repository. This page explains how the functionality is divided between the packages, how they depend on each other and which general-purpose packages they build on. The design of the framework is described in more detail in the MRIReco.jl paper.","title":"Package Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/architecture/#Building-on-the-Julia-Ecosystem","page":"Package Architecture","text":"Julia's package manager installs the dependencies of a package automatically, which makes it cheap to divide functionality between packages. MRIReco.jl uses this to implement only the parts that are specific to MRI and relies on general-purpose packages for the rest. The most important ones are\n\nRegularizedLeastSquares.jl, which provides the iterative solvers, such as CGNR, FISTA and ADMM, and the regularization terms, such as ℓ1, ℓ2 and total variation,\nAbstractImageReconstruction.jl, which provides the interface for reconstruction algorithms, their parameters and the reconstruction plans from which MRIReco.jl builds its reconstructions, see Configure a Reconstruction with Plans,\nLinearOperatorCollection.jl, which provides general linear operators, for example Fourier and wavelet operators,\nNFFT.jl, which computes the non-uniform fast Fourier transform needed for non-Cartesian trajectories, and\nAxisArrays.jl, which stores reconstructed images together with their physical axes.\n\nNone of these packages is specific to MRI. Putting such general functionality into separate packages is one of the design goals of MRIReco.jl, because other projects can then use it as well. RegularizedLeastSquares.jl and LinearOperatorCollection.jl, for example, are also used by MPIReco.jl for magnetic particle imaging, so improvements made for one imaging modality benefit the other.","title":"Building on the Julia Ecosystem"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/architecture/#Packages-in-This-Repository","page":"Package Architecture","text":"The repository contains seven packages, which are registered separately. In the following diagram, each package builds on the packages below it:\n\n(Image: Package architecture: MRIBase at the bottom; MRIOperators, MRIFiles, MRISampling and MRICoilSensitivities on top of it; MRIReco and MRISimulation on top of MRIOperators. MRIReco, MRIOperators and MRIBase are loaded with using MRIReco; the other packages are loaded separately.)\n\nMRIBase is the foundation. It defines the data types that the other packages exchange, such as RawAcquisitionData, AcquisitionData and Trajectory, and does not depend on any other package of the repository. These data types are explained on the Data Model page. All other packages build on MRIBase:\n\nMRIOperators implements the encoding operators, which map an image to k-space data. Besides Fourier operators for Cartesian and non-Cartesian trajectories, it provides operators for coil sensitivities, off-resonance, relaxation and subspace models, which are combined into the encoding operator of an acquisition. It builds on LinearOperatorCollection.jl and NFFT.jl.\nMRIFiles reads and writes ISMRMRD files and reads data from Bruker scanners.\nMRISampling generates undersampling patterns and applies them to acquisition data.\nMRICoilSensitivities estimates coil sensitivities, for example with ESPIRiT, and compresses the data of many coils into fewer virtual coils.\nMRISimulation simulates k-space data of an image, taking into account coil sensitivities, off-resonance and relaxation. It uses the Fourier encoding operators of MRIOperators.\nMRIReco provides the reconstruction function, which combines the encoding operators of MRIOperators with the solvers and regularization terms of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl.","title":"Packages in This Repository"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/architecture/#What-using-MRIReco-Loads","page":"Package Architecture","text":"MRIReco re-exports MRIBase, MRIOperators, RegularizedLeastSquares.jl and AbstractImageReconstruction.jl, and MRIOperators in turn re-exports LinearOperatorCollection.jl. After using MRIReco, the data types, the encoding operators, the general linear operators, the solvers and the functions for reconstruction plans are therefore all available. The other four packages are independent of MRIReco and have to be loaded explicitly, for example\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRICoilSensitivities\n\nThese packages use MRIBase internally but do not re-export it. When one of them is used without MRIReco, MRIBase has to be loaded as well to access its data types:\n\nusing MRIBase, MRIFiles","title":"What using MRIReco Loads"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/architecture/#Using-the-Packages-on-Their-Own","page":"Package Architecture","text":"The division into packages allows using parts of the framework without the reconstruction. Converting raw data between file formats, for example, only needs MRIBase and MRIFiles, and undersampling patterns can be designed with MRISampling. The MRI simulator KomaMRI.jl uses the data types of MRIBase for the raw data it simulates and MRIFiles to store them in ISMRMRD files.","title":"Using the Packages on Their Own"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/architecture/#GPU-Support","page":"Package Architecture","text":"GPU support is implemented in package extensions of MRIOperators and MRIReco, which build on the vendor-independent packages GPUArrays.jl and KernelAbstractions.jl. The extensions are loaded automatically together with a GPU backend such as CUDA.jl or AMDGPU.jl and do not add to the loading time otherwise. GPU Acceleration explains the approach in more detail, and Run a Reconstruction on the GPU shows how to use it.","title":"GPU Support"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/offresonance/#correct-off-resonance","page":"Correct Off-Resonance","text":"During a long readout, as in spiral acquisitions, the signal of a voxel with an off-resonance frequency accumulates a phase, and a reconstruction that does not take this into account blurs or distorts the image. If a field map is known, the iterative reconstructions of MRIReco.jl include it in the encoding operator, as explained in Signal Model and Encoding Operators. This guide shows how to pass the field map, which reconstruction types use it, how to choose the approximation and which sampling times the correction needs.","title":"Correct Off-Resonance"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/offresonance/#Passing-the-Field-Map","page":"Correct Off-Resonance","text":"The reconstruction takes the field map as part of the correction map z = R_2^* + mathrmiDeltaomega, which models the signal of a voxel at the time t as mathrme^-z t. Its imaginary part is the off-resonance frequency in radians per second, so a field map in Hz is multiplied by 2π. Its real part is the relaxation rate R_2^* in 1/s and can be zero. MRIReco.jl does not estimate field maps; they usually come from a separate measurement. The example simulates a single-shot spiral with a readout of 30 ms through the parabolic field map with off-resonance frequencies between -125 Hz and 125 Hz from the simulations of Knopp et al.:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nN = 128\nimage = shepp_logan(N)\nfieldmap = quadraticFieldmap(N, N, 125 * 2pi)\ncorrectionMap = 1im .* fieldmap\n\nsimParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nsimParams[:simulation] = \"fast\"\nsimParams[:trajName] = \"Spiral\"\nsimParams[:numProfiles] = 1\nsimParams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = N * N\nsimParams[:windings] = N ÷ 2\nsimParams[:AQ] = 30e-3\nsimParams[:correctionMap] = correctionMap\nacqData = simulation(image, simParams)\nsize(correctionMap), eltype(correctionMap)\n((128, 128, 1), ComplexF64)\n\nThe correction map is a complex array on the grid of the reconstruction, with the slices of 2D data in its third dimension; for a single slice, a matrix works as well. Its element type has to match the data, so the map of Float32 data is a ComplexF32 array; otherwise, the reconstruction fails with a TypeError. The iterative reconstructions take the map from params[:correctionMap]:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"standard\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (N, N)\nparams[:solver] = CGNR\nparams[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nparams[:iterations] = 10\nuncorrected = reconstruction(acqData, params)\n\nparams[:correctionMap] = correctionMap\ncorrected = reconstruction(acqData, params)\n\nThe figure shows the field map and both reconstructions, with their normalized root mean squared deviation from the phantom, computed with nrmsd:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\ndeviation(x) = round(nrmsd(image, abs.(x[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])), sigdigits = 2)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (940, 320))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Field map in Hz\", aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\nhm = heatmap!(ax, fieldmap[:, :, 1]' ./ 2pi, colormap = :viridis)\nhidedecorations!(ax)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm)\nimages = [uncorrected => \"Without correction\", corrected => \"With correction\"]\nfor (i, (x, title)) in enumerate(images)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i + 2], title = \"$title, NRMSD $(deviation(x))\", aspect = DataAspect(),\n              yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(x[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA map in Hz instead of radians per second corrects hardly anything, and a map with the opposite sign makes the image worse than no correction, which helps to check the convention of a measured map. In two images that MRIReco.jl reconstructs from echoes at the times t_1 and t_2, the phase of a voxel changes by -Deltaomega(t_2 - t_1). MRIFieldmaps.jl estimates field maps from such images in Hz and with the opposite sign, f = -Deltaomega2pi, so the correction map is -2pimathrmi f, or ComplexF32.(-2pi * 1im .* f) for Float32 data.","title":"Passing the Field Map"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/offresonance/#Reconstruction-Types","page":"Correct Off-Resonance","text":"All iterative reconstruction types take the map from params[:correctionMap]: \"standard\", \"multiCoil\", \"multiEcho\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\". For 2D data with several slices, \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" correct every slice with the map of the first slice (#362).\n\nThe direct reconstruction ignores params[:correctionMap] and takes the map from params[:cmap] instead (#296), always with the approximation \"nfft\" described below. It then computes a conjugate phase reconstruction, which in the example is less accurate than the iterative correction, with an NRMSD of 0.044.","title":"Reconstruction Types"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/offresonance/#Choosing-the-Approximation","page":"Correct Off-Resonance","text":"The encoding operator approximates mathrme^-z t by a sum of K terms and computes up to K NFFTs whenever it is applied. The parameter params[:method] chooses how:\n\n\"nfft\", the default, determines K from the ranges of the map and of the readout times and ignores params[:K]. In the example, it uses 14 terms.\n\"leastsquare\" fits K time segments, set by params[:K], to the values of all voxels. It builds a matrix with one entry per voxel and sample, which takes much memory and time for larger images.\n\"hist\" fits the time segments to a histogram of the map and is therefore much faster. It fails for maps with both a relaxation and an off-resonance part (#360).\n\"svd\" derives at most params[:K] functions of time from the off-resonance part, as many as params[:K_tol] requires. It takes them from the first profile and fits them to every tenth sample of it, so its result is wrong without an error if the profiles have different sampling times or if a profile has fewer than about ten samples per function. It also fails for Float32 data, such as data from files (#363).\n\nWith too few terms, the correction can make the image worse than no correction. In the example, K = 2 does so for \"leastsquare\" and \"hist\", and from K = 12 on, all three data-driven methods are about as accurate as \"nfft\". The default \"nfft\", which needs no further parameters, is therefore the method to start with. The keywords alpha and m of FieldmapNFFTOp are not passed on by the reconstruction, and the methods \"leastsquareFS\" and \"histFS\" fail (#345).","title":"Choosing the Approximation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/offresonance/#Sampling-Times","page":"Correct Off-Resonance","text":"The correction uses the sampling times of the trajectory of each contrast, for example readoutTimes(trajectory(acqData, 1)) for the first one, and counts them from its echo time, echoTime(trajectory(acqData, 1)). It therefore reconstructs the magnetization at the echo time. In the example, times that are 10 % too small or too large raise the NRMSD of the corrected image from 0.027 to 0.04.\n\nFor AcquisitionData converted from RawAcquisitionData, as for ISMRMRD and Bruker files, the times of a non-Cartesian trajectory start at zero at the beginning of each readout and follow the sampling interval in the profile headers, and the echo time is zero. For a Cartesian trajectory, the times are placeholders that span 1 ms per profile. AcquisitionData created from a k-space array, as in Create AcquisitionData from Arrays, gives every sample the time zero. If the times don't match the acquisition, set the fields times and TE of the trajectories, one per contrast and in seconds, for example for 2D Cartesian data with one profile per excitation and the sampling interval dt: for tr in acqData.traj; tr.times .= repeat(dt .* (0:numSamplingPerProfile(tr)-1), numProfiles(tr)); end. In a gradient-echo train, such as EPI, the times continue from one profile to the next.","title":"Sampling Times"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/offresonance/#Limits","page":"Correct Off-Resonance","text":"The correction map of 3D data makes the reconstruction fail with a MethodError (#361).\nIf the map of a slice is spatially constant, for example zero while the other slices have a map, \"nfft\" returns NaN for this slice, and the other methods fail, except \"leastsquare\" for a constant other than zero (#360). A map that is zero in every slice is not used at all.\nIf all samples have the same time, as for AcquisitionData created from a k-space array, \"nfft\" returns NaN for the whole image, \"leastsquare\" and \"hist\" fail, and \"svd\" fails in some runs (#360).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation","page":"MRISimulation","text":"MRISimulation simulates MRI data from images, including coil sensitivities, field inhomogeneities and relaxation. MRISimulation depends on MRIBase and MRIOperators. It has to be loaded with using MRISimulation, because MRIReco does not depend on it. The guide Simulate k-Space Data shows how to use these functions.","title":"MRISimulation"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISimulation/#Simulation","page":"MRISimulation","text":"","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISimulation/#Coil-Sensitivities,-Field-Maps-and-Relaxation","page":"MRISimulation","text":"","title":"Coil Sensitivities, Field Maps and Relaxation"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISimulation/#Sequences","page":"MRISimulation","text":"","title":"Sequences"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.simulation","page":"MRISimulation","text":"simulation(image::Array{T,3}, simParams::Dict) where T<:AbstractFloat\n\nSimulate MRI raw data from given image data. All simulation parameters are passed to the function in the form of a dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\nsimulation(image::Array{T,3}, simParams::Dict) where T<:Complex{<:AbstractFloat}\n\nSimulate MRI raw data from given image data. All simulation parameters are passed to the function in the form of a dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\nsimulation(image::Array{T,3}, simParams::Dict, filename::String) where T<:Union{Complex{<:AbstractFloat},AbstractFloat}\n\nPerforms the same simulation as simulation(image, simParams) and saves the result in a file with name filename\n\n\n\n\n\nsimulation(image::Array{T,2}, simParams::Dict) where T<:Union{Complex{<:AbstractFloat},AbstractFloat}\n\nSimulate MRI raw data from given image data. All simulation parameters are passed to the function in the form of a dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\nsimulation(seq::AbstractSequence, tr::Vector{Trajectory}, image::Array{T,3}\n                ; opName=\"fast\", r1map=[], r2map=[], fmap=[], senseMaps=[]\n                , verbose=true, kargs...) where T<:Complex{<:AbstractFloat}\n\nSimulate k-space data for all echoes of a pulse sequence. The echo intensities are simulated using the EPG formalism The Fourier integrals can be evaluated exactly or using NFFT\n\n...\n\nArguments\n\nseq::AbstractSequence      - pulse sequence\ntr::Vector{Trajectory}     - trajectories for all contrasts\nimage::Array{T,3} - image to be transformed\n(r1map=[])                 - R1 map of the object (real)\n(r2map=[])                 - R2 map of the object (real) / (R2* for GRE sequences)\n(fmap=[])                  - fieldmap (real)\n(opName=\"fast\")            - name of operator to use (\"explicit\" or \"fast\")\n(senseMaps=[])             - array of coil sensitivities\n(verbose=true)             - prints the progress if true\nkargs...                   - additional keyword arguments\n\n...\n\n\n\n\n\nsimulation(tr::Trajectory, image::Array{T}, correctionMap = []; opName=\"fast\"\n          , senseMaps=[], verbose=true, kargs...) where T<:Complex{<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTransforms a given image to k-space Domain. Dispatches whether the trajectory is 2d or 3d The Fourier integrals can be evaluated exactly or using NFFT Returns the demodulated signal.\n\n...\n\nArguments\n\ntr::Trajectory             - two- or three-dimensional Trajectory\nimage::Array{T,3} - image to be transformed\n(correctionMap=[])         - sum of the field offresonance (imaginary) map and relaxation map (real)\n(opName=\"fast\")            - name of operator to use (\"explicit\" or \"fast\")\n(senseMaps=[])             - array of coil sensitivities\n(verbose=true)             - prints the progress if true\nkargs...                   - additional keyword arguments\n\n...\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.addNoise","page":"MRISimulation","text":"Adds average white gaussian noise to the signal x\n\nArguments\n\nx::Vector     - signal vector\n'snr::Float64'  - target SNR\n\n\n\n\n\nreturn AcquisitionData with white gaussian noise\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData  - AcquisitionData\n'snr::Float64'              - target SNR\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.addNoise"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.simulation_fast","page":"MRISimulation","text":"Getting (simulated) k-data with the help of NFFTs with lower computational effort\n\n...\n\nArguments\n\ntr::Trajectory           - Two-dimensional Trajectory\nimage::Matrix            - Image to be transformed\ncorrectionMap=[]         - Correctionmap is summed up of the field offresonance and relaxation\nalpha::Float64 = 1.75    - Oversampling factor\nm::Float64=4.0           - Kernel size\nK::Int64=16              - Rank how much coefficients will be used to approx. correctionterm\nmethod=\"nfft\"            - Which Method used to get the approx. coefficients of correctionterm\n(senseMaps=[])           - array of coil sensitivities\nkargs...                 - additional keyword arguments\n\n...\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.simulation_fast"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.simulation_explicit","page":"MRISimulation","text":"Getting (simulated) k-data using an explicit evaluation of the encoding operator\n\n...\n\nArguments\n\ntr::Trajectory           - Two-dimensional Trajectory\nimage::Matrix            - Image to be transformed\ncorrectionMap=[]         - Correctionmap is summed up of the field offresonance and relaxation\nalpha::Float64 = 1.75    - Oversampling factor\nm::Float64=4.0           - Kernel size\nK::Int64=16              - Rank how much coefficients will be used to approx. correctionterm\nmethod=\"nfft\"            - Which Method used to get the approx. coefficients of correctionterm\n(senseMaps=[])           - array of coil sensitivities\nkargs...                 - additional keyword arguments\n\n...\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.simulation_explicit"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.birdcageSensitivity-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Float64}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"birdcageSensitivity(N::Int64, ncoils::Int64, relative_radius::Float64)\n\nComputes the sensitivity maps for each coils that are arranged in a birdcage manner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.birdcageSensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.measured2DSensitivity-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"measured2DSensitivity(N::Int64, ncoils::Int64; T::Type{<:Complex{<:AbstractFloat}} = ComplexF32)\n\nComputes 2D coil sensitivity maps in image space from actually measured coil profiles, as an array of size (N, N, 1, ncoils) with element type T. At most eight coils are available. The code was originally implemented in the Berkeley Advanced Reconstruction Toolbox (BART) by\n\nM. Uecker et al. Software toolbox and programming library for compressed sens-   ing and parallel imaging. In: ISMRM Workshop on Data Sampling and   Image Reconstruction, Sedona, 2013.\n\nCheckout the original file:\n\nhttps://github.com/mrirecon/bart/blob/master/src/simu/sens.c\n\nfor more information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.measured2DSensitivity"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.polarFieldmap","page":"MRISimulation","text":"Computes a polaroid fieldmap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.polarFieldmap"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.quadraticFieldmap","page":"MRISimulation","text":"quadraticFieldmap(Nx::Int64, Ny::Int64, maxOffresonance::Float64=125.0)\n\nComputes a parabolic fieldmap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.quadraticFieldmap"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.stairsFieldmap-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"Computes a fieldmap consisting of stairs, which has discrete linear ascending/descending values\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.stairsFieldmap"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.relaxationMap","page":"MRISimulation","text":"Calculates the relaxationMap based on the pixel values of the image\n\n...\n\nArguments\n\nimage::Matrix : Image whose values determine the relaxationrate at each pixel.\nminRelaxation::Float64=5.0 : minimum relaxationrate measured in [Hz]\nmaxRelaxation::Float64=125.0 : maximum relaxationrate measured in [Hz]\n\n...\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.relaxationMap"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.MESequence","page":"MRISimulation","text":"General Multi-Echo sequence with variable flip angles and TR.\n\nThe phase of the excitation pulse has a phase of -90° in order to fulfill CPMG conditions.\n\nFor simplicity instantaneous pulses are assumed.\n\nThe echoes appear at the times T_echo after the excitation pulse.\n\nFields\n\nexcitationAngle::Float64            - flip angle of the excitation pulse\nrefocusingAngles :: Vector{Float64} - flip angles of the refocusing pulses\nT_rf::Vector{Float64}               - times of the refocusing pulses relative to the excitation pulse\nT_echo:: Vector{Float64}            - echo times relative to the excitation pulse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.MESequence"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.echoAmplitudes","page":"MRISimulation","text":"echoAmplitudes(seq::MESequence, R1::AbstractFloat, R2::AbstractFloat, numStates=nothing)\n\ncalculates echo amplitudes for a given MESequence and given relaxation Rates R1, R2. Calculations are performed using the extended phase graph method. For simplicity instantaneous pulses are assumed. If numStates=nothing all dephasing states will be taken into account\n\nArguments\n\nseq::MESequence - pulse sequence\nR1::AbstractFloat - R1 value to use (1/T1)\nR2::AbstractFloat - R2 value to use (1/T2)\nnumStates=nothing - number of dephasing states to consider\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.echoAmplitudes"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.echoTimes-Tuple{MESequence}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"echoTimes(seq::MESequence)\n\nreturns the echo times of an ME Sequence\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.echoTimes"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.flipAngles-Tuple{MESequence}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"flipAngles(seq::MESequence)\n\nreturns the refocusing flip angles of an ME Sequence\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.flipAngles"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.numContrasts-Tuple{MESequence}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"numContrasts(seq::MESequence)\n\nreturns the number of echoes of an ME Sequence\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.numContrasts"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.numEchoes-Tuple{MESequence}","page":"MRISimulation","text":"numEchoes(seq::MESequence)\n\nreturns the number of echos of a ME Sequence\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.numEchoes"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.epgDephasing-Union{Tuple{Vector{T}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any}} where T","page":"MRISimulation","text":"epgDephasing(F::Vector{T}, n=1) where T = circshift(F[:],n)\n\nshifts the transverse dephasing states F corresponding to n dephasing-cycles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.epgDephasing"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.epgRelaxation-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Float64, AbstractFloat, AbstractFloat, Vector{T}, Vector{T}}} where T","page":"MRISimulation","text":"epgRelaxation( R1::AbstractFloat, R2::AbstractFloat, t::AbstractFloat, F::Vector{T}, Z::Vector{T}) where T\n\napplies relaxation matrices to a set of EPG states.\n\nArguments\n\nR1::AbstractFloat   - R1\nR2::AbstractFloat   - R2\nt::AbstractFloat    - length of time interval in s\nF::Vector{T}  - transverse dephasing stats\nZ::Vector{T}  - longitudinal dephasing stats\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.epgRelaxation"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.epgRotation-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Float64, Vector{T}, Vector{T}}} where T","page":"MRISimulation","text":"epgRotation(alpha::Float64, F::Vector{T}, Z::Vector{T}; statesConsidered=nothing, phi::Float64=0.0)\n\napplies Bloch-rotation (<=> RF pulse) to a set of EPG states.\n\nArguments\n\nalpha::Float64           - flip angle of the RF pulse\nF::Vector{T}             - transverse dephasing stats\nZ::Vector{T}             - longitudinal dephasing stats\nstatesConsidered=nothing - number of dephasing states to consider (nothing means all states are taken into account)\nphi::Float64=0.0         - phase of the RF pulse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.epgRotation"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISimulation/#MRISimulation.rfRotation","page":"MRISimulation","text":"rfRotation(alpha, phi=0.)\n\nreturns the rotation matrix for a pulse with flip angle alpha and phase phi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISimulation.rfRotation"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"MRICoilSensitivities estimates coil sensitivity maps, for example with ESPIRiT, and compresses multi-channel data to fewer virtual coils. MRICoilSensitivities depends on MRIBase. It has to be loaded with using MRICoilSensitivities, because MRIReco does not depend on it. The guide Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT shows how to use espirit.","title":"MRICoilSensitivities"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#Coil-Sensitivities","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"","title":"Coil Sensitivities"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#Coil-Compression","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"","title":"Coil Compression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.espirit-Union{Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}, Int64}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}, Int64, NTuple{D, Int64}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"espirit(acqData::AcquisitionData, ksize::NTuple{D,Int} = (6,6[,6]), ncalib::Int = 24,\n           imsize::NTuple{D,Int}=Tuple(acqData.encodingSize[1:D])\n           ; eigThresh_1::Number=0.02, eigThresh_2::Number=0.95, nmaps = 1) where D\n\nespirit(calibData::Array{T}, imsize::NTuple{D,Int}, ksize::NTuple{S,Int} = (6,6[,6])\n           ; eigThresh_1::Number = 0.02, eigThresh_2::Number = 0.95, nmaps = 1) where D\n\nObtains coil sensitivities from a calibration area using ESPIRiT. Works with 2D and 3D data.\n\nThe code is adapted from the MATLAB code by Uecker et al. (cf. Uecker et al. \"ESPIRiT—an eigenvalue approach to autocalibrating parallel MRI: Where SENSE meets GRAPPA\"). The matlab code can be found at: [http://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~mlustig/Software.html]\n\nRequired Arguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData  - AcquisitionData\n\nor\n\ncalibData::Array{T}       - Center of k-space in the format kx × ky [× kz] × coils, where the kz dimension is optional. The type T of the input data determines the type of the calculated maps. Reasonable choices are in the range of Nx = Ny [= Nz] = 24.\nimsize::NTuple{D,Int}     - matrix size of the final maps (optional when passing acqData)\n\nOptional Arguments\n\nksize::NTuple{D,Int}      - size of the kernel in each dimension; the default is (6,6) for 2D and (6,6,6) for 3D.\nncalib::Int      - size of the central part corresponding to the calibration data.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\neigThresh_1::Number=0.02  - threshold for the singular values of the calibration matrix (relative to the largest value); reduce for more accuracy, increase for saving memory and computation time.\neigThresh_2::Number=0.95  - threshold to mask the final maps: for each voxel, the map will be set to 0, if, for this voxel, no singular value > eigThresh_2 exists.\nnmaps = 1                 - Number of maps that are calculated. Set to 1 for regular SENSE; set to 2 for soft-SENSE (cf. Uecker et al. \"ESPIRiT—an eigenvalue approach to autocalibrating parallel MRI: Where SENSE meets GRAPPA\").\nuse_poweriterations = true - flag to determine if power iterations are used; power iterations are only used if nmaps == 1. They provide speed benefits over the full eigen decomposition, but are an approximation.\necho::Int = 1 - select the k-space corresponding to the echo\nrep::Int = 1 - select the k-space corresponding to the rep\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.espirit"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.estimateCoilSensitivities-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 6}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 6}, Any}} where T","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"s = estimateCoilSensitivities(I::AbstractArray{T,6}, thresh = 1.e-2)\n\nEstimates the coil sensitivity based on a reconstruction where the data from each coil has been reconstructed individually. Returns a 6D array of the size of I, which is zero where the root sum of squares over the coils is not above thresh times its maximum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.estimateCoilSensitivities"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.mergeChannels-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 6}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"I4 = mergeChannels(I::AbstractArray{T,6})\n\nMerge the channels of a multi-coil reconstruction. Returns a 6D array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.mergeChannels"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.applyCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"applyCoilCompression(acqData::AcquisitionData, ccMat; dim = 1)\n\nApply the compression matrix ccMat to all contrasts, slices and repetitions of acqData.\n\nccMat can be\n\na matrix (channels × virtual coils), used for every slice,\na vector with one matrix per slice, as returned by softwareCoilCompression,\na 3D array (positions × channels × virtual coils), as returned by geometricCoilCompression. This requires Cartesian data, with dim fully sampled.\n\nReturn a new AcquisitionData. The sampling pattern, the trajectories and the other fields are copied from acqData.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.applyCoilCompression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.applyCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Array{T, 6}, Union{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}}} where T<:Complex","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"applyCoilCompression(kspace::Array{T,6}, ccMat; dim = 1) where T <: Complex\n\nApply the compression matrix ccMat to the Cartesian kspace (x, y, z, channels, contrasts, repetitions), as returned by kDataCart, for all contrasts and repetitions.\n\nccMat can be\n\na matrix (channels × virtual coils), as returned by softwareCoilCompression for an array,\na vector with one matrix per slice (dimension 3), as returned by softwareCoilCompression for an AcquisitionData,\na 3D array (positions × channels × virtual coils), as returned by geometricCoilCompression. The k-space is then inverse Fourier transformed along dim, compressed position by position and transformed back.\n\nReturn the compressed k-space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.applyCoilCompression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 4}, Union{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{<:AbstractMatrix}}}} where T<:Complex","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps(smaps::AbstractArray{T,4}, ccMat; dim = 1) where T <: Complex\n\nCompress the sensitivity maps smaps (x, y, z, channels) with the compression matrix ccMat used for the data, so that they are the sensitivities of the virtual coils: S_v(mathbfr) = sum_c S_c(mathbfr)A_cv.\n\nccMat can be a matrix, a vector with one matrix per slice (dimension 3 of smaps) or a 3D array from geometricCoilCompression, see applyCoilCompression. For geometric coil compression, the matrices are applied position by position along dim; no Fourier transform is needed because the maps are already in image space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.geometricCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, Int64}} where {T, D}","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"geometricCoilCompression(acqData::AcquisitionData, numVC::Int = numChannels(acqData); dim = 1, sContr = 1, sRep = 1)\n\nCompress the Cartesian acqData to numVC virtual coils with geometric coil compression. See the method for arrays for the meaning of dim, sContr and sRep.\n\nThe compression matrices are computed from the k-space returned by kDataCart and shared by all slices. For 2D data, dim must be 1 or 2. The result keeps the sampling pattern, the trajectories and the other fields of acqData, so undersampled data stay undersampled. Return (acqDataCC, ccMat), where ccMat has size positions × channels × numVC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.geometricCoilCompression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.geometricCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{Array{T, 6}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Array{T, 6}, Int64}} where T<:Complex","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"geometricCoilCompression(kspace::Array{T,6}, numVC::Int = size(kspace, 4); dim = 1, sContr = 1, sRep = 1) where T <: Complex\n\nCompress the Cartesian kspace (x, y, z, channels, contrasts, repetitions), as returned by kDataCart, to numVC virtual coils with geometric coil compression.\n\nDimension dim (1, 2 or 3) must be fully sampled, typically the readout direction. The k-space is inverse Fourier transformed along dim, and one compression matrix is computed for each position along dim from contrast sContr and repetition sRep. The matrices are aligned so that the virtual coils vary smoothly between positions, and applied to all contrasts and repetitions with applyCoilCompression.\n\nReturn (kspaceCC, ccMat), where ccMat has size positions × channels × numVC.\n\nReference\n\nZhang T, Pauly JM, Vasanawala SS, Lustig M. Coil compression for accelerated imaging with Cartesian sampling. Magnetic Resonance in Medicine 2013;69:571–582. doi: 10.1002/mrm.24267\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.geometricCoilCompression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.softwareCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, Int64}} where {T, D}","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"softwareCoilCompression(acqData::AcquisitionData, numVC::Int = numChannels(acqData); sContr = 1, sRep = 1)\n\nCompress acqData to numVC virtual coils. Works for any trajectory and for undersampled data.\n\nOne compression matrix is computed per slice, from contrast sContr and repetition sRep, and applied to all contrasts and repetitions of that slice with applyCoilCompression. Return (acqDataCC, ccMat), where ccMat is a vector with one matrix (channels × numVC) per slice. It can be passed to applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps to compress sensitivity maps.\n\nReference\n\nHuang F, Vijayakumar S, Li Y, Hertel S, Duensing GR. A software channel compression technique for faster reconstruction with many channels. Magnetic Resonance Imaging 2008;26:133–141. doi: 10.1016/j.mri.2007.04.010\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.softwareCoilCompression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.softwareCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{Array{T, 6}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Array{T, 6}, Int64}} where T<:Complex","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"softwareCoilCompression(kspace::Array{T,6}, numVC::Int = size(kspace, 4); sContr = 1, sRep = 1) where T <: Complex\n\nCompress the Cartesian kspace (x, y, z, channels, contrasts, repetitions), as returned by kDataCart, to numVC virtual coils.\n\nA single compression matrix is computed from contrast sContr and repetition sRep and applied to all contrasts and repetitions with applyCoilCompression. Return (kspaceCC, ccMat), with ccMat of size channels × numVC.\n\nReference\n\nHuang F, Vijayakumar S, Li Y, Hertel S, Duensing GR. A software channel compression technique for faster reconstruction with many channels. Magnetic Resonance Imaging 2008;26:133–141. doi: 10.1016/j.mri.2007.04.010\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.softwareCoilCompression"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRICoilSensitivities/#MRICoilSensitivities.softwareCoilCompression-Union{Tuple{Matrix{T}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Matrix{T}, Int64}} where T<:Complex","page":"MRICoilSensitivities","text":"softwareCoilCompression(kdata::Matrix{T}, numVC::Int = size(kdata, 2)) where T <: Complex\n\nCompress the k-space matrix kdata (samples × channels) to numVC virtual coils.\n\nThe compression matrix ccMat (channels × numVC) consists of the right singular vectors of kdata that belong to the numVC largest singular values. Return (kdataCC, ccMat) with kdataCC = kdata * ccMat (samples × numVC).\n\nReference\n\nHuang F, Vijayakumar S, Li Y, Hertel S, Duensing GR. A software channel compression technique for faster reconstruction with many channels. Magnetic Resonance Imaging 2008;26:133–141. doi: 10.1016/j.mri.2007.04.010\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRICoilSensitivities.softwareCoilCompression"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#subspace-reconstruction","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"With several coils, the reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" reconstructs one image per echo from the data of all coils. A subspace reconstruction goes one step further. A few basis curves describe the decay curves of all tissues, so the reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" does not estimate an image for every echo, but one coefficient map for every basis curve. This tutorial reconstructs measured, highly undersampled data of a mouse brain with both types and computes T_2 maps from the results.\n\nBesides MRIReco, the tutorial needs HDF5 to read the data, MRICoilSensitivities for the coil sensitivities, LinearAlgebra for the basis, Random for reproducible results and CairoMakie for the plots. The plotting function is the one from Multi-Echo Reconstruction.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRICoilSensitivities, HDF5, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1234)\n\nfunction showimages(images, titles; columns = length(images), kwargs...)\n    fig = Figure(size = (300 * columns, 310 * cld(length(images), columns)))\n    for (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip(images, titles))\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(i, columns)...], title = title,\n                  aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n        heatmap!(ax, image'; colormap = :grays, kwargs...)\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n    end\n    return fig\nend","title":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#The-Data","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"Trotier et al. scanned a mouse with brain metastases at 4.7 T with a 3D multi-echo spin-echo sequence: 50 echoes, 7 ms apart, four coils and 128 × 128 × 96 voxels. To shorten the scan to 25 minutes, they measured an eighth of the samples in the two phase encoding directions, with a different Poisson-disk mask for each echo and a fully sampled center. Their dataset is licensed under CC BY 4.0.\n\nTo keep this tutorial fast, we use one plane of it. After a Fourier transform along the readout, plane 65 of 128 can be reconstructed on its own, with 96 × 128 voxels. We shifted the plane so that the head does not wrap around the image border, and scaled it. The file contains the samples with the dimensions samples, coils and echoes, their indices in the plane and the echo times in ms:\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfile = joinpath(datadir, \"mouse_brain_MESE_plane65.h5\")\nsamples = h5read(file, \"samples\")\nindices = Int.(h5read(file, \"indices\"))\nechoTimes = h5read(file, \"echoTimes\")\nsize(samples), size(indices)\n((1534, 4, 50), (1534, 50))\n\nAcquisitionData takes one trajectory per contrast and an array of matrices, one matrix with the dimensions samples and coils for each contrast, slice and repetition. With the keyword idx, it also takes the indices of the measured samples for each echo. As we will need different echoes below, we write this as a function:\n\nfunction measuredData(echoes)\n    tr = trajectory(Float32, \"Cartesian\", 128, 96)\n    kdata = [samples[:, :, e] for e in echoes, slice in 1:1, repetition in 1:1]\n    return AcquisitionData(fill(tr, length(echoes)), kdata;\n                           idx = [indices[:, e] for e in echoes], encodingSize = (96, 128))\nend\nacqData = measuredData(1:50)\n\nEach echo contains 12.5 % of the samples of the plane, and together they cover 76 %:\n\nfunction samplingMask(echoes)\n    mask = zeros(96, 128)\n    for e in echoes\n        mask[indices[:, e]] .= 1\n    end\n    return mask\nend\nshowimages(samplingMask.([1, 2, 1:50]),\n           [\"Mask of echo 1\", \"Mask of echo 2\", \"Measured in some echo\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe direct reconstruction of the undersampled data is blurred. With increasing echo time, the signal decays, and the metastases with their long T_2 stand out. Each image has its own gray scale:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (96, 128)\nrootSumOfSquares(images) = sqrt.(sum(abs2, images, dims = 5))[:, :, 1, :, 1, 1]\ndirectImages = rootSumOfSquares(reconstruction(acqData, params))\nshownEchoes = [2, 10, 30]\nshowimages([directImages[:, :, e] for e in shownEchoes],\n           [\"Echo $e, $(echoTimes[e]) ms\" for e in shownEchoes])\nFigure()","title":"The Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#Coil-Sensitivities-and-the-First-Echo","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"As in Multi-Coil Reconstruction, we estimate the coil sensitivities with espirit from the calibration region, here the fully sampled center of the plane with 14 × 14 samples. It uses the first echo, as Trotier et al. do:\n\nsensitivities = espirit(acqData, (4, 4), 14, eigThresh_2 = 0)\n\nReal echo trains also contain stimulated echoes, so that the signal does not follow an exponential decay exactly. With the exponential dictionary below, Trotier et al. therefore leave out the first echo, and we do the same from now on:\n\nacqData = measuredData(2:50)\nechoTimes = echoTimes[2:50]\ndirectImages = directImages[:, :, 2:50]","title":"Coil Sensitivities and the First Echo"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#Multi-Coil-Multi-Echo-Reconstruction","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" combines the SENSE model of Multi-Coil Reconstruction with one image mathbfx_e per echo e:\n\nundersetmathbfx_1 ldots mathbfx_N_eoperatornameargmin \nfrac12sum_esum_c lVert mathbfF_emathbfS_cmathbfx_e - mathbfb_ec rVert_2^2\n+ mathrmR(mathbfx_1 ldots mathbfx_N_e)\n\nwhere mathbfS_c multiplies with the sensitivity of coil c, and mathbfF_e and mathbfb_ec are the Fourier transform and the measured samples, scaled as in Multi-Echo Reconstruction. We pass the sensitivities as params[:senseMaps] and start with CGNR and a small ell_2 regularization. As in Iterative Reconstruction, MeasurementBasedNormalization scales the weight λ of the regularization by the mean magnitude of the measured data. The coil dimension of the result has size 1:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"multiCoilMultiEcho\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (96, 128)\nparams[:senseMaps] = sensitivities\nparams[:solver] = CGNR\nparams[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nparams[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparams[:iterations] = 10\nresult = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nsize(result)\n(96, 128, 1, 49, 1, 1)\n\nFrom now on, we look at the echo time of 70 ms, where the metastases stand out, in a common gray scale. The result is sharper than the direct reconstruction, but with an eighth of the samples, ten iterations already amplify the noise strongly:\n\nechoStack(images) = abs.(images[:, :, 1, :, 1, 1])\nwithoutBasis = echoStack(result)\nshownEcho = findfirst(==(70), echoTimes)\nshowecho(images, labels) = showimages([image[:, :, shownEcho] for image in images], labels,\n                                      colorrange = (0, 0.5))\nshowecho([directImages, withoutBasis], [\"Direct\", \"Without basis\"])\nFigure()","title":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#The-Temporal-Basis","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"We build the basis as Huang et al. do. A dictionary contains the signal e^-T_E  T_2 at the echo times for a range of T_2, as in Trotier et al. from 1 ms to 2000 ms. The first right singular vectors of this dictionary are the orthonormal basis curves:\n\ndictionary = [exp(-TE / T2) for T2 in 1.0:2000.0, TE in echoTimes]\ndictionarySVD = svd(dictionary)\nround.(dictionarySVD.S[1:8] ./ dictionarySVD.S[1], sigdigits = 2)\n8-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 0.096\n 0.019\n 0.0046\n 0.0013\n 0.00035\n 0.0001\n 2.9e-5\n\nThe singular values fall quickly, so that few curves suffice. Huang et al. use two to four curves, Tamir et al. four and Trotier et al. six. Fewer curves describe the signal less exactly, but leave less noise. We use six. Most of them do not look like decays themselves, but their weighted sums do. The basis must be a complex matrix with the element type of the data, although its values are real:\n\nbasis = ComplexF32.(dictionarySVD.V[:, 1:6])\nfig = Figure(size = (560, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"Echo time in ms\")\nfor k in 1:6\n    lines!(ax, echoTimes, real.(basis[:, k]), label = \"Curve $k\")\nend\nLegend(fig[1, 2], ax)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"The Temporal Basis"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#Subspace-Reconstruction","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" writes each echo image as a sum over the basis curves,\n\nmathbfx_e = sum_k phi_ek  boldsymbolalpha_k\n\nwhere phi_ek is the value of curve k at echo e and boldsymbolalpha_k is its coefficient map. It solves the problem above for the coefficient maps, with the regularization mathrmR applied to these maps instead of the echo images. It takes the basis as params[:basis] and returns the coefficient maps in the dimension of the echoes:\n\nparams[:reco] = \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\"\nparams[:basis] = basis\ncoefficients = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nsize(coefficients)\n(96, 128, 1, 6, 1, 1)\n\nEach map has its own gray scale. The first maps show different mixes of the tissues, and the first two hold most of the signal. The last ones hold mostly noise:\n\nshowimages([abs.(coefficients[:, :, 1, k, 1, 1]) for k in 1:6], [\"Map $k\" for k in 1:6],\n           columns = 3)\nFigure()\n\nThe function applySubspace computes the echo images from the coefficient maps. In the echo image at 70 ms, the basis reduces the noise, because the part of the noise outside of the subspace does not enter the result. It also reduces the undersampling artifacts: each coefficient map takes samples from all echoes, and since the masks differ, these cover 76 % of the plane:\n\nwithBasis = echoStack(applySubspace(coefficients, basis))\nshowecho([withoutBasis, withBasis], [\"Without basis\", \"With basis\"])\nFigure()","title":"Subspace Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#Adding-a-Locally-Low-Rank-Regularization","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"The coefficient maps are spatially correlated. Tamir et al. exploit this with a locally low-rank (LLR) regularization. As in Multi-Echo Reconstruction, we use it with POGM and small steps. With POGM, MeasurementBasedNormalization uses the back-projected data. We apply the regularization to the coefficient maps and, for comparison, to the echo images of \"multiCoilMultiEcho\". We chose λ empirically for each of them:\n\nparams[:solver] = POGM\nparams[:rho] = 0.05\nparams[:reg] = LLRRegularization(1.0, shape = (96, 128), blockSize = (8, 8))\nparams[:iterations] = 100\nregularizedWithBasis = echoStack(applySubspace(reconstruction(acqData, params), basis))\n\nparams[:reco] = \"multiCoilMultiEcho\"\ndelete!(params, :basis)\nparams[:reg] = LLRRegularization(2.0, shape = (96, 128), blockSize = (8, 8))\nregularizedWithoutBasis = echoStack(reconstruction(acqData, params))\nshowecho([regularizedWithoutBasis, regularizedWithBasis],\n         [\"LLR, without basis\", \"LLR, with basis\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe regularization removes most of the noise in both cases. With the basis, it works on six maps instead of 49 images, so that, once the code is compiled, the reconstruction is clearly faster.","title":"Adding a Locally Low-Rank Regularization"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#Computing-T-Maps","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"We fit the T_2 maps as in Multi-Echo Reconstruction. At later echoes, the signal of the brain approaches the noise level, which biases this simple fit towards longer T_2. Trotier et al. therefore fit a model with a noise term. We only use the echoes up to 140 ms, which reduces the bias. We show the maps where the first remaining echo of the LLR result with basis exceeds 15 % of its maximum:\n\nfitEchoes = findall(<=(140), echoTimes)\nfunction fitT2(images)\n    A = [ones(length(fitEchoes)) -echoTimes[fitEchoes]]\n    fit = A \\ reshape(log.(max.(images[:, :, fitEchoes], eps(Float32))), 96 * 128, :)'\n    return reshape(1 ./ fit[2, :], 96, 128)\nend\n\nfirstImage = regularizedWithBasis[:, :, 1]\ninside = firstImage .> 0.15 * maximum(firstImage)\nresults = [directImages, withoutBasis, withBasis,\n           regularizedWithoutBasis, regularizedWithBasis]\nlabels = [\"Direct\", \"Without basis\", \"With basis\", \"LLR, without basis\", \"LLR, with basis\"]\nfig = showimages([fitT2(images) .* inside for images in results], labels, columns = 3,\n                 colormap = :viridis, colorrange = (0, 150))\nColorbar(fig[:, 4], colormap = :viridis, limits = (0, 150), label = \"T₂ in ms\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe direct map has little noise, but is blurred. Without the LLR term, the maps are noisy. With it, the noise is low and the metastases with their long T_2 have clear borders, while the rest of the brain reads mostly between 50 and 60 ms. On fully sampled scans, Trotier et al. measure 54 ms in the brain. This dataset has no fully sampled reference, so we compare the images by eye here.","title":"Computing T₂ Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/subspace/#Next-Steps","page":"Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction","text":"Subspace_MESE.jl of Trotier et al. reconstructs the whole dataset, for example with a dictionary simulated with extended phase graphs, which keeps the first echo, and a wavelet regularization.\nMulti-Echo Reconstruction explains the locally low-rank regularization and the role of the sampling masks.\nThe RegularizedLeastSquares.jl documentation describes the available solvers and regularization terms.\nThe API reference describes reconstruction and applySubspace.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling","page":"MRISampling","text":"MRISampling creates undersampling patterns and applies them to k-space data. MRISampling depends on MRIBase. It has to be loaded with using MRISampling, because MRIReco does not depend on it. The guide Undersample k-Space shows how to use it.","title":"MRISampling"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISampling/#Undersampling","page":"MRISampling","text":"","title":"Undersampling"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISampling/#Sampling-Patterns","page":"MRISampling","text":"","title":"Sampling Patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRISampling/#Incoherence","page":"MRISampling","text":"","title":"Incoherence"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{NTuple{N, Int64}, Any, String}} where N","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample(shape::NTuple{N,Int64}, redFac, patFunc::String; kargs...)\n\ngenerates the indices of the samples of an Array of size shape for a reduction factor redFac. An unknown patFunc logs an error and returns nothing.\n\nArguments\n\nshape::NTuple{N,Int64} - size of the Array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\npatFunc::String        - name of the sampling function                           (\"random\", \"regular\", \"lines\", \"poisson\" or \"vdPoisson\"; in one dimension all but                           \"lines\", in three dimensions only \"regular\" and \"lines\")\nkargs...               - additional keyword arguments of the sampling function,                           for example rng and seed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_kspace-Tuple{AbstractArray, Any, AbstractString}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_kspace(data::AbstractArray, redFac::Float64, patFunc::String; kargs...)\n\nsubsamples the Array data with a reduction factor redFac and returns both the subsampled Array (as a vector) and the sampled indices (as a vector)\n\nArguments\n\ndata::AbstractArray    - array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\npatFunc::String        - name of the sampling function                           (\"random\", \"regular\", \"lines\", \"poisson\" or \"vdPoisson\"; for a vector all but \"lines\",                           for a 3D array only \"regular\" and \"lines\")\nkargs...               - additional keyword arguments\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_kspace"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_kspace-Tuple{AcquisitionData, Any, AbstractString}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_kspace(acqData::AcquisitionData,redFac::Float64,\n            patFunc::AbstractString; rand=true, profiles=true,\n            rng = Random.default_rng(), seed = nothing, kargs...)\n\nsubsamples the data in acqData with reduction factor redFac and returns a new AcquisitionData object.\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData  - AcquisitionData to be sampled\nredFac::Float64           - subsampling factor\npatFunc::String           - name of the sampling function                               (\"random\", \"regular\", \"lines\", \"poisson\" or \"vdPoisson\";                               with 2D data and profiles=true all but \"lines\", with 3D data and                               profiles=false only \"regular\" and \"lines\")\n(rand=true)               - use different patterns for the different contrasts\n(profiles=true)           - sample complete profiles\n(rng=Random.default_rng()) - random number generator\n(seed=nothing)            - seed for rng; with nothing, rng is used in its current state\nkargs...                  - additional keyword arguments\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_kspace"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_regular-Tuple{Tuple, Any}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_regular(shape::Tuple{Int64,Int64}, redFac;kargs...)\n\ngenerates a regular sampling pattern for an Array of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac.\n\nArguments\n\nshape::NTuple{N,Int64} - size of the Array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_regular"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_random-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64}, Float64}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_random(shape::Tuple{Int64,Int64},redFac::Float64;calsize::Int64=0,rng=Random.default_rng(),seed=nothing,kargs...)\n\ngenerates a random sampling pattern for an Array of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac.\n\nArguments\n\nshape::NTuple{N,Int64} - size of the Array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\n(calsize::Int64=0)     - size of the fully sampled calibration area\n(rng=Random.default_rng()) - random number generator\n(seed=nothing)         - seed for rng; with nothing, rng is used in its current state\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_random"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_lines-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}, Any}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_lines(shape::Tuple{Int64,Int64,Int64}, redFac; sampleFunc=\"random\",kargs...)\n\ngenerates a pattern to sample complete lines of an Array of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac. The arguments are the same as in the 2d case with the exception that shape is of type NTuple{3,Int64}, and sampleFunc can also be \"lines\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_lines-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64}, Any}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_lines(shape::Tuple{Int64,Int64}, redFac; sampleFunc=\"random\",kargs...)\n\ngenerates a pattern to sample complete lines of an Array of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac.\n\nArguments\n\nshape::Tuple{Int64,Int64} - size of the Array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\nsampleFunc=\"random\"        - name of the sampling function                           (\"random\", \"regular\", \"poisson\" or \"vdPoisson\")\nkargs...               - additional keyword arguments\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_poissondisk-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64}, Float64}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_poissondisk(shape::Tuple{Int64,Int64},redFac::Float64;calsize::Int64=0, rng=Random.default_rng(), seed=nothing,kargs...)\n\ngenerates a Poisson disk sampling pattern for an Array of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac.\n\nArguments\n\nshape::NTuple{2,Int64} - size of the Array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\n(calsize::Int64=0)     - size of the fully sampled calibration area\n(rng=Random.default_rng()) - random number generator\n(seed=nothing)         - seed for rng; with nothing, rng is used in its current state\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_poissondisk"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_poissondisk-Tuple{Tuple{Int64}, Any}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_poissondisk(shape::Tuple{Int64},redFac;calsize::Int64=0,kargs...)\n\ngenerates a Poisson disk sampling pattern for a Vector of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac. The arguments are the same as in the 2d case with the exception that shape is of type Tuple{Int64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_poissondisk"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_vdpoisson-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64}, Float64}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_vdpoisson(shape::Tuple{Int64,Int64},redFac::Float64; rng=Random.default_rng(), seed=nothing,kargs...)\n\ngenerates a variable density Poisson disk sampling pattern for an Array of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac.\n\nArguments\n\nshape::NTuple{2,Int64} - size of the Array to be sampled\nredFac::Float64        - subsampling factor\n(rng=Random.default_rng()) - random number generator\n(seed=nothing)         - seed for rng; with nothing, rng is used in its current state\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_vdpoisson"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.sample_vdpoisson-Tuple{Tuple{Int64}, Any}","page":"MRISampling","text":"sample_vdpoisson(shape::Tuple{Int64},redFac;kargs...)\n\ngenerates a variable density Poisson disk sampling pattern for a Vector of size shape with a subsampling factor redFac. The arguments are the same as in the 2d case with the exception that shape is of type Tuple{Int64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.sample_vdpoisson"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRISampling/#MRISampling.calculateIncoherence","page":"MRISampling","text":"calculateIncoherence(acqData::AcquisitionData, recoParams::Dict, slice=1)\n\ncalculates the incoherence of the sampling pattern contained in acqData\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData  - AcquisitionData containing the sampling pattern\nrecoParams::Dict          - Dict containing reconstruction parameters\n(slice=1)                 - slice for which to calculate the incoherence\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRISampling.calculateIncoherence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#reconstruction-parameters","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":"reconstruction takes the parameters of a reconstruction as a dictionary with Symbol keys, for example params[:iterations] = 20 for params = Dict{Symbol, Any}(). This page lists the keys, their default values and the reconstruction types that use them. Keys that the chosen reconstruction type does not use are ignored, and so are misspelled keys and names that older versions used, such as :λ, :ρ, :regularization, :regTrafo, which :sparseTrafo replaces, :sparseTrafoName and :kwargWarning. This happens without a warning, except for keys whose value is a parameter object, an algorithm or a plan, and for the four keys of the next paragraph with \"direct\", which warns that it cannot set the parameter they select; with ENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"MRIReco\", reconstruction lists the other keys that the plan does not have, :densityWeighting included. A value that a parameter can't take, for example a solver given by name as in older versions, :solver => \"fista\", is ignored with a warning, and the parameter keeps its default; this does not hold for the four keys of the next paragraph.\n\nApart from :reco, which selects the reconstruction plan, the keys set the parameters of the same name in the plan, see Configure a Reconstruction with Plans. For :densityWeighting = false, the :weights of the iterative reconstruction types, :encodingOps and transform operators in :sparseTrafo, reconstruction first replaces the parameter type of the weighting, the encoding or the transform in the plan. These parameter types can also be given directly, for example :weightingParams => UniformWeightingParameters() or :encodingParams => CustomEncodingParameters(encodingOps = ops). reconstruct takes the same names as keywords and has the same defaults, for example reconstruct(\"standard\", acqData; solver = CGNR, reg = L2Regularization(1e-3)), but it needs the parameter types: it ignores the four keys with a warning, as it does every keyword that the plan doesn't have.\n\nThe plan of a reconstruction type shows the parameters that the keys set, for example for \"multiCoil\":\n\nusing MRIReco\nMRIRecoPlan(\"multiCoil\")\nRecoPlan{MultiCoilReconstruction}\n└─ parameter::RecoPlan{ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter}\n   ├─ reconSize\n   ├─ scheduler\n   ├─ arrayType\n   └─ parameter::RecoPlan{MultiCoilIterativeParameters}\n      ├─ weightingParams::RecoPlan{DensityWeightingParameters}\n      ├─ encodingParams::RecoPlan{EncodingParameters}\n      │  ├─ method\n      │  ├─ K\n      │  ├─ K_tol\n      │  ├─ correctionMap\n      │  ├─ kernelSize\n      │  ├─ oversamplingFactor\n      │  └─ toeplitz\n      ├─ coilParams::RecoPlan{CoilParameters}\n      │  ├─ noiseData\n      │  └─ senseMaps\n      └─ solverParams::RecoPlan{LeastSquaresSolverParameter}\n         ├─ rho\n         ├─ absTol\n         ├─ normalizeReg\n         ├─ relTol\n         ├─ tolInner\n         ├─ solver\n         ├─ regParams::RecoPlan{RegularizationParameters}\n         │  ├─ reg\n         │  └─ sparsityParams::RecoPlan{SimpleSparsityParameters}\n         │     └─ sparseTrafo\n         ├─ iterationsInner\n         ├─ restart\n         ├─ verbose\n         ├─ iterationsCG\n         ├─ iterations\n         └─ vary_rho\n","title":"Reconstruction Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Reconstruction-Type-and-Image","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":":reco – the reconstruction type: \"direct\", the default, \"standard\", \"multiEcho\", \"multiCoil\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" or \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\", see Reconstruction Types. It also takes the path of a saved plan, or its name if its folder was added with addMRIRecoPlanPath.\n:reconSize – the size of the image as a tuple, by default the encoding size of the data, encodingSize(acqData).\n:arrayType – the type of the arrays that the reconstruction computes with, by default Array. A GPU array type, such as CuArray, runs the reconstruction on the GPU, see Run a Reconstruction on the GPU.\n:scheduler – iterative types: how the independent problems of a reconstruction, for example of different slices, are distributed over the Julia threads. It takes :dynamic, :static, :greedy, :serial or a scheduler of OhMyThreads.jl. The default is :dynamic on the CPU and :serial on the GPU.","title":"Reconstruction Type and Image"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Encoding-Operator","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":"The iterative reconstruction types build their encoding operator from the data and these keys, see Signal Model and Encoding Operators:\n\n:correctionMap – the complex correction map for off-resonance and relaxation, in the precision of the data, by default none, see Correct Off-Resonance.\n:method – how a correction map is approximated: \"nfft\", the default, \"leastsquare\", \"hist\" or \"svd\".\n:K – the number of terms of \"leastsquare\" and \"hist\" and the maximum number of terms of \"svd\", by default 20. \"nfft\" determines the number itself.\n:K_tol – the tolerance with which \"svd\" chooses the number of terms, by default 1e-3.\n:toeplitz – whether the normal operator uses the Toeplitz approach, by default false, see The Normal Operator and the Toeplitz Approach.\n:oversamplingFactor and :kernelSize – the oversampling factor and the kernel size of the NFFT, by default 1.25 and 3.\n:encodingOps – \"standard\" and \"multiCoil\": encoding operators to use instead of the ones built from the data, as a matrix with one row per contrast and one column per slice, for example reshape(encodingOps_simple(acqData, reconSize), :, 1) for one slice; \"multiCoil\" takes the operators of encodingOps_parallel instead and still needs :senseMaps or :noiseData. The keys above then have no effect, and with :noiseData, \"multiCoil\" decorrelates the data but not these operators. Use Encoding Operators Directly describes the operators.\n\n:toeplitz, :oversamplingFactor and :kernelSize only take effect for non-Cartesian data without a correction map. Without a correction map, Cartesian data uses the FFT; with one, the operator always uses the defaults. The direct reconstruction takes its correction map from :cmap instead, always with the approximation \"nfft\" (#296).","title":"Encoding Operator"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Weighting","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":":densityWeighting – iterative types: whether the data term is weighted with the square roots of the density compensation factors, by default true, or with a constant, see Density Compensation.\n:weights – iterative types: weights to use instead of the density compensation, a vector with one vector per contrast and one weight per sample, in the form that samplingDensity returns.","title":"Weighting"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Coil-Sensitivities-and-Noise","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":"The reconstruction types \"multiCoil\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" take:\n\n:senseMaps – the coil sensitivities, a complex array with the dimensions x, y, z or slice, and coil, in the precision of the data, as espirit returns them when called on an AcquisitionData with nmaps = 1. It is required unless :encodingOps and :noiseData are given, see Multi-Coil Reconstruction.\n:noiseData – noise samples as a complex matrix of samples × coils in the precision of the data, by default none, see Noise Decorrelation.","title":"Coil Sensitivities and Noise"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Regularization","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":":reg – a regularization term of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, for example L2Regularization(λ), L1Regularization(λ) or TVRegularization(λ; shape), or a vector of terms. ADMM and SplitBregman take several terms; FISTA, POGM and OptISTA take one term and CGNR an L2Regularization, and these four also take the projections PositiveRegularization and RealRegularization. The default L1Regularization(0.0) doesn't regularize; CGNR therefore needs this key.\n:sparseTrafo – the transform in whose domain a term applies, by default none: \"Wavelet\", \"nothing\" for the identity, or an operator, for example LinearOperator(M) for a matrix M, see Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform. A single transform belongs to the first term; for several terms, a vector gives one transform per term, with nothing for a term without one. CGNR ignores the transform of its term.\n:normalizeReg – how the weight λ of the terms is scaled: NoNormalization(), the default, MeasurementBasedNormalization() or SystemMatrixBasedNormalization().","title":"Regularization"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Solver","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":":solver – the solver of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl: ADMM, the default, CGNR, FISTA, POGM, OptISTA or SplitBregman. Kaczmarz, DirectSolver and PseudoInverse don't work with the encoding operators.\n:iterations – the maximum number of (outer) iterations, by default 30.\n:rho – by default 0.05: the penalty parameter of ADMM and SplitBregman and the step size of FISTA, POGM and OptISTA. These three would otherwise determine the step size themselves, but the reconstruction always passes :rho (#305).\n:iterationsCG – ADMM and SplitBregman: the maximum number of inner conjugate gradient iterations.\n:iterationsInner – SplitBregman: the maximum number of inner iterations.\n:relTol – the relative tolerance of the stopping criterion of all solvers above; :absTol – the absolute tolerance of ADMM and SplitBregman; :tolInner – the tolerance of their inner conjugate gradient iterations.\n:restart – FISTA and POGM: :none, the default, or :gradient.\n:vary_rho – ADMM: :none, the default, :balance or :PnP.\n:verbose – all solvers above except CGNR: whether the solver prints its progress, by default false.\n\nWithout a value, the tolerances and inner iterations keep the defaults of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl. A solver ignores the keys that it doesn't take, and other keyword arguments of the solvers, such as precon and theta, can't be set.","title":"Solver"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Subspace-Reconstruction","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":":basis – required for \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\": the temporal basis as a complex matrix with one row per contrast and one column per basis function, in the precision of the data, see Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction.","title":"Subspace Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/parameters/#Limits","page":"Reconstruction Parameters","text":"With ADMM or SplitBregman, \"multiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" with more than one contrast and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" with more than one basis function fail for a regularization term without a transform; the transform \"nothing\", for example :sparseTrafo => \"nothing\", avoids this. Both solvers also fail for projection terms such as PositiveRegularization (#392, #396).\nFor data with several coils, :densityWeighting = false and :weights make the iterative reconstructions fail with a DimensionMismatch (#392, #396).\n:encodingOps fails for \"multiEcho\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" (#396).\nIf the correction map of a slice is spatially constant, for example zero while the other slices have a map, the default :method => \"nfft\" gives NaN for this slice and the other methods throw an error, except \"leastsquare\" for a constant other than zero; a map that is zero in every slice is not used. If all samples have the same time, as for AcquisitionData created from a k-space array, \"nfft\" gives NaN, \"leastsquare\" and \"hist\" throw an error, and \"svd\" does so in some runs (#360).\n\"hist\" fails for maps with both a relaxation and an off-resonance part and for many maps with only a relaxation part, and \"svd\" for maps whose off-resonance part is spatially constant, zero included (#360). \"svd\" also fails for Float32 data and is wrong without an error if the profiles have different sampling times or are too short (#363).\nThe correction map of 3D data makes the reconstruction fail with a MethodError (#361).","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/extract_kspace/#extract-kspace","page":"Extract Cartesian k-Space","text":"For Cartesian data, kDataCart returns the k-space of an AcquisitionData object as an array on the Cartesian grid, and AcquisitionData turns such an array back into acquisition data. In between, the k-space can be processed like any other array, for example transformed with FFTW.jl, undersampled or passed to other software, such as BART through BartIO.jl.","title":"Extract Cartesian k-Space"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/extract_kspace/#Extracting-the-k-Space","page":"Extract Cartesian k-Space","text":"The example simulates the k-space of a phantom with 128 × 96 pixels along a Cartesian trajectory and extracts it:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation, FFTW, CairoMakie\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nimage = shepp_logan(128)[:, 1:96]\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:trajName] = \"Cartesian\"\nparams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = 128\nparams[:numProfiles] = 96\nacqData = simulation(image, params)\n\nkspace = kDataCart(acqData)\nsize(kspace)\n(128, 96, 1, 1, 1, 1)\n\nThe array has six dimensions: x, the readout direction, y, and z or the slices, followed by the coils, the contrasts and the repetitions. kDataCart puts each sample at its position in the grid of the encoding size and fills the positions that were not measured with zeros.","title":"Extracting the k-Space"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/extract_kspace/#Reconstructing-with-an-Inverse-FFT","page":"Extract Cartesian k-Space","text":"The k-space follows the convention of the centered Fourier transform, with the center of k-space at index N ÷ 2 + 1 of a dimension of length N. An inverse FFT along x and y, and for a 3D volume also along z, therefore returns the image, while the coils and the other dimensions stay apart:\n\nimageFFT = fftshift(ifft(ifftshift(kspace, 1:2), 1:2), 1:2)\nimageFFT[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1] ≈ image\ntrue\n\nThis is what the direct reconstruction of the acquisition data computes, but its result has the contrasts in the fourth and the coils in the fifth dimension:\n\nrecoParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nrecoParams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nrecoParams[:reconSize] = (128, 96)\nreconstruction(acqData, recoParams) ≈ permutedims(imageFFT, (1, 2, 3, 5, 4, 6))\ntrue","title":"Reconstructing with an Inverse FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/extract_kspace/#Undersampling-and-Going-Back-to-Acquisition-Data","page":"Extract Cartesian k-Space","text":"To undersample the data, set the samples that are not measured to zero. The example keeps every second line in the phase encoding direction y, with a mask of Bools, which keeps the element type of the data:\n\nmask = falses(size(kspace))\nmask[:, 1:2:end, :, :, :, :] .= true\nacqUnder = AcquisitionData(kspace .* mask)\nlength(acqUnder.subsampleIndices[1])\n6144\n\nAcquisitionData interprets the array as follows:\n\nIt leaves out the samples that are zero in the first coil and the first repetition of each contrast and, for 2D data, in the first slice. The sampling pattern can therefore differ between the contrasts, but not between the coils, slices and repetitions, and a measured sample that happens to be exactly zero there is left out as well (#348).\nIt takes a third dimension longer than 1 as the second phase encoding direction of a 3D acquisition. For the slices of a 2D acquisition, pass enc2D = true; otherwise, it treats the slices as the partitions of a volume.\nThe array holds neither the field of view nor the sampling times, so acqUnder.fov, which sets the spacing of the image axes, and all sampling times are zero. acqUnder.fov = acqData.fov restores the field of view.\n\nThe direct reconstruction of the undersampled data shows the phantom and a copy shifted by half the field of view in y, horizontally in the figure, both with half the intensity, because the missing lines alias the image. The figure shows the magnitude of the extracted k-space on a logarithmic scale and the two reconstructions:\n\nxUnder = reconstruction(acqUnder, recoParams)\n\npanels = [log10.(abs.(kspace[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1]) .+ 1e-3) => \"k-space, logarithmic\",\n          abs.(imageFFT[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1]) => \"Inverse FFT\",\n          abs.(xUnder[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1]) => \"Every second line in y\"]\nfig = Figure(size = (940, 330))\nfor (i, (data, title)) in enumerate(panels)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = title, aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, data', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Undersampling and Going Back to Acquisition Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#coil-compression","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"Modern receive arrays have 16 to 64 coils. The memory and the reconstruction time grow with the number of coils, for iterative reconstructions roughly linearly. But the coils are not independent: neighboring coils see the object with similar sensitivities, so their signals are strongly correlated. Coil compression exploits this. It replaces the N_c physical coils by N_v  N_c virtual coils, which are linear combinations of the physical ones and carry most of the signal.\n\nThis guide explains how coil compression works and shows how to\n\nchoose the number of virtual coils,\ncompress the data with the two methods of MRICoilSensitivities, softwareCoilCompression and geometricCoilCompression,\nuse the compressed data in a reconstruction with coil sensitivities.\n\nWe use the fully sampled knee data from Direct Reconstruction, a 2D Cartesian slice acquired with 8 coils. Eight coils is few, so the gain in reconstruction time is small here, but the principle is the same for larger arrays.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling, MRICoilSensitivities, CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra: svdvals\n\nfunction showimages(images, titles; columns = length(images))\n    fig = Figure(size = (300 * columns, 310 * cld(length(images), columns)))\n    for (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip(images, titles))\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(i, columns)...], title = title,\n                  aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n        heatmap!(ax, image', colormap = :grays)\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n    end\n    return fig\nend","title":"Compress Multi-Coil Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#Loading-the-Data","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"As in the other tutorials, the reference is the direct reconstruction with the coil images combined by the root sum of squares:\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfilename = joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")\nacqData = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(filename))\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\nrootSumOfSquares(images) = sqrt.(sum(abs2, images, dims = 5))[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1]\ncoilImages = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nreferenceImage = rootSumOfSquares(coilImages)\ndeviation(image) = round(nrmsd(referenceImage, image), sigdigits = 2)\n\nThe k-space data of a contrast, slice and repetition is stored as a matrix with one column per coil:\n\nkdata = acqData.kdata[1, 1, 1]\nsize(kdata)\n(102400, 8)","title":"Loading the Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#How-Coil-Compression-Works","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"Let mathbfK be this N_s times N_c matrix, with N_s samples and N_c coils. A virtual coil is a linear combination of the columns of mathbfK. With a compression matrix mathbfA of size N_c times N_v, the data of the N_v virtual coils is\n\nmathbfK_v = mathbfKmathbfA\n\nThe best choice of mathbfA, in the sense that mathbfK_v keeps as much of the energy of mathbfK as possible, follows from the singular value decomposition mathbfK = mathbfUmathbfSigmamathbfV^H: mathbfA consists of the first N_v columns of mathbfV, the right singular vectors belonging to the largest singular values. The columns of mathbfA are orthonormal, so the compression is a rotation of the coils followed by discarding the weakest virtual coils.\n\nThe singular values sigma_i tell how much signal each virtual coil carries. Here, the first virtual coil already contains about 70 % of the energy sum_i sigma_i^2:\n\nσ = svdvals(kdata)\nenergy = cumsum(σ .^ 2) ./ sum(σ .^ 2)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (700, 300))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"Virtual coil\", xticks = 1:8, ylabel = \"σᵢ / σ₁\", title = \"Singular values\")\nscatterlines!(ax1, σ ./ σ[1])\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"Number of virtual coils\", xticks = 1:8, ylabel = \"Fraction\",\n           title = \"Cumulative energy\")\nscatterlines!(ax2, energy)\nylims!(ax2, 0, 1.02)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA common rule of thumb is to keep enough virtual coils to retain 95 to 99 % of the energy. For this data set, that means 4 to 6 of the 8 coils.\n\nnote: Note\nThe compression matrix only depends on the correlation between the coils, not on the image. It can therefore be computed from a subset of the data, for example the first contrast or repetition, or the fully sampled calibration region, and applied to the rest. The keywords sContr and sRep select the contrast and repetition used for this calibration.","title":"How Coil Compression Works"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#Software-Coil-Compression","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"softwareCoilCompression computes a single compression matrix for all the data, as described above (Huang et al., 2008). Applied to an AcquisitionData, it returns the compressed data and the compression matrices, one per slice:\n\nnumVC = 4\nacqDataSCC, ccMatSCC = softwareCoilCompression(acqData, numVC)\nsize(acqDataSCC.kdata[1, 1, 1]), size(ccMatSCC[1])\n((102400, 4), (8, 4))\n\nThe images of the virtual coils show how the signal is concentrated: the first virtual coil looks like a coil combined image, the following ones carry less and less signal.\n\ncoilImagesSCC = reconstruction(acqDataSCC, params)\nshowimages([abs.(coilImagesSCC[:, :, 1, 1, c, 1]) for c in 1:numVC],\n           [\"Virtual coil $c\" for c in 1:numVC])\nFigure()\n\nThe root sum of squares of the virtual coil images is close to the reference. Because the compression matrix has orthonormal columns, keeping all 8 virtual coils would reproduce the reference exactly:\n\nimageSCC = rootSumOfSquares(coilImagesSCC)\nshowimages([referenceImage, imageSCC, 10 .* abs.(referenceImage .- imageSCC)],\n           [\"8 coils\", \"$numVC virtual coils (SCC), NRMSD $(deviation(imageSCC))\",\n            \"Difference × 10\"])\nFigure()","title":"Software Coil Compression"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#Geometric-Coil-Compression","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"The coil sensitivities vary in space, so the best combination of the coils is not the same everywhere. For Cartesian data, the readout direction is always fully sampled. geometricCoilCompression (Zhang et al.,\n\nuses this: it applies the inverse Fourier transform along the readout direction, computes one compression\n\nmatrix for each position x along this direction, and transforms the compressed data back to k-space.\n\nThe SVD determines each matrix only up to a rotation of the virtual coils. Neighboring positions would then get virtual coils that do not match, which creates artifacts in the reconstructed images. The matrices are therefore aligned: starting from the center, each matrix is rotated to be as close as possible to its neighbor, so that the virtual coils vary smoothly along x.\n\nThe keyword dim selects the fully sampled direction (1 by default). The compression matrices are returned as an array of size N_x times N_c times N_v:\n\nacqDataGCC, ccMatGCC = geometricCoilCompression(acqData, numVC)\nsize(ccMatGCC)\n(320, 8, 4)\n\nwarning: Warning\ngeometricCoilCompression requires Cartesian data in which dimension dim is fully sampled; otherwise it throws an ArgumentError. The other dimensions can be undersampled: the result keeps the sampling pattern of the input.\n\nFor the same number of virtual coils, geometric coil compression keeps slightly more of the signal:\n\nimageGCC = rootSumOfSquares(reconstruction(acqDataGCC, params))\nshowimages([referenceImage, imageSCC, imageGCC],\n           [\"8 coils\", \"SCC, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSCC))\", \"GCC, NRMSD $(deviation(imageGCC))\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe difference between the methods depends on the number of virtual coils:\n\nnumVCs = 1:7\ndeviationsSCC = [nrmsd(referenceImage, rootSumOfSquares(reconstruction(\n                    first(softwareCoilCompression(acqData, n)), params))) for n in numVCs]\ndeviationsGCC = [nrmsd(referenceImage, rootSumOfSquares(reconstruction(\n                    first(geometricCoilCompression(acqData, n)), params))) for n in numVCs]\n\nfig = Figure(size = (450, 320))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"Number of virtual coils\", xticks = numVCs, ylabel = \"NRMSD\", yscale = log10)\nscatterlines!(ax, numVCs, deviationsSCC, label = \"SCC\")\nscatterlines!(ax, numVCs, deviationsGCC, label = \"GCC\")\naxislegend(ax)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Geometric Coil Compression"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#Compression-and-Coil-Sensitivities","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"A SENSE reconstruction, as in Multi-Coil Reconstruction, needs one sensitivity map per coil. After compression, the data belong to virtual coils, so the sensitivities must be those of the virtual coils. We undersample the data as in that tutorial and reconstruct with all 8 coils for comparison:\n\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30)\nsensitivities = espirit(acqDataSub, (6, 6), 30, eigThresh_2 = 0)\n\nparamsSENSE = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparamsSENSE[:reco] = \"multiCoil\"\nparamsSENSE[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\nparamsSENSE[:solver] = CGNR\nparamsSENSE[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nparamsSENSE[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparamsSENSE[:iterations] = 10\nparamsSENSE[:senseMaps] = sensitivities\nimageSENSE = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, paramsSENSE)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n\nWe compress the undersampled data with softwareCoilCompression. It only uses the measured samples, so it can be applied directly to undersampled data:\n\nacqDataSubCC, ccMatSub = softwareCoilCompression(acqDataSub, numVC)\n\nThere are two ways to obtain the sensitivities of the virtual coils.\n\nEstimate them from the compressed data. This is the simplest option: the virtual coils are treated like physical ones.\n\nsensitivitiesCC = espirit(acqDataSubCC, (6, 6), 30, eigThresh_2 = 0)\nparamsSENSE[:senseMaps] = sensitivitiesCC\nimageSENSE_CC = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSubCC, paramsSENSE)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n\nCompress existing sensitivities. The data of coil c are the Fourier transform of S_cx. A virtual coil combines the data with the weights A_cv, so its sensitivity is the same combination of the coil sensitivities:\n\nS_v(mathbfr) = sum_c S_c(mathbfr) A_cv\n\napplyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps computes this product along the coil dimension. It accepts the vector of matrices returned by softwareCoilCompression, one per slice:\n\nsensitivitiesCC2 = applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps(sensitivities, ccMatSub)\nparamsSENSE[:senseMaps] = sensitivitiesCC2\nimageSENSE_CC2 = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSubCC, paramsSENSE)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n\nshowimages([imageSENSE, imageSENSE_CC, imageSENSE_CC2],\n           [\"8 coils, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE))\",\n            \"$numVC VC, ESPIRiT after CC, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE_CC))\",\n            \"$numVC VC, compressed maps, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE_CC2))\"])\nFigure()\n\nWith 4 instead of 8 coils, the reconstruction is barely worse, while each iteration only has to process half the data.\n\nThe same works for geometric coil compression. The profiles are missing along the second dimension, so the first dimension (the readout) is fully sampled and can be used with dim = 1. The compression matrix now depends on the position x along this dimension, and the sensitivities are compressed row by row: S_v(x y) = sum_c S_c(x y) A_cv(x). No Fourier transform is needed, because the sensitivities are already in image space:\n\nacqDataSubGCC, ccMatSubGCC = geometricCoilCompression(acqDataSub, numVC, dim = 1)\nparamsSENSE[:senseMaps] = applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps(sensitivities, ccMatSubGCC, dim = 1)\nimageSENSE_GCC = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSubGCC, paramsSENSE)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nshowimages([imageSENSE, imageSENSE_CC2, imageSENSE_GCC],\n           [\"8 coils, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE))\",\n            \"$numVC VC (SCC), NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE_CC2))\",\n            \"$numVC VC (GCC), NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE_GCC))\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe same matrices can be applied to other data with applyCoilCompression, for example to another AcquisitionData measured with the same coils, or to the k-space returned by kDataCart:\n\nkspaceCC = applyCoilCompression(kDataCart(acqDataSub), ccMatSub)\nsize(kspaceCC)\n(320, 320, 1, 4, 1, 1)","title":"Compression and Coil Sensitivities"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#Summary","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"Look at the singular values to choose the number of virtual coils.\nsoftwareCoilCompression works for any trajectory and for undersampled data.\ngeometricCoilCompression is restricted to Cartesian data with a fully sampled direction, usually the readout, and keeps a bit more signal for the same number of virtual coils.\nFor SENSE reconstructions, either estimate the sensitivities from the compressed data, or compress the existing sensitivities with the same matrix as the data (applyCoilCompressionSensitivityMaps).","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/coil_compression/#References","page":"Compress Multi-Coil Data","text":"Huang F, Vijayakumar S, Li Y, Hertel S, Duensing GR. A software channel compression technique for faster reconstruction with many channels. Magnetic Resonance Imaging 2008;26:133–141. doi:10.1016/j.mri.2007.04.010\nZhang T, Pauly JM, Vasanawala SS, Lustig M. Coil compression for accelerated imaging with Cartesian sampling. Magnetic Resonance in Medicine 2013;69:571–582. doi:10.1002/mrm.24267\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/inverse_problem/#inverse-problem","page":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem","text":"The function reconstruction computes an image from the measured data with the encoding operator described in Signal Model and Encoding Operators. This page explains the problem it solves, the building blocks it uses and how the reconstruction types differ.","title":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/inverse_problem/#The-Inverse-Problem","page":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem","text":"With the encoding operator mathbfE, the measured samples mathbfb and the unknown image mathbfx satisfy mathbfb approx mathbfEmathbfx, up to noise and model errors. Computing mathbfx from mathbfb is an inverse problem. It is usually ill-posed: for undersampled data, many images fit the data equally well, and noise in the data can be strongly amplified. The iterative reconstructions therefore solve a regularized least-squares problem, like equation (1) in the MRIReco.jl paper but with the weights mathbfW and a factor frac12:\n\nundersetmathbfxoperatornameargmin  frac12lVert mathbfW(mathbfEmathbfx - mathbfb) rVert_2^2 + mathrmR(mathbfx)\n\nThe regularization term mathrmR expresses prior knowledge about the image, for example that it consists of regions with constant intensity or has few large wavelet coefficients, and the diagonal matrix mathbfW weights the samples, as described below. Iterative Reconstruction shows how the choice of the regularization and of its weight λ affects the image. The same problem can also be solved on a GPU, as described in Run a Reconstruction on the GPU.\n\nThe direct reconstruction, \"direct\", doesn't solve this problem. It applies the adjoint of the encoding operator to the weighted data, mathbfx = mathbfE^mathsfHmathbfW^2mathbfb. For fully sampled Cartesian data, this is the inverse FFT, and for non-Cartesian data, it is the gridding reconstruction. With trajectories that cover a circular region of k-space, such as radial and spiral ones, the image is then set to zero outside a circle, or in 3D a sphere, with the diameter of its longest side.","title":"The Inverse Problem"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/inverse_problem/#Density-Compensation","page":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem","text":"Non-Cartesian trajectories sample k-space unevenly, radial trajectories for example much more densely at the center than at the edges. The density compensation factors on the diagonal of mathbfW^2 compensate for this in the direct reconstruction. For non-Cartesian data, MRIReco.jl computes them with the iterative method of Pipe and Menon, and for Cartesian data, they are constant. samplingDensity returns their square roots, the diagonal of mathbfW. The iterative reconstructions weight the data term with mathbfW as well, which speeds up the convergence of the solvers. With :densityWeighting = false, they use a constant mathbfW instead.","title":"Density Compensation"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/inverse_problem/#Solvers-and-Regularization","page":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem","text":"MRIReco.jl takes the solvers and the regularization terms from RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, as described in section 2.5 of the paper:\n\nThe parameter :solver selects the solver, for example CGNR, the conjugate gradient method applied to the normal equations, which supports an ell_2 regularization, or ADMM and FISTA, which also support non-smooth terms such as the ell_1 norm and total variation. These solvers only need the encoding operator, its adjoint and the normal operator.\nThe parameter :reg sets the regularization term, for example L2Regularization(λ), L1Regularization(λ) or TVRegularization(λ; shape). Each term provides its proximal map, which proximal solvers such as ADMM and FISTA use.\nThe parameter :sparseTrafo applies the regularization to a transformed image. With \"Wavelet\", an ell_1 regularization penalizes lVert mathbfPhimathbfx rVert_1 instead of lVert mathbfx rVert_1, where mathbfPhi is the wavelet transform. Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform shows how to use other transforms.\nThe parameter :normalizeReg scales λ. By default, λ is used as given, and with MeasurementBasedNormalization, it is scaled with the magnitude of the data, so that for terms such as the ell_1 norm and total variation, the same value works for data of different scale.\n\nThe examples in the paper select the solver and the regularization by name, with params[:solver] = \"admm\" and params[:regularization] = \"TV\" together with params[:λ]. The current version takes them as types and objects, params[:solver] = ADMM and params[:reg] = TVRegularization(λ; shape), and the other parameters of the solvers have the names of their keyword arguments in RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, for example :rho instead of :ρ for the penalty parameter of ADMM and :iterationsCG instead of :iterationsInner for its inner CG iterations. The old names, such as :regularization, :λ and :ρ, are ignored.","title":"Solvers and Regularization"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/inverse_problem/#Reconstruction-Types","page":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem","text":"The reconstruction type, :reco, decides which data is reconstructed together and with which encoding operator. Different slices and repetitions are always reconstructed separately.\n\n:reco Reconstructed together Encoding operator\n\"direct\" the data of one coil and contrast Fourier encoding, only its adjoint is applied\n\"standard\" the data of one coil and contrast Fourier encoding\n\"multiEcho\" all contrasts of one coil Fourier encoding of each contrast\n\"multiCoil\" all coils of one contrast coil sensitivities and Fourier encoding\n\"multiCoilMultiEcho\" all coils and contrasts coil sensitivities and Fourier encoding of each contrast\n\"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" all coils and contrasts as for \"multiCoilMultiEcho\", applied to the images of a temporal basis\n\nThe reconstruction types with coil sensitivities, which :senseMaps provides, combine the data of all coils into one image. The subspace reconstruction represents the images of all contrasts by a few coefficient images in a temporal basis, which :basis provides, and reconstructs these coefficient images, as shown in Subspace Reconstruction.","title":"Reconstruction Types"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/inverse_problem/#Noise-Decorrelation","page":"Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem","text":"The noise of different receive coils is usually correlated. If a noise measurement is given with :noiseData, the reconstruction types \"multiCoil\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" estimate the noise covariance matrix mathbfPsi from it, compute its Cholesky factorization mathbfPsi = mathbfLmathbfL^mathsfH and apply mathbfL^-1 to the data and to the coil sensitivities. This decorrelates the noise, which the least-squares problem assumes to be uncorrelated and of equal variance in all coils. The other reconstruction types ignore :noiseData. With several contrasts, \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" currently decorrelate the data wrongly, which gives a wrong image without a warning (#338).","title":"Noise Decorrelation"},{"category":"section","location":"gettingStarted/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"gettingStarted/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"MRIReco.jl requires Julia 1.10 or newer. Install the main package with\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"MRIReco\")\n\nusing MRIReco also loads MRIBase, MRIOperators and RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, which provides the solvers. The other packages are installed in the same way when you need them, for example Pkg.add(\"MRIFiles\") to read raw data files. The examples in this documentation also use CairoMakie for plots and ImageUtils for test images.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"gettingStarted/#Development-Version","page":"Getting Started","text":"The development documentation describes the master branch, which can contain changes that are not released yet. To use master, add the package and the packages of this repository that it depends on, each with rev = \"master\". Otherwise Pkg takes these dependencies from the registry, and their released versions may not work with master. For MRIReco, which depends on MRIBase and MRIOperators:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add([PackageSpec(name = \"MRIBase\", rev = \"master\"),\n         PackageSpec(name = \"MRIOperators\", rev = \"master\"),\n         PackageSpec(name = \"MRIReco\", rev = \"master\")])\n\nFor other packages, such as MRIFiles or MRISimulation, the table of packages on the Home page lists the packages of this repository that they depend on. Pkg.update() moves the packages to the newest commit of master, and Pkg.free([\"MRIBase\", \"MRIOperators\", \"MRIReco\"]) returns them to their released versions. To work on the packages themselves, follow the development setup of the contributing guide.","title":"Development Version"},{"category":"section","location":"gettingStarted/#A-First-Reconstruction","page":"Getting Started","text":"This example simulates k-space data of a Shepp-Logan phantom along a radial trajectory and reconstructs an image from it. Besides MRIReco it needs MRISimulation, ImageUtils and CairoMakie.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation, CairoMakie\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nN = 256\nphantom = shepp_logan(N)\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:simulation] = \"fast\"\nparams[:trajName] = \"Radial\"\nparams[:numProfiles] = floor(Int64, pi/2 * N)\nparams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = 2N\nacqData = simulation(phantom, params)\n\nacqData is an AcquisitionData object that holds the simulated k-space data together with the trajectory. A direct reconstruction applies the adjoint of the Fourier encoding to the density-compensated data:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (N, N)\nimage = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nsize(image)\n(256, 256, 1, 1, 1, 1)\n\nThe reconstructed image always has six dimensions: x, y, z, echoes, coils and repetitions. Here only the first two have more than one entry:\n\nimages = [phantom, abs.(image[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])]\ntitles = [\"Phantom\", \"Reconstruction\"]\nfig = Figure(size = (700, 350))\nfor i in 1:2\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true, title = titles[i])\n    heatmap!(ax, images[i]', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"A First Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"gettingStarted/#Next-Steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"The tutorials walk through complete reconstructions step by step, starting with Direct Reconstruction and Iterative Reconstruction.\nThe how-to guides show how to solve specific tasks, such as loading and saving raw data, binning profiles, extracting Cartesian k-space data or running a reconstruction on the GPU.\nThe API reference lists the functions and types of each package.","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/gpu/#gpu-reconstruction","page":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU","text":"MRIReco.jl can run reconstructions on a GPU. This guide shows how to do this on an NVIDIA GPU with CUDA.jl. Whether the GPU is faster than the CPU depends on the size of the problem and on the hardware. Iterative reconstructions, which apply the encoding operator many times, benefit the most. GPU Acceleration explains how MRIReco.jl runs on the GPU.","title":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/gpu/#Installing-CUDA.jl","page":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU","text":"MRIReco.jl loads its GPU code together with a GPU package, which should be installed in the same environment as MRIReco.jl. For NVIDIA GPUs, this is CUDA.jl:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"CUDA\")\n\nCUDA.jl only needs the driver of the GPU and downloads the CUDA libraries itself. After using CUDA, CUDA.functional() returns true if CUDA.jl can use the GPU, and CUDA.versioninfo() lists the GPUs and the versions of the libraries.\n\nFor AMD GPUs, the GPU package is AMDGPU.jl, and its array type ROCArray takes the place of CuArray below.","title":"Installing CUDA.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/gpu/#Choosing-the-Array-Type","page":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU","text":"The parameter arrayType sets the type of the arrays that the reconstruction computes with. The default Array keeps the reconstruction on the CPU, and the GPU array type CuArray moves it to the GPU:\n\nusing MRIReco, CUDA\nparams[:arrayType] = CuArray\n\nPass the type without parameters. MRIReco.jl adds the element type, which follows from the data, so for example CuArray{ComplexF32} fails.\n\nApart from this parameter, the reconstruction is set up as on the CPU. The documentation is built on a computer without a GPU, so the following example uses gpu = Array and runs on the CPU. With an NVIDIA GPU, gpu = CuArray runs it on the GPU. The example reconstructs the knee data from Direct Reconstruction with CG-SENSE:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRICoilSensitivities\ngpu = Array # with an NVIDIA GPU: using CUDA; gpu = CuArray\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nacqData = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")))\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"multiCoil\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\nparams[:senseMaps] = espirit(acqData)\nparams[:solver] = CGNR\nparams[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nparams[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparams[:iterations] = 10\nparams[:arrayType] = gpu\nimage = reconstruction(acqData, params)\n\nOnly the reconstruction itself runs on the GPU. Reading the data and estimating the coil sensitivities with espirit happen on the CPU, and reconstruction copies the image back to the CPU. It returns the same six-dimensional AxisArray as without GPU, so the code after the reconstruction stays the same. Because the GPU computes in a different order, its result differs from the CPU result by rounding errors. With non-Cartesian data, the difference is larger, because the NFFT evaluates its window function differently on the CPU and the GPU, but it is still far smaller than the error of the reconstruction itself.","title":"Choosing the Array Type"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/gpu/#Precision","page":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU","text":"The reconstruction computes in the precision of the data. MRIFiles reads raw data in single precision, as the type parameter of the knee data shows:\n\ntypeof(acqData)\nAcquisitionData{Float32, 2}\n\nMost GPUs compute much faster in single than in double precision, so single-precision data is preferable on the GPU.","title":"Precision"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/gpu/#Selecting-a-GPU","page":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU","text":"CUDA.jl uses the first GPU by default. On a computer with several GPUs, select another one with CUDA.device! before the reconstruction, for example the second GPU with CUDA.device!(1).","title":"Selecting a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/gpu/#Current-Limitations","page":"Run a Reconstruction on the GPU","text":"Some parts of MRIReco.jl don't work on the GPU yet:\n\nWith several Julia threads, \"multiCoil\" and \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" give wrong results on the GPU (#317). Start Julia with one thread, which is the default, for these reconstructions.\nNoise decorrelation with :noiseData fails on the GPU with Julia 1.12 and 1.13. With Julia 1.10 and 1.11, it runs with a wrong decorrelation matrix (#319).\n\"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" fails on the GPU (#320).\nWith AMD GPUs, encoding operators that are built on the CPU and passed with :encodingOps can crash Julia (#393).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Current Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase","page":"MRIBase","text":"MRIBase defines the data types that the other packages build on: raw data organized like an ISMRMRD file (RawAcquisitionData), k-space data prepared for reconstruction (AcquisitionData) and k-space trajectories (Trajectory), together with functions to access and convert them. MRIBase does not depend on other packages of this repository. MRIReco re-exports it, so using MRIReco makes all names on this page available.","title":"MRIBase"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIBase/#Raw-Data","page":"MRIBase","text":"","title":"Raw Data"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIBase/#Flags","page":"MRIBase","text":"The guide Select and Prepare Profiles shows how to use the flags. Each name in FLAGS is also exported as a UInt64 bit mask, for example ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT, for working with head.flags directly.","title":"Flags"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIBase/#AcquisitionData","page":"MRIBase","text":"","title":"AcquisitionData"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIBase/#Trajectories","page":"MRIBase","text":"The guide Create a Trajectory shows how to create trajectories.","title":"Trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIBase/#Predefined-Trajectories","page":"MRIBase","text":"","title":"Predefined Trajectories"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData","page":"MRIBase","text":"RawAcquisitionData object.\n\nFields\n\nparams::Dict{String, Any} - Dict containing the information of the XML header in ISMRMRD\nprofiles::Vector{Profile} - Vector containing all the profiles of the acquisition\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.Profile","page":"MRIBase","text":"Struct to describe data from one profile of a trajectory (from all coils).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.Profile"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.AcquisitionHeader","page":"MRIBase","text":"Header of a profile of a RawAcquisitionData object, with the fields of the acquisition header of the ISMRMRD format, for example the number of samples number_of_samples, the time between two samples sample_time_us in µs, the position and orientation of the slice and the encoding counters idx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.AcquisitionHeader"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.EncodingCounters","page":"MRIBase","text":"Encoding counters used in each Profile of a RawAcquisitionData object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.EncodingCounters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.Limit","page":"MRIBase","text":"Limit(minimum, maximum, center)\n\nMinimum, maximum and center of one of the encoding limits in the ISMRMRD header, for example of the phase encoding steps. When an ISMRMRD file is read, the limits of the k-space encoding steps, averages, slices, contrasts, phases, repetitions, sets and segments in the header are stored in params with the prefix enc_lim_, for example params[\"enc_lim_kspace_encoding_step_1\"].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.Limit"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.MeasurementDependency","page":"MRIBase","text":"MeasurementDependency(dependencyType, measurementID)\n\nMeasurement that the data depends on, from the ISMRMRD header, for example a noise measurement with the dependencyType \"Noise\". When an ISMRMRD file is read, it is stored in params[\"measurementDependency\"], as a vector if there are several.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.MeasurementDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.CoilDescription","page":"MRIBase","text":"CoilDescription(number, name)\n\nNumber and name of a receive coil from the element coilLabel of the ISMRMRD header. When an ISMRMRD file is read, the coils are stored in params[\"coilLabel\"], as a vector if there are several.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.CoilDescription"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"RawAcquisitionData(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nconverts acqData into the equivalent RawAcquisitionData object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.AcquisitionData-Tuple{RawAcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"AcquisitionData(f::RawAcquisitionData; estimateProfileCenter::Bool=false, OffsetBruker=false)\n\nconverts RawAcquisitionData into the equivalent AcquisitionData object. If OffsetBruker=true, add a phase offset to correct position only along Y/Z direction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.AcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.minimalHeader-Tuple{Any, Tuple{AbstractFloat, AbstractFloat, AbstractFloat}}","page":"MRIBase","text":"minimalHeader(encodingSize, fov::NTuple{3,AbstractFloat};\n              f_res::Integer=1, tr_name::AbstractString=\"cartesian\", numChannels::Int=1)\n\nreturns a parameter dictionary for RawAcquisitionData with the entries \"encodedSize\" and \"encodedFOV\", which hold encodingSize and fov (in mm) as vectors, \"trajectory\" (tr_name), \"receiverChannels\" (numChannels) and \"H1resonanceFrequency_Hz\" (f_res). encodingSize is the size of the encoded grid, for example (256, 256, 1) for 2D data. These entries suffice to convert raw data with Cartesian profiles into AcquisitionData.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.minimalHeader"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numChannels-Tuple{RawAcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numChannels(f::RawAcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the number of channels in a RawAcquisitionData object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numChannels"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.trajectory-Tuple{RawAcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"trajectory(f::RawAcquisitionData; slice::Int=1, contrast::Int=1)\n\nreturns the Trajectory for given slice and contrast of a RawAcquisitionData object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.trajectory"},{"category":"constant","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.FLAGS","page":"MRIBase","text":"FLAGS::Dict{String, Int}\n\nA dictionary mapping string keys (representing flag names) to bit positions, counting from 1 for the least significant bit. Flags are used to indicate specific attributes of the corresponding Profile. Example flag names include \"ACQ_FIRST_IN_ENCODE_STEP1\", \"ACQ_LAST_IN_SLICE\", etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.FLAGS"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.create_flag_bitmask-Tuple{T} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"create_flag_bitmask(flag::AbstractString) -> UInt64\ncreate_flag_bitmask(flag::Integer) -> UInt64\n\nCreates a bitmask for the given flag.\n\nIf the flag is a string, its integer value is looked up in FLAGS.\nIf the flag is an integer, it must be positive. The bitmask is created by left-shifting 1 to the corresponding bit position.\n\nArguments\n\nflag: A string representing the flag name or a positive integer.\n\nReturns\n\nA UInt64 bitmask where only the bit corresponding to the flag is set.\n\nThrows\n\nKeyError if the string flag is not found in FLAGS.\nDomainError if the integer flag is not positive.\n\nExample\n\ncreate_flag_bitmask(\"ACQ_FIRST_IN_ENCODE_STEP1\")  # 0x0000000000000001\ncreate_flag_bitmask(2)                           # 0x0000000000000002\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.create_flag_bitmask"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.filter_raw_by_flags-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{RawAcquisitionData, Union{Vector{T}, T}}} where T<:Union{Int64, AbstractString}","page":"MRIBase","text":"filter_raw_by_flags(rawData::RawAcquisitionData, flags::Vector{String})\n\nFilters the profiles in a RawAcquisitionData object based on specified flags.\n\nArguments\n\nrawData::RawAcquisitionData: The RawAcquisitionData object to filter.\nflags::Vector{String}: A vector of flag names to filter the profiles by.\n\nReturns\n\nRawAcquisitionData: A new RawAcquisitionData object containing only the profiles that have all the specified flags set.\n\nExamples\n\n# Assuming `rawData` is a RawAcquisitionData object\nfilteredData = filter_raw_by_flags(rawData, [\"ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.filter_raw_by_flags"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.flag_is_set-Tuple{Profile, Any}","page":"MRIBase","text":"flag_is_set(obj::Profile, flag) -> Bool\nflag_is_set(head::AcquisitionHeader, flag) -> Bool\n\nChecks whether a specific flag is set in the given Profile or AcquisitionHeader object.\n\nArguments\n\nobj: A Profile or AcquisitionHeader object field containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nflag: A string (flag name) or an integer (bit position).\n\nReturns\n\ntrue if the flag is set in the obj.head.flags bitmask; otherwise, false.\n\nExample\n\nflag_is_set(profile, \"ACQ_FIRST_IN_ENCODE_STEP1\")  # true or false\nflag_is_set(head, \"ACQ_FIRST_IN_ENCODE_STEP1\")  # true or false\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.flag_is_set"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.flag_remove!-Tuple{Profile, Any}","page":"MRIBase","text":"flag_remove!(obj::Profile, flag)\nflag_remove!(head::AcquisitionHeader, flag)\n\nRemoves (clears) the specified flag in the given Profile or AcquisitionHeader object.\n\nArguments\n\nobj: A Profile object with a head field containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nhead: An AcquisitionHeader object containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nflag: A string (flag name) or an integer (bit position), or a vector of such values.\n\nModifies\n\nobj.head.flags or head.flags by clearing the bit corresponding to the flag.\n\nExample\n\nflag_remove!(profile, \"ACQ_LAST_IN_PHASE\")\nflag_remove!(profile, [\"ACQ_LAST_IN_PHASE\",\"ACQ_USER8\"])\n\nhead = AcquisitionHeader()\nflag_remove!(head, \"ACQ_LAST_IN_PHASE\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.flag_remove!"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.flag_remove_all!-Tuple{Profile}","page":"MRIBase","text":"flag_remove_all!(obj::Profile)\nflag_remove_all!(head::AcquisitionHeader)\n\nClears all flags in the given Profile object or AcquisitionHeader object.\n\nArguments\n\nobj: A Profile object with a head field containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nhead: An AcquisitionHeader object containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\n\nModifies\n\nobj.head.flags or head.flags, setting it to 0 (all flags cleared).\n\nExample\n\nflag_remove_all!(profile)\nflag_remove_all!(head)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.flag_remove_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.flag_set!-Tuple{Profile, Any}","page":"MRIBase","text":"flag_set!(obj::Profile, flag)\nflag_set!(head::AcquisitionHeader, flag)\n\nSets the specified flag in the given Profile or AcquisitionHeader object.\n\nArguments\n\nobj: A Profile object with a head field containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nhead: An AcquisitionHeader object containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nflag: A string (flag name) or an integer (bit position), or a vector of such values.\n\nModifies\n\nobj.head.flags or head.flags by setting the bit corresponding to the flag.\n\nExample\n\nflag_set!(profile, \"ACQ_FIRST_IN_SLICE\")\nflag_set!(profile, [\"ACQ_LAST_IN_PHASE\",\"ACQ_USER8\"])\n\nhead = AcquisitionHeader()\nflag_set!(head, \"ACQ_FIRST_IN_SLICE\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.flag_set!"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.flags_of-Tuple{Profile}","page":"MRIBase","text":"flags_of(obj::Profile) -> Vector{String}\nflags_of(head::AcquisitionHeader) -> Vector{String}\n\nReturns a list of all flags that are set in the given Profile object or AcquisitionHeader object.\n\nArguments\n\nobj: A Profile object with a head field containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\nhead: An AcquisitionHeader object containing a flags attribute (as a bitmask).\n\nReturns\n\nAn array of strings representing the names of the flags that are currently set.\n\nExample\n\nflags = flags_of(profile)  # [\"ACQ_FIRST_IN_ENCODE_STEP1\", \"ACQ_LAST_IN_SLICE\"]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.flags_of"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.remove_raw_by_flags","page":"MRIBase","text":"remove_raw_by_flags(rawData::RawAcquisitionData, flags::Vector{String} = [\"ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT\", \"ACQ_IS_PARALLEL_CALIBRATION\", \"ACQ_IS_NAVIGATION_DATA\", \"ACQ_IS_PHASECORR_DATA\", \"ACQ_IS_DUMMYSCAN_DATA\", \"ACQ_IS_PHASE_STABILIZATION_REFERENCE\", \"ACQ_IS_PHASE_STABILIZATION\"])\n\nRemoves profiles from a RawAcquisitionData object based on specified flags.\n\nArguments\n\nrawData::RawAcquisitionData: The RawAcquisitionData object to filter.\nflags::Vector{String}: A vector of flag names to filter the profiles by. Defaults to a list of common flags indicating non-imaging data.\n\nReturns\n\nRawAcquisitionData: A new RawAcquisitionData object containing only the profiles that do not have any of the specified flags set.\n\nExamples\n\n# Assuming `rawData` is a RawAcquisitionData object\nfilteredData = remove_raw_by_flags(rawData)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.remove_raw_by_flags"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.AcquisitionData","page":"MRIBase","text":"struct describing MRI acquisition data with floating point precision T and D encoding dimensions (2 or 3).\n\nFields\n\nsequenceInfo::Dict{Symbol,Any}          - additional information on the pulse sequence\ntraj::Vector{Trajectory{T}}             - trajectories for each echo/contrast\nkdata::Array{Matrix{Complex{T}},3}      - each matrix contains data for one trajectory                                             (1. dim k-space nodes, 2. dim coils)                                             the outer dims describe:                                             1. dim echoes, 2. dim slices, 3. dim repetitions\nsubsampleIndices::Vector{Vector{Int64}} - indices sampled for each echo/contrast\nencodingSize::NTuple{D,Int64}           - size of the underlying image matrix\nfov::NTuple{3,Float64}                  - field of view in mm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.AcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.AcquisitionData-Union{Tuple{Array{Complex{T}, 6}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"AcquisitionData(kspace::Array{Complex{T},6}; enc2D=false)\n\nReturns an AcquisitionData struct created from a zero-filled kspace with 6 dimensions :     [x,y,z or slices,channels,echoes,repetitions]\n\nDifferent undersampling patterns can be handled only along the echo dimension.\n\nThis method assumes the data to correspond to a single volume encoded with a 3d Cartesian sequence or in 2D multi-slice with keyword enc2D = true\n\nKeywords :\n\nenc2D=false: if true, the 3rd dimension is supposed to store the 2D slices\n\nNotes :\n\nThe current implementation is using the same subsampling mask for all NR and Slices (only echoes can be different)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.AcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.AcquisitionData-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{K, Array{Array{Complex{T}, 2}, 3}}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, K<:Union{Array{Trajectory{T}, 1}, Trajectory}}","page":"MRIBase","text":"AcquisitionData(tr::K,kdata::Array{Matrix{Complex{T}},3}; seqInfo=Dict{Symbol,Any}()\n                    , idx=nothing, encodingSize=ntuple(d->0, ndims(tr)), fov=(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)\n                    , kargs...) where {T <: AbstractFloat, K <: Union{Trajectory,Vector{Trajectory{T}}}}\n\nconstructor for AcquisitionData\n\nArguments\n\ntr - a Trajectory{T}, or a Vector{Trajectory{T}} with one trajectory per contrast\nkdata::Array{Matrix{Complex{T}},3} - k-space data, a matrix of samples × coils for each contrast, slice and repetition\n(seqInfo=Dict{Symbol,Any}()) - stored in the field sequenceInfo\n(idx=nothing) - sampled indices of each contrast (subsampleIndices), by default all nodes\n(encodingSize) - size of the underlying image matrix, by default zeros; pass it for a vector of trajectories, for which the default has only one entry\n(fov=(0.0, 0.0, 0.0)) - field of view in mm\n\nOther keyword arguments are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.AcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.changeEncodingSize2D-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{AcquisitionData, NTuple{D, Int64}}} where D","page":"MRIBase","text":"changeEncodingSize2D(acqData::AcquisitionData, newEncodingSize::NTuple{2,Int64})\n\nchanges the encoding size of 2d encoded acqData to newEncodingSize by rescaling the k-space positions and removing the samples outside of the new size; the field encodingSize keeps its old value. Returns a new AcquisitionData object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.changeEncodingSize2D"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.convert3dTo2d-Union{Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, 3}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"convert3dTo2d(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nconvert the 3d encoded AcquisitionData acqData to the equivalent 2d AcquisitionData.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.convert3dTo2d"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.convertUndersampledData-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"convertUndersampledData(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nconverts undersampled AcquisitionData, where only profiles contained in acqData.subsampleIndices are sampled, into a format where trajectories only contain the sampled profiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.convertUndersampledData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.encodingSize-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"encodingSize(acq::AcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the size of the image grid that acq encodes, which the reconstruction uses as the default image size (zeros if it was not given when acq was created)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.encodingSize"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.fieldOfView-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"fieldOfView(acq::AcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the field of view of acq in mm, a tuple with three entries that are zero for data without this information\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.fieldOfView"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.kData","page":"MRIBase","text":"kData(acqData::AcquisitionData, echo::Int64=1, coil::Int64=1, slice::Int64=1;rep::Int64=1)\n\nreturns the k-space contained in acqData for given echo, coil, slice and rep(etition).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.kData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.kDataCart-Union{Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIBase","text":"kDataCart(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nReturns the cartesian k-space contained in acqData for all echo, coil, slice and rep(etition) with dimension : [x,y,z*slices,channels,echoes,repetitions]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.kDataCart"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.multiCoilData-Tuple{AcquisitionData, Int64, Int64}","page":"MRIBase","text":"multiCoilData(acqData::AcquisitionData, echo::Int64, slice::Int64;rep::Int64=1)\n\nreturns the k-space contained in acqData for all coils and given echo, slice and rep(etition).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.multiCoilData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.multiCoilMultiEchoData-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T}, Int64}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"multiCoilMultiEchoData(acqData::AcquisitionData, slice::Int64; rep=1)\n\nreturns the k-space contained in acqData for all coils, echoes and given slice and rep(etition).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.multiCoilMultiEchoData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.multiEchoData-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T}, Int64, Int64}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"multiEchoData(acqData::AcquisitionData, coil::Int64, slice::Int64;rep::Int64=1)\n\nreturns the k-space contained in acqData for all echoes and given coil, slice and rep(etition).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.multiEchoData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numChannels-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numChannels(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the number of channels/coils in acqData\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numChannels"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numContrasts-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numContrasts(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the number of contrasts/echoes in acqData\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numContrasts"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numRepetitions-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numRepetitions(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the number of repetitions in acqData\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numRepetitions"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numSlices-Tuple{AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numSlices(acqData::AcquisitionData)\n\nreturns the number of slices in acqData\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numSlices"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.samplingDensity-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T}, Tuple}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"samplingDensity(acqData::AcquisitionData, shape::Tuple)\n\nreturns the square roots of the density compensation factors of the trajectories contained in acqData, one vector for each contrast, for an image of size shape. For Cartesian trajectories, they are constant, 1/sqrt(prod(shape)), one for each measured sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.samplingDensity"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.trajectory","page":"MRIBase","text":"trajectory(acqData::AcquisitionData,i::Int64=1)\n\nreturns the i-th trajectory contained in acqData.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.trajectory"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.Trajectory","page":"MRIBase","text":"struct describing a trajectory with element type T\n\nFields\n\nname::String                  - name of the trajectory\nnodes::Matrix{T}              - sampling locations in k-space.                                   (1.dim <-> dimensions of k-space, 2. dim <-> sampling points)\ntimes::Vector{T}              - sampling times in s\nTE::T                         - echo time in s\nAQ::T                         - readout duration in s (per profile)\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\nnumSlices::Int64              - number of slices (for 3d trajectories)\ncartesian::Bool               - true if sampling points lie on a cartesian grid\ncircular::Bool                - true if kspace is covered in a circular domain\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.Trajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.Trajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix{T}, Int64, Any}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"MRIBase","text":"Trajectory(nodes::AbstractMatrix{T}, numProfiles::Int64, numSamplingPerProfile;\n           times=nothing, TE=0.0, AQ=1.e-3, numSlices::Int64=1,\n           cartesian::Bool=false, circular::Bool=false) where T <: AbstractFloat\n\ncreates a trajectory named \"Custom\" from the k-space positions nodes, a Matrix{T} with one row per dimension and one column per sample, ordered profile by profile and, in a stacked 3d trajectory, partition by partition. Without times, the samples of each profile are spread over the readout duration AQ, starting at TE.\n\nArguments\n\nnodes::Matrix{T}          - k-space positions between -0.5 and 0.5 (other matrix types, such as a transpose,                               have to be converted with Matrix first)\nnumProfiles::Int64        - number of profiles per partition\nnumSamplingPerProfile     - number of sampling points per profile\n(times=nothing)           - sampling times in s, a Vector{T} with one time per node\n(TE=0.0)                  - echo time in s\n(AQ=1.e-3)                - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(numSlices::Int64=1)      - number of partitions (slices) of a stacked 3d trajectory\n(cartesian::Bool=false)   - true if the nodes lie on the cartesian grid in the order of CartesianTrajectory                               (CartesianTrajectory3D in 3d): without a correction map, the reconstruction and                               the default simulation then use FFTs, which ignore the nodes\n(circular::Bool=false)    - true if the nodes cover a circular region of k-space, as in radial and spiral                               trajectories: the reconstructions \"direct\", \"standard\" and \"multiCoil\" then set                               the image outside a circle (a sphere in 3d) whose diameter is the largest side of the                               image to zero, and with a correction map, the encoding operator ignores the image                               outside it\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.Trajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.acqTimePerProfile-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"acqTimePerProfile(tr::Trajectory) returns the acquisition time per profile of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.acqTimePerProfile"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.echoTime-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"echoTime(tr::Trajectory) returns the echo time of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.echoTime"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.isCartesian-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"isCartesian(tr::Trajectory) returns whether the trajectory nodes lie on a cartesian grid\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.isCartesian"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.isCircular-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"isCircular(tr::Trajectory) returns whether the trajectory has circular k-space coverage \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.isCircular"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.kspaceNodes-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"kspaceNodes(tr::Trajectory) returns the kspace sampling points of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.kspaceNodes"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numProfiles-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numProfiles(tr::Trajectory) returns the number of profiles of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numProfiles"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numSamplingPerProfile-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numSamplingPerProfile(tr::Trajectory) returns the number of samples per profile of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numSamplingPerProfile"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.numSlices-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"numSlices(tr::Trajectory) returns the number of slices of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.numSlices"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.readoutTimes-Tuple{Trajectory}","page":"MRIBase","text":"readoutTimes(tr::Trajectory) returns the readoutTimes for the sampling points of a trajectory \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.readoutTimes"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.scale-Tuple{Trajectory, Real}","page":"MRIBase","text":"scale(tr::Trajectory, factor::Real)\n\nreturns a copy of tr with the nodes divided by factor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.scale"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.trajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, AbstractString, Int64, Int64}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"trajectory(::Type{T}, trajName::AbstractString, numProfiles::Int, numSamplingPerProfile::Int;\n           numSlices::Int64=1, TE::AbstractFloat=0.0, AQ::AbstractFloat=1.e-3, kargs...) where T\n\nis a factory method to construct a trajectory with element type T, for example Float64, from its name trajName\n\nArguments\n\nT                             - element type of the nodes and times\ntrajName::AbstractString      - name of the trajectory\nnumProfiles::Int              - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int    - number of sampling points per profile\n(numSlices::Int64=1)          - number of slices (for 3d trajectories)\n(TE::AbstractFloat=0.0)       - echo time in s\n(AQ::AbstractFloat=1.e-3)     - readout duration in s (per profile)\nkargs...                      - additional keyword arguments, passed on to the constructor of the trajectory\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.trajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.CartesianTrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"CartesianTrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::T=0.0\n              , AQ::T=1.e-3\n              , kmin=(-0.5,-0.5)\n              , kmax=(0.5,0.5)\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d cartesian trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(kmin=(-0.5,-0.5))            - minimum values of the covered k-space (for partial Fourier imaging)\n(kmax=(0.5,0.5))              - maximum values of the covered k-space (for partial Fourier imaging)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.CartesianTrajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.EPITrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"EPITrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::T=0.0\n              , AQ::T=1.e-3\n              , EPI_factor::Int64=1\n              , profileOffset= :equispaced\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d cartesian trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::T=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::T=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(EPI_factor::Int64=1)         - EPI factor, e.g. how many profiles to acquire per shot\n(profileOffset= :equispaced)  - equispaced or random ordering of the shots\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.EPITrajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.RadialTrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"RadialTrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::T=0.0\n              , AQ::T=1.e-3\n              , angleOffset= :equispaced\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d radial trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::T=0.0)                   - echo time in s\n(AQ::T=1.e-3)                 - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(angleOffset= :equispaced)    - spacing of profile angles (:equispaced sampling, :golden angle sampling or :random sampling)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.RadialTrajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.SpiralTrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"SpiralTrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::Float64=0.0\n              , AQ::Float64=1.e-3\n              , windings::Real= 6.25\n              , kmax::Real=0.5\n              , angleOffset::String=\"equispaced\"\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d spiral trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(windings::Real= 6.25)        - number of windings of the spiral profiles\n(kmax::Real=0.5)              - distance of the last sample of each profile from the center of k-space\n(angleOffset::String=\"equispaced\") - spacing of profile angles (\"equispaced\" sampling, \"golden\" angle sampling or \"random\" sampling)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.SpiralTrajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.spiralDensity-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Int64, Int64}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"Calculating the density using VoronoiCells\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.spiralDensity"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.SpiralTrajectoryVarDens-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"SpiralTrajectoryVarDens(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::Float64=0.0\n              , AQ::Float64=1.e-3\n              , windings::Real= 6.25\n              , alpha=2.0\n              , kmax::Real=0.5\n              , angleOffset::String=\"equispaced\"\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d spiral trajectory with variable density\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(windings::Real= 6.25)        - number of windings of the spiral profiles\n(alpha=2.0)                   - exponent describing the evolution of the magnitude of the sampling points along the profiles\n(kmax::Real=0.5)              - distance of the last sample of each profile from the center of k-space\n(angleOffset::String=\"equispaced\") - spacing of profile angles (\"equispaced\" sampling, \"golden\" angle sampling or \"random\" sampling)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.SpiralTrajectoryVarDens"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.SpiralPerturbedTrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"SpiralPerturbedTrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::Float64=0.0\n              , AQ::Float64=1.e-3\n              , windings::Real= 6.25\n              , alpha::Real=2.0\n              , kmax::Real=0.5\n              , angleOffset::String=\"equispaced\"\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d spiral trajectory with variable density, whose radius at the k-th sample of each profile is that of SpiralTrajectoryVarDens with the same arguments times (1 + 0.025 cos(0.1 k)) (kmax - 0.025) / kmax. Each coordinate is limited to the interval [-0.5, 0.5), which changes the nodes only for kmax above about 0.51.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(windings::Real= 6.25)        - number of windings of the spiral profiles\n(alpha::Real=2.0)             - exponent describing the evolution of the magnitude of the sampling points                                   along the profiles\n(kmax::Real=0.5)              - the sampling points lie within this distance from the center of k-space\n(angleOffset::String=\"equispaced\") - spacing of profile angles (\"equispaced\" sampling, \"golden\" angle                                   sampling or \"random\" sampling)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.SpiralPerturbedTrajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.CartesianTrajectory3D-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"CartesianTrajectory3D(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::Float64=0.0\n              , AQ::Float64=1.e-3\n              , numSlices=1\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 3d cartesian trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(numSlices=1)                 - number of slices\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.CartesianTrajectory3D"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.KooshballTrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"KooshballTrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::Float64=0.0\n              , AQ::Float64=1.e-3\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 3d kooshball trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.KooshballTrajectory"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIBase/#MRIBase.StackOfStarsTrajectory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"MRIBase","text":"StackOfStarsTrajectory(T, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile\n              ; TE::Float64=0.0\n              , AQ::Float64=1.e-3\n              , numSlices=1\n              , angleOffset= :equispaced\n              , kargs...)\n\nreturns a 2d radial trajectory.\n\nArguments\n\nnumProfiles::Int64            - number of profiles\nnumSamplingPerProfile::Int64  - number of sampling points per profile\n(TE::Float64=0.0)             - echo time in s\n(AQ::Float64=1.e-3)           - readout duration in s (per profile)\n(numSlices=1)                 - number of slices\n(angleOffset= :equispaced)    - spacing of profile angles (:equispaced sampling, :golden angle sampling or :random sampling)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.StackOfStarsTrajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#undersample-kspace","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"Reconstruction methods for undersampled data are often tested on fully sampled data from which samples are removed afterwards. MRISampling does this for Cartesian data with sample_kspace. This guide shows how to choose a sampling pattern, keep the center of k-space, make the pattern reproducible and undersample 3D data and data with several contrasts. Data Model explains how AcquisitionData stores the sampling pattern.","title":"Undersample k-Space"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#Choosing-a-Pattern","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"sample_kspace takes the data, the reduction factor and the name of a pattern and returns the undersampled data as a new object. The example keeps a quarter of the knee data from Direct Reconstruction:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nacqData = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")))\n\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", seed = 1234)\nsize(acqData.kdata[1]), size(acqDataSub.kdata[1])\n((102400, 8), (25600, 8))\n\nBy default, the pattern keeps complete profiles, here 80 of 320, as an accelerated acquisition would. The number of kept profiles is the number of profiles divided by the reduction factor, rounded down. Pass the factor as a floating-point number, such as 4.0, because the random patterns do not accept an integer. There are four patterns:\n\n\"regular\" keeps every n-th profile, as in a conventional parallel imaging acquisition. Use a whole number as the factor, because other factors leave the end of k-space unsampled.\n\"random\" chooses the profiles at random.\n\"poisson\" chooses the profiles at random but keeps a minimum distance between them as long as there is room left, which is known as Poisson-disk sampling.\n\"vdPoisson\" is a Poisson-disk pattern with variable density, which keeps more profiles near the center of k-space.\n\nThe docstring also lists \"lines\", the pattern that sample_kspace uses internally to keep complete profiles. The following figure shows the four patterns for the knee data:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure()\nfor (i, name) in enumerate([\"regular\", \"random\", \"poisson\", \"vdPoisson\"])\n    data = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, name, seed = 1234)\n    mask = zeros(320, 320)\n    mask[data.subsampleIndices[1]] .= 1\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = name, width = 200, height = 200,\n              xlabel = \"Sample in the readout\", ylabel = \"Phase encoding step\")\n    heatmap!(ax, mask, colormap = :grays)\nend\nresize_to_layout!(fig)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Choosing a Pattern"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#Keeping-the-Center-of-k-Space","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"Some methods need a fully sampled region in the center of k-space. In Multi-Coil Reconstruction, for example, ESPIRiT estimates the coil sensitivities from this region. For \"random\" and \"poisson\", the keyword calsize sets the number of central profiles that are always kept. The other profiles follow the pattern, and the reduction factor stays the same:\n\nacqDataCal = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30, seed = 1234)\nprofiles = unique(fld1.(acqDataCal.subsampleIndices[1], 320))\nlength(profiles), all(in(profiles), 146:175)\n(80, true)\n\ncalsize must not exceed the number of kept profiles. \"regular\" and \"vdPoisson\" ignore the keyword.","title":"Keeping the Center of k-Space"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#Reproducible-Patterns","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"The random patterns differ from call to call. The keyword seed fixes them, so that a result can be reproduced:\n\na = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", seed = 1234)\nb = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", seed = 1234)\na.subsampleIndices == b.subsampleIndices\ntrue\n\nseed also resets Julia's default random number generator, so random numbers that are drawn afterwards repeat as well. To avoid this, pass a generator of your own as rng, which seed then seeds instead.","title":"Reproducible Patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#3D-Data-and-Single-Samples","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"For 3D data, sample_kspace chooses complete readouts in the plane of the two phase encoding directions, so the patterns are two-dimensional. \"regular\" keeps every n-th point of this plane, counting along the first phase encoding direction. sample returns such a pattern without applying it to data, as a vector with the indices of the kept points of a grid:\n\nidx = sample((64, 64), 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 12, seed = 1234)\nlength(idx)\n1024\n\nIn two dimensions, calsize keeps a central square, here of 12 × 12 points, which must not contain more points than the pattern keeps. The \"vdPoisson\" pattern becomes slow for large grids. The following figure shows both Poisson-disk patterns:\n\nfig = Figure()\nfor (i, (name, options)) in enumerate([\"poisson\" => (calsize = 12,), \"vdPoisson\" => (;)])\n    mask = zeros(64, 64)\n    mask[sample((64, 64), 4.0, name; seed = 1234, options...)] .= 1\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = name, width = 250, height = 250,\n              xlabel = \"Phase encoding step 1\", ylabel = \"Phase encoding step 2\")\n    heatmap!(ax, mask, colormap = :grays)\nend\nresize_to_layout!(fig)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWith profiles = false, sample_kspace uses such a two-dimensional pattern for 2D data as well and removes single samples instead of complete profiles, which treats a slice like the phase encoding plane of a 3D acquisition. The random patterns support this option only for 2D data.","title":"3D Data and Single Samples"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#Several-Contrasts","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"For data with several contrasts, each contrast gets its own random pattern by default, and rand = false uses one pattern for all contrasts. Multi-Echo Reconstruction shows how this choice affects a joint reconstruction of the contrasts.","title":"Several Contrasts"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/undersample/#Other-Kinds-of-Data","page":"Undersample k-Space","text":"sample_kspace expects fully sampled Cartesian data whose samples are stored in the order of the k-space grid. For fully sampled data, this is the case if the indices of all contrasts are sorted, as for the knee data:\n\nall(issorted, acqData.subsampleIndices)\ntrue\n\nData that is loaded from a file can be stored in another order. sample_kspace then assigns the kept samples to wrong k-space positions without an error (#352). Bring such data into the order of the grid first with AcquisitionData(kDataCart(acqData)), and add enc2D = true for data with several slices. This resets the field of view and the sampling times, and it treats samples that are exactly zero in the first coil of the first slice and repetition as not measured.\nData that is already undersampled cannot be undersampled again with sample_kspace.\nFor non-Cartesian data, sample_kspace removes complete profiles from the k-space data but keeps all nodes of the trajectory (#353). Pass the result to convertUndersampledData, which reduces the trajectory to the nodes of the kept samples: convertUndersampledData(sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"random\")).\nTo apply a pattern of your own, set the samples that are not measured to zero in the k-space array, as shown in Extract Cartesian k-Space.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Other Kinds of Data"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles","page":"MRIFiles","text":"MRIFiles handles raw data files, for example ISMRMRD files (ISMRMRDFile) and Bruker datasets (BrukerFile). MRIFiles depends on MRIBase. It has to be loaded with using MRIFiles, because MRIReco does not depend on it. The guide Load and Save Raw Data shows how to use these file types.","title":"MRIFiles"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIFiles/#ISMRMRD-Files","page":"MRIFiles","text":"","title":"ISMRMRD Files"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIFiles/#Bruker-Datasets","page":"MRIFiles","text":"","title":"Bruker Datasets"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIFiles/#Other-Formats","page":"MRIFiles","text":"","title":"Other Formats"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.ISMRMRDFile","page":"MRIFiles","text":"ISMRMRDFile(filename::String)\n\nreference to the file filename in the ISMRMRD format, an HDF5 file with an XML header and the acquired profiles. Creating an ISMRMRDFile does not open the file. RawAcquisitionData and AcquisitionData read the file, and MRIFiles.save writes it.\n\nExamples\n\nf = ISMRMRDFile(\"scan.h5\")\nraw = RawAcquisitionData(f)\nMRIFiles.save(ISMRMRDFile(\"copy.h5\"), raw)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.ISMRMRDFile"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData-Tuple{ISMRMRDFile, Any}","page":"MRIFiles","text":"RawAcquisitionData(f::ISMRMRDFile, dataset=\"dataset\"; slice=nothing, repetition=nothing, contrast=nothing)\n\nreads the ISMRMRDFile f and stores the result in a RawAcquisitionData object. With slice, repetition or contrast, only the profiles whose encoding counter has the given value, or one of the given values, are read. The counters start at 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIBase.AcquisitionData-Tuple{ISMRMRDFile, Any}","page":"MRIFiles","text":"AcquisitionData(f::ISMRMRDFile, dataset=\"dataset\")\n\nreads the ISMRMRDFile f and converts the result into an AcquisitionData object, as AcquisitionData(RawAcquisitionData(f, dataset)) does. All profiles are read, also noise measurements, which can then take the place of image profiles in the k-space data, see Select and Prepare Profiles. To select or remove profiles before the conversion, for example with remove_raw_by_flags, or to pass keyword arguments to the conversion, read the file with RawAcquisitionData first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.AcquisitionData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIFiles/#FileIO.save-Tuple{ISMRMRDFile, RawAcquisitionData}","page":"MRIFiles","text":"save(f::ISMRMRDFile, acq::RawAcquisitionData, dataset=\"dataset\")\n\nwrites the raw data acq to the ISMRMRD file f: the header parameters acq.params as the XML header and the profiles as the acquisitions of the HDF5 group dataset. An existing file is replaced as a whole, together with its other groups. Header parameters that MRIFiles does not know are left out, and elements that the ISMRMRD schema requires, such as reconSpace and encodingLimits, are written only if acq.params has their values, which raw data from a Bruker dataset doesn't. MRIFiles does not export save, a function of FileIO, so call it as MRIFiles.save. To save AcquisitionData, convert it with RawAcquisitionData first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FileIO.save"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.BrukerFile","page":"MRIFiles","text":"BrukerFile(path::String; maxEntriesAcqp=2000)\n\nreference to a Bruker ParaVision dataset, the folder path of one scan. The folder contains the parameter files acqp and method, the raw data (fid for ParaVision 6, rawdata.job0 for ParaVision 360) and the reconstructions of the scanner in the subfolder pdata. Creating a BrukerFile reads nothing.\n\nb[name] returns the parameter name as JcampdxFile reads it, for example a string or an array of strings, or an empty string if the parameter is not found. If the file of the parameter was not read yet, it is read first: acqp for names starting with ACQ or GO and for NA, NR, NI, NSLICES and SW, the file visu_pars of the first reconstruction for names starting with Visu, mpi.par for names starting with CONFIG, and method for other names. Other parameters of acqp, such as SW_h, are therefore found only after acqp was read, and other names with fewer than three characters, such as RG, throw a BoundsError until then. Only the first maxEntriesAcqp - 1 parameters of acqp are read.\n\nb[name, procno] returns a parameter of the reconstruction pdata/procno, read from its file visu_pars for names starting with Visu, from reco for names starting with reco in any case and from methreco for names starting with PVM. The parameters of all reconstructions read so far are kept together: a parameter that pdata/procno lacks, or a procno without visu_pars, returns the value of a reconstruction read before, and only a parameter that none of them has throws a KeyError. reco, of which only the first 12 parameters are read, and methreco are read only for the first procno asked for.\n\nRead the raw data with RawAcquisitionData and the images of the scanner with recoData.\n\nExamples\n\nb = BrukerFile(\"study/12\")\nb[\"PVM_Matrix\"]       # matrix size, for example [\"128\", \"128\"]\nraw = RawAcquisitionData(b)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.BrukerFile"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData-Tuple{BrukerFile}","page":"MRIFiles","text":"RawAcquisitionData(b::BrukerFile)\n\nreads the raw data of the Bruker dataset b into a RawAcquisitionData object. Data from ParaVision 360 is read with RawAcquisitionDataFid_360. From other versions, such as ParaVision 6, scans whose ACQ_protocol_name is UTE3D are read with RawAcquisitionData_3DUTE and other scans as Cartesian data from the file fid. Scans whose ACQ_protocol_name is SPIRAL are not supported: for them, the function returns the contents of rawdata.job0 as an array. The header parameters, such as the matrix size and the field of view, come from the parameter files, also from visu_pars, so the subfolder pdata has to exist. For Cartesian data of ParaVision 6, the encoded size is the acquired size from ACQ_size, which can be smaller than the image size PVM_Matrix of the scanner's reconstruction. Each profile holds one readout of all receive channels.\n\nAcquisitionData does not accept a BrukerFile; convert the result with AcquisitionData(RawAcquisitionData(b)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIBase.RawAcquisitionData"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.RawAcquisitionDataFid_360","page":"MRIFiles","text":"RawAcquisitionDataFid_360(b::BrukerFile)\n\nreads Cartesian raw data of ParaVision 360 from the file rawdata.job0 of the Bruker dataset b into a RawAcquisitionData object. The matrix size is PVM_Matrix times PVM_AntiAlias, and the k-space position of each profile is taken from PVM_EncGenSteps1 and PVM_EncGenSteps2, so scans with undersampled phase encoding are read as well. RawAcquisitionData(b) calls this function for all ParaVision 360 datasets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.RawAcquisitionDataFid_360"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.RawAcquisitionData_3DUTE","page":"MRIFiles","text":"RawAcquisitionData_3DUTE(b::BrukerFile)\n\nreads a 3D UTE scan of ParaVision 6 from the files fid and traj of the Bruker dataset b into a RawAcquisitionData object with the trajectory \"custom\". Each profile holds one center-out radial spoke together with its k-space positions. RawAcquisitionData(b) calls this function for scans whose ACQ_protocol_name is UTE3D; call it directly for 3D UTE scans of ParaVision 6 with another protocol name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.RawAcquisitionData_3DUTE"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.recoData","page":"MRIFiles","text":"recoData(f::BrukerFile, procno::Int=1)\n\nreads the images that the scanner reconstructed from the file pdata/procno/2dseq of the Bruker dataset f and returns them as an Array{Float32} of size (VisuCoreSize..., VisuCoreFrameCount). The values are returned as stored, without the scaling of each frame by f[\"VisuCoreDataSlope\", procno] and f[\"VisuCoreDataOffs\", procno] (value = stored value × slope + offset). The frames are in the order of the file, with the first frame group of VisuFGOrderDesc varying fastest: for a complex reconstruction with the frame groups FG_COIL and FG_COMPLEX, all real frames come first. VisuCoreDiskSliceOrder is not applied, so the slices can be in reverse order, and the dimensions follow the layout of 2dseq, which can differ from the order of PVM_Matrix. If the file does not exist, recoData logs an error and returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.recoData"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.JcampdxFile","page":"MRIFiles","text":"JcampdxFile()\n\nempty set of parameters from files in the JCAMP-DX format, which Bruker ParaVision uses for its parameter files, for example acqp, method and visu_pars. read(j, filename) adds the parameters of a file to j and returns j, and read(j, filename, keylist) adds only the parameters named in keylist. Reading a file stops before the parameter VisuAcqFrameTime, so it and the parameters after it are missing.\n\nj[name], haskey(j, name) and get(j, name, default) work as for a dictionary. A value is a string for a single value and an array of strings for an array, whose dimensions are those of the file in reverse order; for an array of tuples, each entry is a vector of the values of a tuple, with numbers as Float64. A string in angle brackets on the line of its name keeps the brackets, for example \"<Bruker:RARE>\", while an array of strings in angle brackets is read as its first string only, without the brackets. BrukerFile reads its parameter files with JcampdxFile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.JcampdxFile"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.CFLFile","page":"MRIFiles","text":"CFLFile(filenameBase::String)\n\nreference to an array stored in the format of the BART toolbox: the file filenameBase.hdr holds the dimensions and filenameBase.cfl the complex values in single precision. MRIFiles.load(CFLFile(filenameBase)) reads the array and returns an Array{ComplexF64} with all dimensions listed in the header. MRIFiles does not write this format.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.CFLFile"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.DFFile","page":"MRIFiles","text":"DFFile(datafilename::String, trajfilename::String)\n\nreference to 2D non-Cartesian data stored in two binary files, trajfilename with the k-space nodes and datafilename with the samples. Both files start with four Int32 values. In the trajectory file, the second and third are the number of samples per profile and the number of profiles, followed by the nodes as Float32 values (2 × samples × profiles). In the data file, the second to fourth are the numbers of samples per profile, profiles and slices, followed by the samples as ComplexF32 values.\n\ntrajectory(f) returns the trajectory, and keyword arguments are passed on to Trajectory. MRIFiles.rawdata(f) returns the samples as an Array{ComplexF64,3}, and MRIFiles.rawdata(f, slice) those of one slice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.DFFile"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIFiles/#MRIFiles.readRawSiemens","page":"MRIFiles","text":"readRawSiemens(filename)\n\nunfinished reader of Siemens raw data files: it reads only the first eight bytes of the file and no data and returns nothing, and it throws an error for files of the older VB format. Convert Siemens raw data with siemens_to_ismrmrd and read the result with ISMRMRDFile instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIFiles.readRawSiemens"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/load_save/#load-save","page":"Load and Save Raw Data","text":"MRIFiles reads raw data from ISMRMRD files and from Bruker datasets, and it writes ISMRMRD files. This guide shows how to load and save ISMRMRD files, how to save acquisition data, and how to load a Bruker dataset and convert it into an ISMRMRD file. Data Model explains the two data types that appear here, RawAcquisitionData and AcquisitionData.","title":"Load and Save Raw Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/load_save/#Loading-an-ISMRMRD-File","page":"Load and Save Raw Data","text":"An ISMRMRDFile refers to a file by its name, and AcquisitionData reads it into the form that the reconstruction needs. The example loads the knee data from Direct Reconstruction:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfile = ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\"))\nacqData = AcquisitionData(file)\n\nAcquisitionData(file) reads the file into a RawAcquisitionData object, which holds the header and the profiles as they are stored in the file, and then converts this object. To look at the raw data or to change it before the conversion, do the two steps separately:\n\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(file)\nacqData = AcquisitionData(rawData)\nlength(rawData.profiles), size(acqData.kdata[1])\n(320, (102400, 8))\n\nThe file contains 320 profiles, and the converted data has 102400 samples from 8 coils. Bin Profiles into Contrasts is an example of changing raw data before the conversion.\n\nFiles from a scanner can also contain readouts that are not part of the image, such as noise measurements. The conversion does not skip them, so remove them first with remove_raw_by_flags, for example rawData = remove_raw_by_flags(rawData). Data Model describes what the conversion does and what it expects.\n\nAn ISMRMRD file stores the data of an acquisition in an HDF5 group, and MRIFiles reads the group dataset by default. If the group of a file has another name, loading fails with a KeyError, and the name has to be passed as the second argument, for example AcquisitionData(file, \"scan1\").\n\nRaw data from Siemens, GE and Philips scanners can be converted into ISMRMRD files with the converters of the ISMRMRD project: siemens_to_ismrmrd, ge_to_ismrmrd and philips_to_ismrmrd.","title":"Loading an ISMRMRD File"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/load_save/#Saving-Raw-Data","page":"Load and Save Raw Data","text":"save writes a RawAcquisitionData object to an ISMRMRD file:\n\noutfile = joinpath(mktempdir(), \"knee.h5\")\nMRIFiles.save(ISMRMRDFile(outfile), rawData)\n\nThe file then contains the header parameters and the profiles of rawData. An existing file is overwritten, and the name of the group can be passed as a third argument. MRIFiles does not export save (#315), so it is called with the module name.","title":"Saving Raw Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/load_save/#Saving-Acquisition-Data","page":"Load and Save Raw Data","text":"save accepts only raw data. Convert AcquisitionData, for example from a simulation, with RawAcquisitionData first. For non-Cartesian data that was loaded from a file, this conversion can fail (#346). Here, the knee data is undersampled with MRISampling and saved:\n\nusing MRISampling\n\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30, seed = 1234)\nsubfile = joinpath(mktempdir(), \"knee_undersampled.h5\")\nMRIFiles.save(ISMRMRDFile(subfile), RawAcquisitionData(acqDataSub))\n\nFor this Cartesian data, loading the file returns the same k-space data and sampling pattern:\n\nacqDataLoaded = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(subfile))\nacqDataLoaded.kdata == acqDataSub.kdata,\n    acqDataLoaded.subsampleIndices == acqDataSub.subsampleIndices\n(true, true)\n\nA file written from acquisition data does not keep everything:\n\nThe samples and the k-space positions are stored in single precision, so double-precision data, for example from simulation, is loaded in single precision. The field of view is stored with the precision of the data.\nOnly \"Cartesian\" and \"Cartesian3D\" trajectories are loaded as Cartesian trajectories again. Any other trajectory is loaded as a non-Cartesian trajectory with the name \"Custom\" and the same nodes. This includes \"EPI\" trajectories, which MRIReco otherwise treats as Cartesian, so their reconstruction changes. Data whose trajectory already had the name \"Custom\" is loaded with additional profiles of zeros if it has several slices or repetitions (#331).\nThe loaded \"Custom\" trajectory is always marked as circular. If the original trajectory was not, reconstructions such as the direct one now set the corners of the image to zero. The subsampleIndices of the loaded data can differ as well, which is one reason why converting such data into raw data again can fail.\nAfter loading, the echo time is zero, sequenceInfo is empty and the readout duration returned by acqTimePerProfile is the default of 1 ms. The sampling times of a non-Cartesian trajectory are rebuilt from the original readout duration, those of a Cartesian trajectory from the default. This matters if the reconstruction uses these times, for example for an off-resonance correction.\nThe header has only five entries, among them the matrix size and the field of view. Elements that the ISMRMRD schema requires, such as reconSpace and encodingLimits, are missing, so other ISMRMRD software may not accept the file (#33).","title":"Saving Acquisition Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/load_save/#Loading-a-Bruker-Dataset","page":"Load and Save Raw Data","text":"A Bruker dataset is the folder of one scan, which ParaVision names by its number, inside the folder of the study. MRIFiles reads several files of this folder, among them acqp, method, the raw data (fid for ParaVision 6, rawdata.job0 for ParaVision 360) and the file visu_pars of a reconstruction in the subfolder pdata, so copy the folder as a whole. A BrukerFile is created from the path of this folder. The data is loaded in two steps, because AcquisitionData does not accept a BrukerFile. The example loads a Cartesian dataset that is part of the documentation data:\n\nfile = BrukerFile(joinpath(datadir, \"brukerfileCart\"))\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(file)\nacqData = AcquisitionData(rawData)\nencodingSize(acqData), numSlices(acqData)\n((96, 96), 15)\n\nThe data can then be reconstructed as in Direct Reconstruction. Leave out params[:reconSize], which the tutorial sets to the size of the knee data. The image then has the matrix size of the data, here 96 × 96 for each of the 15 slices.\n\nMRIFiles reads Cartesian acquisitions from ParaVision 6 and ParaVision 360. The reader is tested with FLASH and RARE data from ParaVision 6 and with FLASH data from ParaVision 360. MRIFiles also reads 3D UTE acquisitions from ParaVision 6. It recognizes them by the protocol name UTE3D. For a UTE scan with another protocol name, call RawAcquisitionData_3DUTE(file) instead of RawAcquisitionData(file). For a UTE scan with several repetitions, the conversion into AcquisitionData currently adds profiles of zeros to each repetition and the same number of profiles at the center of k-space to the trajectory (#331).\n\nIf the field of view was shifted along a phase encoding direction when the scan was planned, the image reconstructed with MRIReco is shifted relative to the image from the scanner. For Cartesian data, AcquisitionData(rawData, OffsetBruker = true) corrects this shift.\n\nrecoData(file) returns the images that the scanner reconstructed (pdata/1/2dseq), and a second argument selects another reconstruction by its number in pdata. The matrix size and orientation of these images can differ from those of an image reconstructed with MRIReco. Parameters of the method file can be read by indexing the BrukerFile with their name. file[\"PVM_Fov\"], for example, returns the field of view as a vector of strings, and indexing with a name that does not exist returns an empty string.","title":"Loading a Bruker Dataset"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/load_save/#Converting-a-Bruker-Dataset-into-an-ISMRMRD-File","page":"Load and Save Raw Data","text":"Saving the raw data of a Bruker dataset with save converts the dataset into an ISMRMRD file, which can be read like any other:\n\nbrukerOutfile = joinpath(mktempdir(), \"brukerfileCart.h5\")\nMRIFiles.save(ISMRMRDFile(brukerOutfile), rawData)\nlength(RawAcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(brukerOutfile)).profiles)\n1440\n\nAs with a file written from acquisition data, the header of this file lacks elements that the ISMRMRD schema requires (#33). MRIFiles does not write Bruker datasets.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Converting a Bruker Dataset into an ISMRMRD File"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#iterative-reconstruction","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"Acquiring fewer k-space samples shortens the scan, but a direct reconstruction of undersampled data shows artifacts. An iterative reconstruction instead searches for the image that agrees best with the measured samples, and it can use prior knowledge about the image. This tutorial undersamples single-coil data and compares the direct reconstruction with the iterative reconstruction type \"standard\".\n\nBesides MRIReco, the tutorial needs MRIFiles to read the data, MRISampling to undersample it and CairoMakie for the plots. We use the same plotting function as in Direct Reconstruction.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling, CairoMakie\n\nfunction showimages(images, titles; columns = length(images))\n    fig = Figure(size = (300 * columns, 310 * cld(length(images), columns)))\n    for (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip(images, titles))\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(i, columns)...], title = title,\n                  aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n        heatmap!(ax, image', colormap = :grays)\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n    end\n    return fig\nend","title":"Iterative Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#Loading-the-Data","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"The data is a simulated single-coil dataset of a brain image with 256 × 256 pixels, fully sampled on a Cartesian grid. The file belongs to the documentation data, which is downloaded as in Direct Reconstruction. The direct reconstruction of the fully sampled data serves as the reference. Of the six dimensions of the reconstructed image, we keep x and y and take the magnitude:\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfilename = joinpath(datadir, \"acqDataBrainSim100.h5\")\nacqData = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(filename))\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (256, 256)\nreferenceImage = abs.(reconstruction(acqData, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])","title":"Loading the Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#Undersampling","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"To simulate an acquisition that is three times faster, sample_kspace keeps about a third of the profiles. They are chosen by variable-density Poisson-disk sampling, which samples the center of k-space more densely than the outer parts, and the seed makes this random choice reproducible. The result is a new AcquisitionData object that stores the indices of the kept samples in subsampleIndices:\n\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 3.0, \"vdPoisson\", seed = 1234)\n\nsamplingMask = zeros(256, 256)\nsamplingMask[acqDataSub.subsampleIndices[1]] .= 1\n\nThe direct reconstruction treats the missing samples as zeros, which causes aliasing artifacts. We measure the deviation from the reference with nrmsd, the normalized root mean squared deviation:\n\ndirectImage = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\ndeviation(image) = round(nrmsd(referenceImage, image), sigdigits = 2)\nshowimages([referenceImage, samplingMask, directImage],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"Sampling mask\",\n            \"Direct, NRMSD $(deviation(directImage))\"])\nFigure()","title":"Undersampling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#Iterative-Reconstruction","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction type \"standard\" solves the regularized least-squares problem\n\nundersetmathbfxoperatornameargmin \nfrac12lVert mathbfEmathbfx - mathbfb rVert_2^2 + mathrmR(mathbfx)\n\nwhere mathbfb contains the measured samples, the encoding operator mathbfE maps an image mathbfx to these samples, here by a Fourier transform followed by the undersampling, and mathrmR is a regularization term. The solvers and regularization terms come from RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, which using MRIReco loads.\n\nWe first use CGNR, the conjugate gradient method applied to the normal equations, with a small ell_2 regularization, which penalizes lVert mathbfx rVert_2^2 with a weight set by λ:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"standard\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (256, 256)\nparams[:solver] = CGNR\nparams[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nparams[:iterations] = 20\nimageL2 = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nshowimages([referenceImage, directImage, imageL2],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"Direct, NRMSD $(deviation(directImage))\",\n            \"CGNR and ℓ2, NRMSD $(deviation(imageL2))\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe result is practically the same as the direct reconstruction. The data term only involves the measured samples, and the ell_2 term prefers small values, so the missing samples stay zero. To fill them in, the regularization has to describe the image better.","title":"Iterative Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#Total-Variation","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"Total variation (TV) regularization, which penalizes operatornameTV(mathbfx) with a weight set by λ, favors images that consist of regions with constant intensity, like this simulated brain. We solve the problem with ADMM, which is also the default solver. So that ADMM converges within 50 iterations for every λ used below, we let it adapt its penalty parameter rho with vary_rho = :balance and use more inner iterations for the TV term than by default, iterationsTV = 50. With MeasurementBasedNormalization, λ is scaled by the mean magnitude of the measured data, so that the same value works for data of different scale:\n\nparams[:solver] = ADMM\nparams[:vary_rho] = :balance\nparams[:reg] = TVRegularization(0.03, shape = (256, 256), iterationsTV = 50)\nparams[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparams[:iterations] = 50\nimageTV = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nshowimages([referenceImage, directImage, imageTV],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"Direct, NRMSD $(deviation(directImage))\",\n            \"TV, NRMSD $(deviation(imageTV))\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe TV reconstruction removes most of the artifacts.","title":"Total Variation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#Choosing-λ","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"The weight λ balances the agreement with the measured samples against the regularization. The larger it is, the smoother the image becomes, and if it is too large, details are lost:\n\nlambdas = [0.003, 0.03, 0.3]\nimages = map(lambdas) do λ\n    params[:reg] = TVRegularization(λ, shape = (256, 256), iterationsTV = 50)\n    abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nend\nshowimages(images, [\"λ = $λ, NRMSD $(deviation(image))\"\n                    for (λ, image) in zip(lambdas, images)])\nFigure()\n\nBecause the simulated data contains no noise, even λ = 0.003 gives an image similar to λ = 0.03, while λ = 0.3 loses details. Measured data contains noise, and a small λ leaves more of it in the image. The best value depends on the data, its noise and the undersampling, so it is usually found by comparing several reconstructions.","title":"Choosing λ"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/iterative/#Next-Steps","page":"Iterative Reconstruction","text":"The RegularizedLeastSquares.jl documentation describes the available solvers and regularization terms.\nWith several coils, \"standard\" reconstructs the image of each coil separately. Multi-Coil Reconstruction shows how the reconstruction type \"multiCoil\" uses the coil sensitivities to reconstruct a single image from all coils.\nWith several echoes, \"standard\" reconstructs each echo on its own. Multi-Echo Reconstruction shows how the reconstruction type \"multiEcho\" reconstructs them jointly.\nThe API reference describes reconstruction and sample_kspace.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#multicoil-reconstruction","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"Each receive coil is most sensitive to the tissue close to it, so the coils see the object with different weightings. These differences carry spatial information, which parallel imaging uses to reconstruct images from undersampled data. This tutorial undersamples the knee data from Direct Reconstruction, estimates the coil sensitivities and reconstructs a single image from the data of all eight coils with the reconstruction type \"multiCoil\". Finally, it adds a regularization that combines parallel imaging with compressed sensing.\n\nBesides MRIReco, the tutorial needs MRIFiles to read the data, MRISampling to undersample it, MRICoilSensitivities to estimate the coil sensitivities and CairoMakie for the plots. We use the same plotting function as in Direct Reconstruction.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling, MRICoilSensitivities, CairoMakie\n\nfunction showimages(images, titles; columns = length(images))\n    fig = Figure(size = (300 * columns, 310 * cld(length(images), columns)))\n    for (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip(images, titles))\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(i, columns)...], title = title,\n                  aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n        heatmap!(ax, image', colormap = :grays)\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n    end\n    return fig\nend","title":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#Loading-the-Data","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"The knee data is fully sampled. Its direct reconstruction, with the coil images combined by the root sum of squares, serves as the reference:\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfilename = joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")\nacqData = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(filename))\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\nrootSumOfSquares(images) = sqrt.(sum(abs2, images, dims = 5))[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1]\nreferenceImage = rootSumOfSquares(reconstruction(acqData, params))","title":"Loading the Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#Undersampling","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"We keep a quarter of the profiles. sample_kspace chooses them by Poisson-disk sampling and, with calsize = 30, always includes the 30 profiles in the center of k-space. These profiles form a fully sampled calibration region, from which we estimate the coil sensitivities below. The seed makes the random choice reproducible:\n\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30, seed = 1234)\n\nsamplingMask = zeros(320, 320)\nsamplingMask[acqDataSub.subsampleIndices[1]] .= 1\n\nThe direct reconstruction of the undersampled data is blurred and shows vertical stripes, because profiles are missing along the horizontal direction. We measure the deviation from the reference with nrmsd, the normalized root mean squared deviation:\n\ndirectImage = rootSumOfSquares(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params))\ndeviation(image) = round(nrmsd(referenceImage, image), sigdigits = 2)\nshowimages([referenceImage, samplingMask, directImage],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"Sampling mask\",\n            \"Direct, NRMSD $(deviation(directImage))\"])\nFigure()","title":"Undersampling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#Coil-Sensitivities","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction type \"multiCoil\" describes the data mathbfb_c of coil c by\n\nmathbfb_c = mathbfFmathbfS_cmathbfx\n\nwhere mathbfS_c multiplies the image mathbfx by the sensitivity of the coil, and mathbfF applies the Fourier transform and keeps the measured samples, like the encoding operator in Iterative Reconstruction. Image reconstruction with this model is known as SENSE. The sensitivities can be estimated from the data itself. espirit uses the calibration region for this; its second and third arguments are the kernel size and the size of the calibration region. It can also set the sensitivities to zero in voxels without signal. In this slice, the default threshold does this only for some patches of the background, so we turn it off with eigThresh_2 = 0:\n\nsensitivities = espirit(acqDataSub, (6, 6), 30, eigThresh_2 = 0)\nsize(sensitivities)\n(320, 320, 1, 8)\n\nThe dimensions are x, y, z and coils. Inside the knee, the sensitivities vary smoothly, and the coils are most sensitive in different regions. Outside the knee, where there is no signal, they are not meaningful:\n\nshowimages([abs.(sensitivities[:, :, 1, c]) for c in 1:8],\n           [\"Coil $c\" for c in 1:8], columns = 4)\nFigure()","title":"Coil Sensitivities"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#SENSE-Reconstruction","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"From the data of all coils, \"multiCoil\" reconstructs a single image by solving\n\nundersetmathbfxoperatornameargmin \nfrac12sum_c lVert mathbfFmathbfS_cmathbfx - mathbfb_c rVert_2^2 + mathrmR(mathbfx)\n\nSolving this problem with the conjugate gradient method is known as CG-SENSE. We pass the sensitivities as params[:senseMaps] and, as in Iterative Reconstruction, use CGNR with a small ell_2 regularization. With MeasurementBasedNormalization, λ is scaled by the mean magnitude of the measured data:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"multiCoil\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\nparams[:senseMaps] = sensitivities\nparams[:solver] = CGNR\nparams[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nparams[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparams[:iterations] = 10\nimageSENSE = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nshowimages([referenceImage, directImage, imageSENSE],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"Direct, NRMSD $(deviation(directImage))\",\n            \"CG-SENSE, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE))\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe result is a single image instead of eight coil images. It is sharper than the direct reconstruction, but at this reduction factor, the sensitivities do not suffice to remove all stripes.","title":"SENSE Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#Compressed-Sensing","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"MR images can be approximated well by a few large coefficients of a wavelet transform mathbfPhi. Compressed sensing exploits this. Because most of the profiles are chosen at random, the undersampling artifacts are spread like noise along the horizontal direction instead of forming shifted copies of the image. An ell_1 regularization of the wavelet coefficients, which penalizes lVert mathbfPhimathbfx rVert_1 with a weight set by λ, favors images with few large coefficients and thereby suppresses these artifacts. With params[:sparseTrafo] = \"Wavelet\", the ell_1 regularization applies to the wavelet coefficients. As in the knee example of the MRIReco.jl paper, we use ADMM with λ = 0.2 and 30 iterations:\n\nparams[:solver] = ADMM\nparams[:reg] = L1Regularization(0.2)\nparams[:sparseTrafo] = \"Wavelet\"\nparams[:iterations] = 30\nimageWavelet = abs.(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params)[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])\nshowimages([referenceImage, imageSENSE, imageWavelet],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"CG-SENSE, NRMSD $(deviation(imageSENSE))\",\n            \"ℓ1-wavelet, NRMSD $(deviation(imageWavelet))\"])\nFigure()\n\nThe wavelet regularization reduces the remaining stripes. The differences are easier to see in a detail of the joint:\n\ndetail = (100:260, 80:240)\nimages = [referenceImage, directImage, imageSENSE, imageWavelet]\nshowimages([image[detail...] for image in images],\n           [\"Fully sampled\", \"Direct\", \"CG-SENSE\", \"ℓ1-wavelet\"])\nFigure()","title":"Compressed Sensing"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multicoil/#Next-Steps","page":"Multi-Coil Reconstruction","text":"Iterative Reconstruction shows how the weight λ changes the result.\nThe RegularizedLeastSquares.jl documentation describes the available solvers and regularization terms.\nThe API reference describes reconstruction, espirit and sample_kspace.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#signal-model","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"A reconstruction needs a model of how the measured k-space data arises from the image. This page describes the signal model of MRIReco.jl, its discretization into the encoding operator and the operators that evaluate it for different trajectories, for several coils and with relaxation and off-resonance. Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem explains how a reconstruction uses this operator.","title":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#The-Signal-Model","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"MRIReco.jl uses the signal model of equation (2) in the MRIReco.jl paper, written here with the symbols of the tutorials. The demodulated signal that coil c receives at time t is\n\nb_c(t) = int S_c(mathbfr) x(mathbfr) mathrme^-z(mathbfr) t mathrme^-2pimathrmi mathbfk(t) cdot mathbfr mathrmdmathbfr\n\nwhere x(mathbfr) is the transverse magnetization at the position mathbfr, which is the image to reconstruct, S_c(mathbfr) is the sensitivity of the coil and mathbfk(t) is the k-space trajectory. The complex map z(mathbfr) = R_2^*(mathbfr) + mathrmiDeltaomega(mathbfr) contains the relaxation rate R_2^* in its real part and the off-resonance frequency Deltaomega, in radians per second, in its imaginary part. MRIReco.jl calls it the correction map. Without the correction map and with a single coil of uniform sensitivity, the signal is the Fourier transform of the image at the k-space position mathbfk(t), which is why the direct reconstruction of fully sampled Cartesian data is an inverse FFT.","title":"The Signal Model"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#The-Encoding-Operator","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"The signal is sampled at the times t_j, at the k-space positions mathbfk_j = mathbfk(t_j), and the image is represented by its values x_n at the positions mathbfr_n of a grid. The integral then becomes a sum,\n\nb_cj = sum_n S_c(mathbfr_n) x_n mathrme^-z(mathbfr_n) t_j mathrme^-2pimathrmi mathbfk_j cdot mathbfr_n\n\nso the samples of all coils, stacked into the vector mathbfb, depend linearly on the image vector mathbfx: mathbfb = mathbfEmathbfx with the encoding operator mathbfE.\n\nMRIReco.jl never stores mathbfE as a matrix, which for an image of 256 × 256 voxels and as many samples would have more than four billion entries. Like the operators of MRIOperators and LinearOperatorCollection.jl, it is matrix-free: it only implements how mathbfE and its adjoint mathbfE^mathsfH act on a vector, using fast algorithms such as the FFT. Operators are applied with *, their adjoint is adjoint(E), and ∘ combines them, which is how MRIOperators builds mathbfE from the operators described in the following sections.","title":"The Encoding Operator"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#Fourier-Encoding","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"Without the correction map and the coil sensitivities, mathbfE is a Fourier transform at the sampled k-space positions. MRIOperators chooses the operator by the trajectory, using operators of LinearOperatorCollection.jl:\n\nFor Cartesian trajectories, the samples lie on a grid, and the operator is an FFT, FFTOp. If only some of the grid points were measured, a SamplingOp keeps the measured samples.\nFor non-Cartesian trajectories, such as radial and spiral ones, the operator is a non-uniform FFT, NFFTOp, computed by NFFT.jl. The NFFT approximates the sum with an FFT on a grid that is finer than the image grid by the oversampling factor σ and a convolution with a small window. The parameters :oversamplingFactor and :kernelSize of the iterative reconstructions, with the defaults 1.25 and 3, set σ and the size of the window, and with them the balance between accuracy and speed. The direct reconstruction and reconstructions with a correction map always use the defaults.\n\nMRIOperators also provides ExplicitOp, which evaluates the sum directly, including the correction map. It is exact, but its cost grows with the number of samples times the number of voxels. The reconstruction therefore uses the fast operators. ExplicitOp mainly serves for simulations with :simulation = \"explicit\" and as a reference for testing the fast operators.","title":"Fourier Encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#Coil-Sensitivities","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"With several receive coils, the image is first multiplied by the sensitivities of all coils, which SensitivityOp does, and the image of each coil is then Fourier encoded:\n\nmathbfE = beginpmatrix mathbfF     ddots     mathbfF endpmatrix beginpmatrix mathbfS_1  vdots  mathbfS_C endpmatrix\n\nwhere mathbfF is the Fourier operator from above and mathbfS_c multiplies the image by the sensitivity of coil c. The block-diagonal operator is a DiagOp, which applies mathbfF to the image of each coil. This is the encoding operator of SENSE, which the reconstruction types with coil sensitivities use. Use Encoding Operators Directly builds it and solves the reconstruction problem with it.","title":"Coil Sensitivities"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#Relaxation-and-Off-Resonance","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"The factor mathrme^-z(mathbfr) t depends on both position and time, so it can't be moved out of the Fourier transform. FieldmapNFFTOp therefore approximates it by a sum of K products of a function of time and a function of position,\n\nmathrme^-z(mathbfr_n) t_j approx sum_kappa=1^K a_jkappa c_kappa n\n\nwhich turns the operator into a weighted sum of K NFFTs. The parameter :method of the iterative reconstructions chooses how the coefficients are computed. The default \"nfft\" approximates the exponential analytically and determines K from the ranges of the correction map and of the readout times, while data-driven methods such as \"leastsquare\" fit the coefficients for K time segments, which the parameter :K sets, to the correction map. Section 2.2 of the paper gives references for these approximations.\n\nThe iterative reconstructions take the correction map from the parameter :correctionMap. They count the times from the field TE of the trajectory and so reconstruct the magnetization at that time. For the built-in trajectories and custom ones without times, this is the time of the first sample of a readout, so for readouts that start at the edge of k-space, such as Cartesian lines and full radial spokes, it comes before the echo. Correct Off-Resonance shows how to pass a field map and which sampling times the correction needs.","title":"Relaxation and Off-Resonance"},{"category":"section","location":"explanations/signal_model/#The-Normal-Operator-and-the-Toeplitz-Approach","page":"Signal Model and Encoding Operators","text":"Iterative solvers such as CGNR apply the normal operator mathbfE^mathsfHmathbfE in every iteration, in MRIReco.jl together with the weighting described in Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem. MRIReco.jl builds it with normalOperator, which can use a faster implementation than applying mathbfE and mathbfE^mathsfH one after the other. For the NFFTOp, the normal operator is a convolution of the image with a kernel that depends only on the trajectory and the weights. With :toeplitz = true, MRIReco.jl computes this convolution with two FFTs on a grid of twice the image size in each dimension, so that the window convolutions of the NFFT are no longer needed in the iterations. Section 3.2 of the paper compares both approaches for a radial dataset: they were similarly fast for fully sampled data, while for fourfold undersampled data, the NFFT with σ = 1.25 was clearly faster, because its FFTs are smaller and fewer samples have to be gridded.","title":"The Normal Operator and the Toeplitz Approach"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/binning/#binning","page":"Bin Profiles into Contrasts","text":"Self-gated acquisitions derive a gating signal from data recorded after every excitation, for example a navigator echo, the free induction decay or the center of k-space in each readout, and sort the profiles afterwards. In the cardiac cine imaging of Trotier et al., the profiles are sorted into bins of the cardiac cycle, and each bin is reconstructed as an image of its own; Ribot et al. discard the profiles acquired during breathing instead. In MRIReco.jl, the bins become the contrasts of the data, usually with different numbers of profiles. This guide assigns the profiles of raw data to bins and reconstructs each bin. Data Model explains how RawAcquisitionData and AcquisitionData store the contrasts.","title":"Bin Profiles into Contrasts"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/binning/#Raw-Data","page":"Bin Profiles into Contrasts","text":"This guide bins raw data, in which every profile has its own header. Data that is already sorted into bins in arrays can instead be passed with one trajectory per bin, as in Create AcquisitionData from Arrays. The example simulates 400 radial profiles of a phantom with 256 samples each and converts the result into raw data:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation, CairoMakie\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nN = 256\nimage = shepp_logan(N)\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:trajName] = \"Radial\"\nparams[:numProfiles] = 400\nparams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = N\nacqData = simulation(image, params)\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(acqData)\nlength(rawData.profiles)\n400","title":"Raw Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/binning/#Assigning-the-Bins","page":"Bin Profiles into Contrasts","text":"The counter contrast in the header of each profile, counting from 0, assigns the profile to a bin. The ISMRMRD format also has a counter phase for cardiac phases, but the conversion into AcquisitionData does not use it, so bins stored in phase have to be copied into contrast. In a self-gated acquisition, the gating signal decides; here, every fourth profile goes into the first bin and the others into the second, so that the bins hold 100 and 300 profiles:\n\nfor (i, profile) in enumerate(rawData.profiles)\n    profile.head.idx.contrast = mod(i, 4) == 0 ? 0 : 1\nend\n\nThe conversion creates one trajectory per contrast. Unless the trajectory name is \"cartesian\", it takes the k-space positions from the profiles: with the name \"custom\", it collects the profiles of each contrast in their order. With the name \"Radial\" that the simulation stored, it would place them by their phase encoding counters instead, so that the trajectory of each bin would have all 400 profiles, those of the other bin at the center of k-space, and the reconstruction would fail.\n\nrawData.params[\"trajectory\"] = \"custom\"\nacqBinned = AcquisitionData(rawData)\n[numProfiles(trajectory(acqBinned, contrast)) for contrast in 1:2]\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 100\n 300","title":"Assigning the Bins"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/binning/#Reconstructing-the-Bins","page":"Bin Profiles into Contrasts","text":"The reconstruction returns one image per contrast, in the fourth dimension:\n\nrecoParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nrecoParams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nrecoParams[:reconSize] = (N, N)\nimages = reconstruction(acqBinned, recoParams)\nsize(images)\n(256, 256, 1, 2, 1, 1)\n\nThe figure shows both bins in the same gray scale, cut at a quarter of the maximum to make the streaks visible. The first bin, with a quarter of the profiles, shows more streaks than the second:\n\nm = maximum(abs.(images))\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 340))\nfor (i, title) in enumerate([\"Bin 1, 100 profiles\", \"Bin 2, 300 profiles\"])\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = title, aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(images[:, :, 1, i, 1, 1])', colormap = :grays, colorrange = (0, m / 4))\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Reconstructing the Bins"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/binning/#Limits","page":"Bin Profiles into Contrasts","text":"AcquisitionData has one trajectory per contrast for all slices and repetitions, so bins that hold different profiles in different slices or repetitions need one AcquisitionData per slice and repetition.\nThe conversion of a custom trajectory only works for one slice, repetition and average. With several slices or repetitions, it counts the profiles of a bin in all of them and adds profiles of zeros, which changes the result without an error, and several averages are kept as separate profiles instead of being averaged (#331).\n\nIn both cases, convert each slice and repetition on its own: keep only its profiles, set their counters slice and repetition to 0, and convert and reconstruct them as above.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Contribute-to-MRIReco.jl","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"Thanks for helping to improve MRIReco.jl! This guide explains how to ask for help, how to report problems and how to contribute changes. Everyone taking part in this project is expected to follow the Code of Conduct.","title":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Questions-and-Bug-Reports","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"If you have a question about using the packages, please ask in GitHub Discussions.\n\nIf you find a bug or want to suggest a feature, please search the existing issues first and open a new one if nothing matches. A good bug report contains\n\na short example that we can run to reproduce the problem, ideally with simulated data or a small public dataset,\nthe complete error message and stack trace,\nthe versions you used, which you can print with\n\nusing Pkg\nprintln(\"Julia \", VERSION)\nfor (_, pkg) in Pkg.dependencies()\n    startswith(pkg.name, \"MRI\") && println(pkg.name, \" \", pkg.version)\nend","title":"Questions and Bug Reports"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Repository-Layout","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"MRIReco.jl is a monorepo. The root directory contains the MRIReco package, and each subpackage lives in its own directory with its own Project.toml:\n\nPackage Purpose\nMRIBase Data types for raw data, acquisition data and trajectories\nMRIOperators Encoding operators\nMRIFiles Reading and writing raw data files\nMRISampling Undersampling patterns\nMRICoilSensitivities Coil sensitivity estimation and coil compression\nMRISimulation Simulation of MRI data\nMRIReco Image reconstruction\n\nThe subpackages are registered as separate packages, but all of them except MRIBase depend on other packages in this repository. During development, use the local copies of these packages instead of the registered versions, as described below.","title":"Repository Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Development-Setup","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"Fork the repository on GitHub and clone your fork.\nStart Julia 1.10 or newer in the repository root and activate the package you want to work on, for example MRISampling.\nDevelop the local copies of the packages it needs and install the remaining dependencies:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"MRISampling\")\nPkg.develop([PackageSpec(path = \"MRIBase\")])\nPkg.instantiate()\n\nThe packages to develop are the ones that the CI uses (deps in .github/workflows/ci.yml). They include packages that are only needed for the tests:\n\nPackage Path to activate Paths to develop\nMRIReco . MRIBase, MRIOperators, MRISimulation, MRISampling\nMRIBase MRIBase none\nMRIFiles MRIFiles ., MRIBase, MRICoilSensitivities\nMRIOperators MRIOperators MRIBase, MRISimulation\nMRISampling MRISampling MRIBase\nMRISimulation MRISimulation MRIBase, MRIOperators\nMRICoilSensitivities MRICoilSensitivities ., MRIBase, MRISimulation, MRISampling\n\nFor MRIOperators, developing MRISimulation creates a dependency cycle, because MRISimulation itself depends on MRIOperators. Pkg warns about the cycle and skips precompiling these two packages; the CI uses the same setup.\n\nPkg.develop changes the Manifest.toml of the activated package and may also change its Project.toml, for example by adding the developed packages. Manifest files are ignored by git, but please don't commit changes that Pkg makes to a Project.toml unless your pull request is about dependencies.","title":"Development Setup"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Running-the-Tests","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"With the environment of a package activated as above, run its tests with\n\nPkg.test(\"MRISampling\")\n\nThe CI tests a pull request in three ways:\n\nThe \"All Packages (latest)\" workflow tests every package with the local copies from the table above, on the latest Julia release and on Julia 1.10. It shows whether the packages work together in their current state.\nEach package also has its own workflow, which runs when the package changes. It tests the package on Linux, macOS and Windows with the released versions of its dependencies, which shows whether the package can be released on its own.\nChanges to MRIBase and MRIOperators are also tested against the released versions of the packages that depend on them, which shows whether the changes would break them.\n\nThe steps of these workflows are in .github/workflows/, so you can also run them locally. GPU tests run on Buildkite with NVIDIA and AMD GPUs; the commands are in .buildkite/pipeline.yml, and the tests are in test/gpu/ and MRIOperators/test/gpu/.","title":"Running the Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Building-the-Documentation","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"The documentation is built with Documenter.jl, and the scripts in docs/src/literate are converted into pages with Literate.jl. To build it locally, develop all packages into the docs environment, as the CI does:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"docs\")\nPkg.develop([PackageSpec(path = \".\"), PackageSpec(path = \"MRIBase\"),\n             PackageSpec(path = \"MRIOperators\"), PackageSpec(path = \"MRIFiles\"),\n             PackageSpec(path = \"MRISampling\"), PackageSpec(path = \"MRISimulation\"),\n             PackageSpec(path = \"MRICoilSensitivities\")])\nPkg.instantiate()\n\nThen run julia --project=docs docs/make.jl from the repository root and open docs/build/index.html. The build runs all examples, so it takes several minutes. Don't commit the changes that Pkg.develop makes to docs/Project.toml.","title":"Building the Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Changing-Several-Packages","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"Some changes affect more than one package, for example a new function in MRIBase that MRIFiles uses. Work in the environment of the package that depends on the other one, here MRIFiles. MRIBase is developed in this environment (see the table above), so MRIFiles uses your local copy of MRIBase, and the tests of MRIFiles run with your changes to both packages. Then run the tests of MRIBase in its own environment:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.activate(\"MRIFiles\")\nPkg.develop([PackageSpec(path = \".\"), PackageSpec(path = \"MRIBase\"),\n             PackageSpec(path = \"MRICoilSensitivities\")])\nPkg.instantiate()\nPkg.test(\"MRIFiles\")\n\nPkg.activate(\"MRIBase\")\nPkg.instantiate()\nPkg.test(\"MRIBase\")\n\nThe documentation build also uses the local copies of all packages, so it includes both changes. In the CI, the workflow of MRIFiles tests it with the released version of MRIBase, so it fails until the changes in MRIBase are released. Please mention in the pull request that MRIFiles needs a new release of MRIBase, so the packages can be released in the right order.","title":"Changing Several Packages"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Pull-Requests","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"If you are looking for something to work on, the issues labeled good first issue are a good place to start.\n\nKeep a pull request focused on one fix or feature; smaller pull requests are easier to review.\nAdd tests for new functionality and bug fixes.\nAdd a docstring to every new exported function or type, and check that the documentation still builds.\nThere is no enforced code formatter. Please follow the style of the surrounding code.\nMention the issue that the pull request addresses, e.g. Fixes #123.\n\nPlease make sure that the CI passes, or explain in the pull request why a check fails.","title":"Pull Requests"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#License","page":"Contribute to MRIReco.jl","text":"By contributing, you agree that your contributions are licensed under the MIT License of this repository.","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco","page":"MRIReco","text":"MRIReco provides the image reconstruction of the framework. Of the packages in this repository, it depends on MRIBase and MRIOperators. It re-exports both, together with RegularizedLeastSquares.jl and AbstractImageReconstruction.jl, so the data types, encoding operators, solvers, regularization terms and the functions for reconstruction plans are available after using MRIReco. MRIFiles, MRISampling, MRICoilSensitivities and MRISimulation are independent of MRIReco and have to be loaded separately.","title":"MRIReco"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#Reconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"","title":"Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#Reconstruction-Plans","page":"MRIReco","text":"reconstruction translates the parameter dictionary into a reconstruction plan of AbstractImageReconstruction.jl and runs it. A plan for each reconstruction type is stored in reco_plans/ and can be loaded with MRIRecoPlan, e.g. MRIRecoPlan(\"multiCoil\"), or run directly with reconstruct(\"multiCoil\", acqData; kwargs...).","title":"Reconstruction Plans"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#Iterative-Reconstructions","page":"MRIReco","text":"","title":"Iterative Reconstructions"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#Parameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#Deprecated-Reconstruction-Functions","page":"MRIReco","text":"Before reconstruction plans, reconstruction called one of the following functions. They are deprecated.","title":"Deprecated Reconstruction Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIReco/#Tools","page":"MRIReco","text":"","title":"Tools"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.reconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"reconstruction(acqData::AcquisitionData, recoParams::Dict)\n\nPerforms image reconstruction of an AcquisitionData object. Parameters are specified in a dictionary.\n\nReconstruction types are specified by the key :reco. Valid reconstruction names are:\n\n\"direct\"             - direct Fourier reconstruction (default)\n\"standard\"           - iterative reconstruction for all contrasts, coils & slices independently\n\"multiEcho\"          - iterative joint reconstruction of all echo images\n\"multiCoil\"          - SENSE-type iterative reconstruction\n\"multiCoilMultiEcho\" - SENSE-type iterative reconstruction of all echo images\n\"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" - SENSE-type iterative reconstruction of the coefficient maps of a temporal basis\n\nThe dictionary is merged into the defaults of defaultRecoParams, and :reconSize defaults to the encoding size of acqData. reconstruction then loads the plan of the reconstruction type with MRIRecoPlan. For the iterative reconstructions, a few keys first select the type of a parameter: :densityWeighting => false selects UniformWeightingParameters, :weights selects CustomWeightingParameters, :encodingOps selects CustomEncodingParameters, and a :sparseTrafo with an operator selects CustomSparsityParameters; \"direct\" does not support these keys and warns that it cannot set the parameter they select. reconstruction then sets the parameters of the same name as the other keys, for example iterations of LeastSquaresSolverParameter for :iterations, and runs the plan.\n\nOther keys that the plan does not have are ignored. If the value of such a key is a parameter object such as UniformWeightingParameters(), an algorithm or a plan, reconstruction warns; with ENV[\"JULIA_DEBUG\"] = \"MRIReco\", it lists the remaining keys that the plan does not have, :densityWeighting included.\n\n\n\n\n\nreconstruction(acqData::AcquisitionData, recoParams::Dict, filename::String;\n               force=false)\n\nThis method is meant to save the result of reconstruction(acqData, recoParams) in the file filename, or to load it from there if force=false and the file exists. The functions it calls for this are no longer part of MRIReco, so it throws an UndefVarError: right away if force=false and the file exists, otherwise after the reconstruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.reconstruction"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.defaultRecoParams","page":"MRIReco","text":"defaultRecoParams() -> Dict{Symbol, Any}\n\nReturn the default parameters of reconstruction: :reco => \"direct\", :reg => L1Regularization(0.0), :normalizeReg => NoNormalization(), :solver => ADMM, :rho => 5e-2, :iterations => 30 and :arrayType => Array. reconstruction merges the given dictionary into them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.defaultRecoParams"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#AbstractImageReconstruction.reconstruct-Tuple{AbstractString, AcquisitionData}","page":"MRIReco","text":"reconstruct(name::AbstractString, acqData::AcquisitionData; modules = getMRIRecoPlanModules(), kwargs...)\n\nPerform a reconstruction of acqData with the RecoPlan specified by name. Additional keyword arguments can be passed to the reconstruction plan. modules are the modules whose types the plan file can use, see addMRIRecoPlanModule.\n\nThe plan is looked up as the file name.toml in the folders added with addMRIRecoPlanPath, the last added first, and then in the folder reco_plans of MRIReco. Alternatively, name can be the path of a plan file.\n\nExamples\n\nIreco = reconstruct(\"standard\", acqData; kwargs...)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractImageReconstruction.reconstruct"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MRIRecoPlan","page":"MRIReco","text":"MRIRecoPlan(value, modules = getMRIRecoPlanModules(); kwargs...)\n\nLoad a RecoPlan and set the keyword arguments if applicable. Value can be the name of a plan or path to plan file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MRIRecoPlan"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.addMRIRecoPlanModule-Tuple{Module}","page":"MRIReco","text":"addMRIRecoPlanModule(mod::Module)\n\nAdd the mod module to the list of modules which are used for plan loading, so that plan files can use the types that mod exports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.addMRIRecoPlanModule"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.addMRIRecoPlanPath-Tuple{Any}","page":"MRIReco","text":"addMRIRecoPlanPath(path)\n\nAdd the folder path to the folders in which MRIRecoPlan, reconstruct and reconstruction look for plan files. Added folders are searched before the folder reco_plans of MRIReco, the last added first, so a plan file with the name of a built-in plan, for example standard.toml, replaces it. A folder is added only once and only for the current Julia session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.addMRIRecoPlanPath"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.getMRIRecoPlanList-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"getMRIRecoPlanList(; full = false)\n\nRetrieve a list of currently known RecoPlans. If full is true then full paths are returned. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.getMRIRecoPlanList"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.getMRIRecoPlanModules-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"getMRIRecoPlanModules()\n\nRetrieve a list of currently used modules for plan loading \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.getMRIRecoPlanModules"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MRIRecoContext","page":"MRIReco","text":"MRIRecoContext{D, arrT, S, Acq}\n\nContainer for reconstruction context parameters that are used throughout the  reconstruction pipeline. Uses a ScopedValue for task-local access.\n\nFields\n\nreconSize::NTuple{D, Int64} - Size of the reconstructed image\narrayType::Type{arrT} - Array type for computation (Array, JLArray, etc.)\nstorageType::Type{S} - Storage type for operators\nacqData::Acq - Acquisition data or nothing, provides metadata like trajectory info\nred3d::Bool - true if 3D-encoded data was converted to 2D slices and the user gave a 3D reconSize; user-supplied volumes (e.g. sensitivity maps) are then in 3D order and need to be permuted\n\nUsage\n\n# Set the context for reconstruction\nctx = MRIRecoContext((128,128), Float64, Array)\n\n# Or with acquisition data\nctx = MRIRecoContext((128,128), acqData, Array)\n\n# Using with() to set scoped value\nwith(MRIRECO_CONTEXT => ctx) do\n    # reconstruction code\nend\n\n# And then access anywhere in the reconstruction pipeline\nfunction foo(sparsity_params)\n    rs = ctx_reconSize()  # Get from scoped value\n    ad = ctx_acqData()    # Get acquisition data if needed\n    # ...\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MRIRecoContext"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.ctx_acqData-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"ctx_acqData() -> Union{AcquisitionData, Nothing}\n\nGet the acquisition data from the current MRI reconstruction context. Returns nothing if not set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.ctx_acqData"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.ctx_arrayType-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"ctx_arrayType() -> Type\n\nGet the array type from the current MRI reconstruction context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.ctx_arrayType"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.ctx_reconSize-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"ctx_reconSize() -> NTuple\n\nGet the reconstruction size from the current MRI reconstruction context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.ctx_reconSize"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.ctx_red3d-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"ctx_red3d() -> Bool\n\nReturn true if the current reconstruction treats 3D-encoded data that was converted to 2D slices with a 3D reconSize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.ctx_red3d"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.ctx_storageType-Tuple{}","page":"MRIReco","text":"ctx_storageType() -> Type\n\nGet the storage type from the current MRI reconstruction context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.ctx_storageType"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.AbstractDirectMRIRecoParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"AbstractDirectMRIRecoParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoParameters\n\nAbstract type for direct (non-iterative) MRI reconstruction parameters. Performs a direct Fourier-based image reconstruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.AbstractDirectMRIRecoParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.DirectMRIReco","page":"MRIReco","text":"DirectMRIReco{P} <: AbstractDirectMRIRecoAlgorithm\n\nThe reconstruction type \"direct\": a direct image reconstruction which applies the adjoint of the encoding operator to the density-compensated k-space data.\n\nIts parameter is a DirectMRIRecoParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.DirectMRIReco"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.DirectMRIRecoParameter","page":"MRIReco","text":"DirectMRIRecoParameter{S, C, W, arrT} <: AbstractDirectMRIRecoParameters\n\nParameters of the direct reconstruction DirectMRIReco.\n\nFields\n\nreconSize::S - Size of the image to reconstruct (default: nothing, the encoding size)\ncmap::C - Complex correction map of two-dimensional data for off-resonance and relaxation, in the precision of the data, always approximated with \"nfft\", which works for a map that varies within each slice and sampling times that are not all equal (default: nothing); in the parameter dictionary of reconstruction, its key is :cmap, not :correctionMap\nweights::W - Has to stay nothing: the reconstruction then weights the data with the density compensation factors, the squares of the weights from samplingDensity\narrayType::Type{arrT} - Array type of the reconstruction (default: Array)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.DirectMRIRecoParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.AbstractIterativeRecoParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"AbstractIterativeRecoParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoParameters\n\nAbstract base type for iterative reconstruction algorithm parameters. Subtypes must implement callable methods for use with  AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter.\n\nRequired Callable Interface\n\nAllocation\n\n(params)(algo, reconSize) -> (Ireco, indices, weights, extra...)\n\nAllocates the output array and returns iteration indices and weights.\n\nalgo - The algorithm\nreconSize::NTuple{D, Int64} - Reconstruction size\nReturns a tuple of (Ireco, indices, weights, extra...) where:\nIreco::Array{Complex{T}} - Output image array\nindices - CartesianIndices for iteration\nweights - Sampling weights\nextra... - Optional additional data (e.g., decorrelatedSenseMaps, L_inv for coil recon)\n\nLoop Body\n\n(params)(algo, Ireco, index, weights, extra...)\n\nCalled once per iteration index. Writes results to Ireco.\n\nalgo - The algorithm\nIreco - Output array to write to\nindex::CartesianIndex - Current index, such as (rep, slice)\nweights - Sampling weights from allocation\nextra... - Optional additional data from allocation\n\nFinalization\n\n(params)(algo, Ireco) -> result\n\nFinalizes and returns the reconstruction result.\n\nalgo - The algorithm\nIreco::Array{Complex{T}} - The filled output array\nReturns final result (typically AxisArray)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.AbstractIterativeRecoParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter","page":"MRIReco","text":"AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter{P} <: AbstractMRIRecoParameters\n\nAbstract base type for iterative reconstruction context parameters. Provides the runtime framework (context, parallelization, allocation)  while the inner parameter implements the algorithm-specific loop body.\n\nType Parameters\n\nP - The inner algorithm parameter type (e.g., AbstractStandardParameters)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.SerialIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter","page":"MRIReco","text":"SerialIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter{P, RS, AT} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter{P}\n\nSerial (non-parallel) iterative reconstruction context parameter. Uses a simple for loop for iteration.\n\nType Parameters\n\nP - The inner algorithm parameter type\nRS - ReconSize type (NTuple or Nothing)\nAT - Array type\n\nFields\n\nreconSize::RS - Reconstruction size (nothing = infer from acqData)\narrayType::Type{AT} - Array type for computation\nparameter::P - Inner algorithm-specific parameter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.SerialIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter","page":"MRIReco","text":"ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter{P, RS, AT, S} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter{P}\n\nThreaded iterative reconstruction context parameter using OhMyThreads. Uses @tasks for parallel execution.\n\nType Parameters\n\nP - The inner algorithm parameter type\nRS - ReconSize type (NTuple or Nothing)\nAT - Array type\nS - Scheduler type\n\nFields\n\nreconSize::RS - Reconstruction size (nothing = infer from acqData)\narrayType::Type{AT} - Array type for computation\nscheduler::S - Task scheduler (nothing = auto-based on arrayType)\nparameter::P - Inner algorithm-specific parameter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.StandardIterativeParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"StandardIterativeParameters{E, W, S} <: AbstractStandardParameters\n\nParameters of the iterative reconstruction StandardReconstruction.\n\nFields\n\nencodingParams::E - Encoding operator parameters\nweightingParams::W - Sampling weighting parameters\nsolverParams::S - Least squares solver parameters\n\nCallable Interface\n\n# Allocation - allocates output array and returns iteration indices and weights\n(params::StandardIterativeParameters)(algo, reconSize) -> (Ireco, indices, weights)\n\n# Loop body - called per index with weights and output array\n(params::StandardIterativeParameters)(algo, Ireco, index, weights)\n\n# Finalization - reshapes and wraps result\n(params::StandardIterativeParameters)(algo, Ireco) -> AxisArray\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.StandardIterativeParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.StandardReconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"StandardReconstruction{P, C} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm\n\nThe reconstruction type \"standard\": an iterative image reconstruction which reconstructs the data of all coils, contrasts and slices independently.\n\nIts parameter is an AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter that holds StandardIterativeParameters; its stored plan uses a ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.StandardReconstruction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MultiEchoIterativeParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"MultiEchoIterativeParameters{E, W, S} <: AbstractMultiEchoParameters\n\nParameters of the iterative reconstruction MultiEchoReconstruction.\n\nFields\n\nencodingParams::E - Encoding operator parameters\nweightingParams::W - Sampling weighting parameters\nsolverParams::S - Least squares solver parameters\n\nCallable Interface\n\n# Allocation - allocates output array and returns iteration indices and weights\n(params::MultiEchoIterativeParameters)(algo, reconSize) -> (Ireco, indices, weights)\n\n# Loop body - called per index with weights and output array\n(params::MultiEchoIterativeParameters)(algo, Ireco, index, weights)\n\n# Finalization - reshapes and wraps result\n(params::MultiEchoIterativeParameters)(algo, Ireco) -> AxisArray\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MultiEchoIterativeParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MultiEchoReconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"MultiEchoReconstruction{P, C} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm\n\nThe reconstruction type \"multiEcho\": an iterative image reconstruction which reconstructs all contrasts jointly. Different slices and coil images are reconstructed independently. For more than one contrast, with ADMM, the default solver, or with SplitBregman, give every regularization term a transform, also the default term used without :reg; \"nothing\" is the identity, for example :sparseTrafo => \"nothing\" for one term or :sparseTrafo => [\"nothing\", \"nothing\"] for two.\n\nIts parameter is an AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter that holds MultiEchoIterativeParameters; its stored plan uses a ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MultiEchoReconstruction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MultiCoilIterativeParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"MultiCoilIterativeParameters{E, W, S, C} <: AbstractMultiCoilParameters\n\nParameters of the SENSE-type iterative reconstructions MultiCoilReconstruction, MultiCoilMultiEchoReconstruction and MultiCoilMultiEchoSubspaceReconstruction.\n\nFields\n\nencodingParams::E - Encoding operator parameters\nweightingParams::W - Sampling weighting parameters\nsolverParams::S - Least squares solver parameters\ncoilParams::C - Coil sensitivity and noise decorrelation parameters\n\nCallable Interface\n\n# Allocation - allocates output array and returns iteration indices, weights, and coil data\n(params::MultiCoilIterativeParameters)(algo, reconSize) -> (Ireco, indices, weights, decorrelatedSenseMaps, L_inv)\n\n# Loop body - called per index with weights and output array\n(params::MultiCoilIterativeParameters)(algo, Ireco, index, weights, decorrelatedSenseMaps, L_inv)\n\n# Finalization - reshapes and wraps result\n(params::MultiCoilIterativeParameters)(algo, Ireco) -> AxisArray\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MultiCoilIterativeParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MultiCoilReconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"MultiCoilReconstruction{P, C} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm\n\nThe reconstruction type \"multiCoil\": a SENSE-type iterative image reconstruction. Different slices and contrasts are reconstructed independently.\n\nIts parameter is an AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter that holds MultiCoilIterativeParameters; its stored plan uses a ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MultiCoilReconstruction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MultiCoilMultiEchoReconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"MultiCoilMultiEchoReconstruction{P, C} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm\n\nThe reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEcho\": a SENSE-type iterative image reconstruction which reconstructs all contrasts jointly. Different slices are reconstructed independently. For more than one contrast, with ADMM, the default solver, or with SplitBregman, give every regularization term a transform, also the default term used without :reg; \"nothing\" is the identity, for example :sparseTrafo => \"nothing\" for one term or :sparseTrafo => [\"nothing\", \"nothing\"] for two.\n\nIts parameter is an AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter that holds MultiCoilIterativeParameters; its stored plan uses a ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MultiCoilMultiEchoReconstruction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.AbstractSubspaceParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"AbstractSubspaceParameters <: AbstractIterativeRecoParameters\n\nAbstract type for subspace-based reconstruction parameters. Subspace methods reconstruct images in a lower-dimensional subspace basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.AbstractSubspaceParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.MultiCoilMultiEchoSubspaceReconstruction","page":"MRIReco","text":"MultiCoilMultiEchoSubspaceReconstruction{P, C} <: AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm\n\nThe reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\": a SENSE-type iterative image reconstruction which reconstructs all contrasts jointly using a subspace basis. Its result holds one coefficient map per basis function. Different slices are reconstructed independently. For more than one basis function, with ADMM, the default solver, or with SplitBregman, give every regularization term a transform, also the default term used without :reg; \"nothing\" is the identity, for example :sparseTrafo => \"nothing\" for one term or :sparseTrafo => [\"nothing\", \"nothing\"] for two.\n\nIts parameter is an AbstractIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter that holds MultiCoilIterativeParameters, whose field encodingParams has to be a SubspaceEncodingParameters with the basis; its stored plan uses a ThreadedIterativeMRIRecoContextParameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.MultiCoilMultiEchoSubspaceReconstruction"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.AbstractMRIRecoEncodingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"AbstractMRIRecoEncodingParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoParameters\n\nAbstract base type for encoding operator parameters used in MRI reconstruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.AbstractMRIRecoEncodingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.CustomEncodingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"CustomEncodingParameters{E} <: AbstractMRIRecoEncodingParameters\n\nParameters with user-provided encoding operators.\n\nType Parameters\n\nE - The type of the encoding operators\n\nFields\n\nencodingOps::E - Pre-computed encoding operators of \"standard\" and \"multiCoil\" as a matrix with one row per contrast and one column per slice. For \"multiCoil\", the operators include the coil sensitivities, like those of encodingOps_parallel, and the reconstruction still needs its CoilParameters; with noiseData, it decorrelates the data but not these operators.\n\nCallable Interface\n\n(ep::CustomEncodingParameters)(algo, slice::Int, args...)\n\nReturns\n\nThe encoding operators for the given slice, encodingOps[:, slice]\n\nExample\n\nep = CustomEncodingParameters(; encodingOps=myEncodingOps)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.CustomEncodingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.EncodingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"EncodingParameters{C} <: AbstractMRIRecoEncodingParameters\n\nParameters for computing encoding operators from acquisition data.\n\nType Parameters\n\nC - Type of correctionMap, an AbstractArray{<:Complex} or Nothing\n\nFields\n\nAll fields are nothing by default, and the operator then uses its own default given below.\n\ncorrectionMap::C - Complex correction map z of two-dimensional data for off-resonance and relaxation, in the precision of the data, see Correct Off-Resonance (default: none)\nmethod::Union{String, Nothing} - Approximation of exp(-z t), where t is the sampling time, for a map that varies within each slice and sampling times that are not all equal: \"nfft\", \"leastsquare\", \"hist\", which works for a map with only an off-resonance part, or \"svd\", which works for double-precision data, a map with a spatially varying off-resonance part and profiles with the same sampling times and about ten or more samples per term (default: \"nfft\"), see FieldmapNFFTOp\ntoeplitz::Union{Bool, Nothing} - Whether the normal operator uses the Toeplitz approach (default: false)\noversamplingFactor::Union{Float64, Nothing} - NFFT oversampling factor (default: 1.25)\nkernelSize::Union{Int, Nothing} - NFFT kernel size (default: 3)\nK::Union{Int, Nothing} - Number of terms of \"leastsquare\" and \"hist\" and maximum number of terms of \"svd\" (default: 20); \"nfft\" determines the number itself\nK_tol::Union{Float64, Nothing} - Tolerance with which \"svd\" chooses the number of terms (default: 1e-3)\n\nmethod, K and K_tol only take effect with a correction map, and toeplitz, oversamplingFactor and kernelSize only for non-Cartesian data without a correction map.\n\nCallable Interface\n\n(ep::EncodingParameters)(algo, slice::Int)\n(ep::EncodingParameters)(algo, slice::Int, senseMaps)\n\nReturns\n\nThe encoding operators of the given slice: one per contrast for the reconstructions that treat the contrasts separately, and otherwise one for all contrasts. The second form, used by the reconstructions with coil sensitivities, includes the sensitivities.\n\nNote\n\nGets the data, the reconstruction size and the storage type from the MRIRecoContext of the reconstruction\n\nExample\n\nep = EncodingParameters()\n\n# With a correction map\nep = EncodingParameters(; correctionMap = cmap, method = \"leastsquare\", K = 10)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.EncodingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.SubspaceEncodingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"SubspaceEncodingParameters{E, B} <: AbstractMRIRecoEncodingParameters\n\nEncoding parameters with subspace projection for dimensionality reduction.\n\nType Parameters\n\nE - The inner encoding parameters type\nB - The basis matrix type\n\nFields\n\ninner::E - Inner encoding parameters\nbasis::B - Subspace basis matrix with one row per contrast and one column per basis function, in the precision of the data\n\nCallable Interface\n\n(ep::SubspaceEncodingParameters)(algo, slice::Int, args...)\n\nReturns\n\nEncoding operator of inner composed with a SubspaceOp of the basis\n\nExample\n\nep = SubspaceEncodingParameters(; inner=EncodingParameters(), basis=basisMatrix)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.SubspaceEncodingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.AbstractMRIRecoWeightingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"AbstractMRIRecoWeightingParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoParameters\n\nAbstract base type for weighting parameters used in MRI reconstruction. Subtypes define how weights are computed for the reconstruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.AbstractMRIRecoWeightingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.CustomWeightingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"CustomWeightingParameters{W} <: AbstractMRIRecoWeightingParameters\n\nWeighting parameters that use user-provided custom weights.\n\nType Parameters\n\nW - The type of the weights (typically Vector{Vector{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}})\n\nFields\n\nweights::W - User-provided weights\n\nDescription\n\nAllows explicit specification of weighting vectors for data with one coil: a vector of vectors, one per contrast, in the form that samplingDensity returns.\n\nCallable Interface\n\n(wp::CustomWeightingParameters)(::Type{<:AbstractMRIRecoAlgorithm})\n\nReturns\n\nThe stored weights vector\n\nValidation\n\nWeights are validated during reconstruction to ensure they match the acquisition data structure (same number of contrasts and samples).\n\nExample\n\nweights = [[1.0+0im, 2.0+0im], [3.0+0im, 4.0+0im]]\nwp = CustomWeightingParameters(; weights=weights)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.CustomWeightingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.DensityWeightingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"DensityWeightingParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoWeightingParameters\n\nWeighting parameters that compute density compensation weights from the acquisition data.\n\nDescription\n\nUses the trajectory information in the acquisition data to compute appropriate density compensation weights. This is the default weighting scheme for most reconstructions.\n\nCallable Interface\n\n(wp::DensityWeightingParameters)(::Type{<:AbstractMRIRecoAlgorithm})\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Vector{Complex{T}}} - One weight vector per contrast, from samplingDensity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.DensityWeightingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.UniformWeightingParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"UniformWeightingParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoWeightingParameters\n\nWeighting parameters that use uniform weights for all k-space samples.\n\nDescription\n\nFor data with one coil, assigns uniform weight 1/sqrt(prod(reconSize)) to each k-space sample.\n\nCallable Interface\n\n(wp::UniformWeightingParameters)(::Type{<:AbstractMRIRecoAlgorithm})\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Vector{Complex{T}}} - One weight vector per contrast\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.UniformWeightingParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.CoilParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"CoilParameters{T, S, N} <: AbstractCoilParameters\nCoilParameters(; senseMaps = nothing, noiseData = nothing)\n\nCoil sensitivities and noise data of the SENSE-type reconstructions \"multiCoil\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\", as complex arrays in the precision of the data. The constructor needs at least one of the two, and the reconstructions with EncodingParameters need senseMaps.\n\nFields\n\nsenseMaps::S - Coil sensitivities with the dimensions x, y, z or slice, and coil, as espirit returns them when                  called on an AcquisitionData with nmaps = 1, see                  Estimate Coil Sensitivities with ESPIRiT\nnoiseData::N - Noise samples as a matrix of samples × coils; if given, the k-space data and the coil                  sensitivities are decorrelated, see Noise Decorrelation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.CoilParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.CustomSparsityParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"CustomSparsityParameters{L} <: AbstractSparsityParameters\n\nParameters specifying custom sparsifying transforms, see Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform.\n\nFields\n\nsparseTrafo::L - The transform of the first regularization term (an AbstractLinearOperator such as                    LinearOperator(M) for a matrix M, or a name as for SimpleSparsityParameters) or a                    vector with one entry per term (default: nothing). With nothing or a missing entry, a term                    has no transform.\n\nExamples\n\nCustomSparsityParameters(op)                # op for the first term\nCustomSparsityParameters([op, nothing])     # op for the first term, none for the second\nCustomSparsityParameters()                  # no transform\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.CustomSparsityParameters"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.CustomSparsityParameters-Union{Tuple{L}, Tuple{Type{<:AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm}, Int64}} where L","page":"MRIReco","text":"(sparsity::CustomSparsityParameters)(algo, numTerms::Int64) -> Vector\n\nBuild the transforms of numTerms regularization terms: the given operators, a SparseOp for each name and nothing for each term without a transform. Reconstructions that solve for all contrasts or basis functions at once repeat each transform for every contrast or basis function, so an operator has to act on a single image.\n\nNote\n\nGets the reconstruction size and the storage type from the MRIRecoContext of the reconstruction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.CustomSparsityParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.LeastSquaresSolverParameter","page":"MRIReco","text":"LeastSquaresSolverParameter{SL, R, T} <: SolverParameters\n\nParametric solver parameters for iterative MRI reconstruction using RegularizedLeastSquares.\n\nType Parameters\n\nSL - Solver type: ADMM, CGNR, FISTA, POGM, OptISTA or SplitBregman\nR - RegularizationParameters type\nT - Floating-point type of rho and the tolerances\n\nFields\n\nExcept for solver and regParams, a field is passed to the solver as the keyword argument of the same name, but only if it is not nothing and the solver takes that keyword; otherwise the solver uses its own default.\n\nsolver::Type{SL} - Solver type (default: ADMM)\nregParams::R - Regularization terms and their transforms (default: RegularizationParameters())\nnormalizeReg::AbstractRegularizationNormalization - Scaling of the weights λ of the terms: NoNormalization(), MeasurementBasedNormalization() or SystemMatrixBasedNormalization() (default: NoNormalization())\niterations::Int - Maximum iterations; for SplitBregman, the outer iterations (default: 30)\nrho::T - Penalty parameter of ADMM and SplitBregman and step size of FISTA, POGM and OptISTA (default: 5e-2).            FISTA, POGM and OptISTA always receive it, so they do not determine the step size themselves.\niterationsInner::Union{Int, Nothing} - Inner iterations of SplitBregman (default: nothing)\niterationsCG::Union{Int, Nothing} - CG iterations of ADMM and SplitBregman (default: nothing)\nabsTol::Union{T, Nothing} - Absolute tolerance of ADMM and SplitBregman (default: nothing)\nrelTol::Union{T, Nothing} - Relative tolerance (default: nothing)\ntolInner::Union{T, Nothing} - Tolerance of the CG iterations of ADMM and SplitBregman (default: nothing)\nverbose::Bool - Print progress in all solvers except CGNR (default: false)\nrestart::Symbol - Restart of FISTA and POGM: :none or :gradient (default: :none)\nvary_rho::Symbol - Adaptation of rho in ADMM: :none, :balance or :PnP (default: :none)\n\nExamples\n\n# With L1 + Wavelet regularization\nparams = LeastSquaresSolverParameter(solver = FISTA,\n  regParams = RegularizationParameters(\n    reg = L1Regularization(1e-3),\n    sparsityParams = SimpleSparsityParameters(\"Wavelet\")\n  ),\n  iterations = 100\n)\n\n# Multiple regularization terms (ADMM)\nparams = LeastSquaresSolverParameter(solver = ADMM,\n  regParams = RegularizationParameters(\n    reg = [L1Regularization(1e-3), L2Regularization(1e-4)],\n    sparsityParams = SimpleSparsityParameters([\"Wavelet\", \"nothing\"])\n  ),\n  rho = 1e-1\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.LeastSquaresSolverParameter"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.LeastSquaresSolverParameter-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{SL}, Tuple{AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm, AbstractVector, Any}, Tuple{AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm, AbstractVector, Any, Any}} where {SL<:AbstractLinearSolver, R, T}","page":"MRIReco","text":"(params::LeastSquaresSolverParameter)(algo, b::AbstractVector, A, AHA=normalOperator(A))\n\nSolve an inverse problem using the configured solver.\n\nArguments\n\nalgo::AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm - The reconstruction algorithm\nb::AbstractVector - k-space data\nA - Forward operator\nAHA - Normal operator (optional, computed from A if not provided)\n\nReturns\n\nReconstructed image vector\n\nNote\n\nGets the reconstruction size and the storage type from the MRIRecoContext of the reconstruction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.LeastSquaresSolverParameter"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.LeastSquaresSolverParameter-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{SL}, Tuple{AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm, Any}, Tuple{AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm, Any, Any}} where {SL<:AbstractLinearSolver, R, T}","page":"MRIReco","text":"(params::LeastSquaresSolverParameter)(algo, A, AHA=normalOperator(A))\n\nCreate a configured solver for the given problem.\n\nArguments\n\nalgo::AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm - The reconstruction algorithm\nA - Forward operator\nAHA - Normal operator (optional, computed from A if not provided)\n\nReturns\n\nA configured AbstractLinearSolver ready for use with solve!\n\nNote\n\nGets the reconstruction size and the storage type from the MRIRecoContext of the reconstruction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.LeastSquaresSolverParameter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.RegularizationParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"RegularizationParameters{R, S} <: AbstractImageReconstructionParameters\n\nParameters specifying regularization terms and their sparsity transforms.\n\nType Parameters\n\nR - Regularization type (nothing, AbstractRegularization, or Vector)\nS - Sparsity parameters type\n\nFields\n\nreg::R - Regularization term(s). Can be nothing, a single term, or a vector (default: nothing). nothing            stands for L1Regularization(0.0), which does not regularize and which CGNR does not accept.\nsparsityParams::S - Sparsifying transforms of the terms, see SimpleSparsityParameters and                       CustomSparsityParameters (default: SimpleSparsityParameters()).\n\nExamples\n\n# L1 with wavelet\nRegularizationParameters(reg = L1Regularization(1e-3),\n                         sparsityParams = SimpleSparsityParameters(\"Wavelet\"))\n\n# Multiple terms\nRegularizationParameters(reg = [L1Regularization(1e-3), L2Regularization(1e-4)],\n                         sparsityParams = SimpleSparsityParameters([\"Wavelet\", \"nothing\"]))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.RegularizationParameters"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.RegularizationParameters-Union{Tuple{SL}, Tuple{AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm, Type{SL}}} where SL<:AbstractLinearSolver","page":"MRIReco","text":"(params::RegularizationParameters)(algo, solver::Type{<:AbstractLinearSolver})\n\nTransform the regularization parameters for a specific solver type. ADMM and SplitBregman take several terms; FISTA, POGM and OptISTA take one term; CGNR takes one L2Regularization and ignores its transform. FISTA, POGM, OptISTA and CGNR also take the projections PositiveRegularization and RealRegularization.\n\nArguments\n\nalgo::AbstractIterativeMRIRecoAlgorithm - The reconstruction algorithm\nsolver::Type{<:AbstractLinearSolver} - The solver type (for example ADMM or FISTA)\n\nReturns\n\nFor ADMM and SplitBregman, which take the transforms separately: the tuple (reg, regTrafo) with one transform per term, the identity of one image for a term without a transform\nFor the other solvers: the vector of terms, each term with a transform as a TransformedRegularization\n\nNote\n\nGets the reconstruction size and the storage type from the MRIRecoContext of the reconstruction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.RegularizationParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.SimpleSparsityParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"SimpleSparsityParameters{S} <: AbstractSparsityParameters\n\nParameters specifying sparsifying transforms by name, see SparseOp.\n\nFields\n\nsparseTrafo::S - The transform of the first regularization term (\"Wavelet\", or \"nothing\" for the                    identity) or a vector with one entry per term. With \"\" (the default) or nothing, no term                    has a transform; in a vector, nothing or a missing entry leaves that term without one. A term                    without a transform applies to the image itself.\n\nExamples\n\nSimpleSparsityParameters(\"Wavelet\")               # Wavelet for the first term\nSimpleSparsityParameters([\"Wavelet\", \"nothing\"])  # one transform per term\nSimpleSparsityParameters()                        # no transform\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.SimpleSparsityParameters"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.SimpleSparsityParameters-Tuple{Type{<:AbstractMRIRecoAlgorithm}, Int64}","page":"MRIReco","text":"(sparsity::SimpleSparsityParameters)(algo, numTerms::Int64) -> Vector\n\nBuild the transforms of numTerms regularization terms: a SparseOp for each name and nothing for each term without a transform. Reconstructions that solve for all contrasts or basis functions at once repeat each transform for every contrast or basis function.\n\nNote\n\nGets the reconstruction size and the storage type from the MRIRecoContext of the reconstruction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.SimpleSparsityParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.SolverParameters","page":"MRIReco","text":"SolverParameters <: AbstractMRIRecoParameters\n\nAbstract base type for solver parameters in MRIReco. Subtypes define how linear solvers are configured for iterative reconstruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.SolverParameters"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.reconstruction_simple","page":"MRIReco","text":"Performs iterative image reconstruction independently for the data of all coils, contrasts and slices\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData            - AcquisitionData object\nreconSize::NTuple{2,Int64}              - size of image to reconstruct\nreg::Vector{<:AbstractRegularization}                 - Regularization to be used\nsparseTrafo::AbstractLinearOperator - sparsifying transformation\nweights::Vector{Vector{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}} - sampling density of the trajectories in acqData\nsolver::Type{<:AbstractLinearSolver}                  - name of the solver to use\narrayType             - array type to use for the reconstruction, defaults to Array (CPU execution)\nscheduler             - (OhMyThreads) scheduler to use during reconstruction, defaults to dynamic/parallel on CPU, sequential/serial on GPU\n(normalize::Bool=false)             - adjust regularization parameter according to the size of k-space data\n(params::Dict{Symbol,Any})          - Dict with additional parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.reconstruction_simple"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.reconstruction_multiEcho","page":"MRIReco","text":"Performs a iterative image reconstruction jointly for all contrasts. Different slices and coil images are reconstructed independently.\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData            - AcquisitionData object\nreconSize::NTuple{2,Int64}              - size of image to reconstruct\nreg::Vector{<:AbstractRegularization}                 - Regularization to be used\nsparseTrafo::AbstractLinearOperator - sparsifying transformation\nweights::Vector{Vector{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}} - sampling density of the trajectories in acqData\nsolver::Type{<:AbstractLinearSolver}                  - name of the solver to use\narrayType             - array type to use for the reconstruction, defaults to Array (CPU execution)\nscheduler             - (OhMyThreads) scheduler to use during reconstruction, defaults to dynamic/parallel on CPU, sequential/serial on GPU\n(normalize::Bool=false)             - adjust regularization parameter according to the size of k-space data\n(params::Dict{Symbol,Any})          - Dict with additional parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.reconstruction_multiEcho"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.reconstruction_multiCoil","page":"MRIReco","text":"Performs a SENSE-type iterative image reconstruction. Different slices and contrasts images are reconstructed independently.\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData            - AcquisitionData object\nreconSize::NTuple{2,Int64}              - size of image to reconstruct\nreg::Vector{<:AbstractRegularization}                 - Regularization to be used\nsparseTrafo::AbstractLinearOperator - sparsifying transformation\nweights::Vector{Vector{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}} - sampling density of the trajectories in acqData\nL_inv::Array{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}        - noise decorrelation matrix\nsolver::Type{<:AbstractLinearSolver}                  - name of the solver to use\nsenseMaps::AbstractArray{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}        - coil sensitivities\narrayType             - array type to use for the reconstruction, defaults to Array (CPU execution)\nscheduler             - (OhMyThreads) scheduler to use during reconstruction, defaults to dynamic/parallel on CPU, sequential/serial on GPU\n(normalize::Bool=false)             - adjust regularization parameter according to the size of k-space data\n(params::Dict{Symbol,Any})          - Dict with additional parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.reconstruction_multiCoil"},{"category":"function","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.reconstruction_multiCoilMultiEcho","page":"MRIReco","text":"Performs a SENSE-type iterative image reconstruction which reconstructs all contrasts jointly. Different slices are reconstructed independently.\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData            - AcquisitionData object\nreconSize::NTuple{2,Int64}              - size of image to reconstruct\nreg::Vector{<:AbstractRegularization}                 - Regularization to be used\nsparseTrafo::AbstractLinearOperator - sparsifying transformation\nweights::Vector{Vector{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}} - sampling density of the trajectories in acqData\nsolver::Type{<:AbstractLinearSolver}                  - name of the solver to use\nsenseMaps::AbstractArray{Complex{<:AbstractFloat}}        - coil sensitivities\narrayType             - array type to use for the reconstruction, defaults to Array (CPU execution)\nscheduler             - (OhMyThreads) scheduler to use during reconstruction, defaults to dynamic/parallel on CPU, sequential/serial on GPU\n(normalize::Bool=false)             - adjust regularization parameter according to the size of k-space data\n(params::Dict{Symbol,Any})          - Dict with additional parameters\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.reconstruction_multiCoilMultiEcho"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.remove_oversampling-Tuple{Any}","page":"MRIReco","text":"raw = remove_oversampling(rawData::RawAcquisitionData)\nremove_oversampling!(rawData::RawAcquisitionData)\n\nRemoves oversampling from the profiles in a RawAcquisitionData object by creating a deep copy of the input data and applying the remove_oversampling! function.\n\nArguments\n\nrawData::RawAcquisitionData: The RawAcquisitionData object to process.\n\nReturns\n\nRawAcquisitionData: A new RawAcquisitionData object with oversampling removed.\n\nExamples\n\n# Assuming `rawData` is a RawAcquisitionData object\nprocessedData = remove_oversampling(rawData)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.remove_oversampling"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.regrid-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, 2}, Tuple{Int64, Int64}}} where T","page":"MRIReco","text":"`regrid(acqData::AcquisitionData{T}, kspaceSize::NTuple{2,Int64}\n          ; cgnr_iter::Int64=3, correctionMap::Array{Complex{T}}=Complex{T}[]) where (D,T)`\n\nRegrid non-cartesian k-space data in acqData to a cartesian grid of size kspaceSize. Uses the CGNR method to invert the non-cartesian Fourier encoding.\n\nArguments:\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData{T}        - AcquisitionData\nksize::NTuple{2,Int64}          - size of the cartesian k-space grid\ncgnr_iter::Int64=3              - number of CGNR iterations\ncorrectionMao::Array{Complex{T}}- relaxation/b0 map\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.regrid"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.applySubspace-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 6}, AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 2}}} where T","page":"MRIReco","text":"applySubspace(I, basis)\n\ncomputes the echo images from the coefficient maps I of the reconstruction type \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\", by multiplying the dimension of the coefficient maps with basis'.\n\nI has the dimensions x, y, z, coefficient maps, coils and repetitions. basis is the matrix passed as :basis, with one row per echo and one column per coefficient map, and has the element type of I. The result has the dimensions of I, with the echoes instead of the coefficient maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.applySubspace"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.nrmsd-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"MRIReco","text":"nrmsd(I,Ireco)\n\ncomputes the normalized root mean squared error of the image Ireco with respect to the image I.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.nrmsd"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.optimalScalingFactor-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"MRIReco","text":"optimalScalingFactor(I,Ireco)\n\ncomputes the optimal scaling factor α such that || I - α Ireco ||₂ is minimal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.optimalScalingFactor"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.covariance-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 2}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"MRIReco","text":"covariance(noiseData)\n\ncomputes the covariance matrix of the coils from the noise samples noiseData, an array of size (samples, coils), as noiseData' * noiseData / (N - 1) for N samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIReco/#MRIReco.decorrelateSenseMaps-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{Nothing, LinearAlgebra.LowerTriangular{Complex{T}, <:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 2}}}, AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, Int64}} where T","page":"MRIReco","text":"decorrelateSenseMaps(L_inv, senseMaps, numChan)\n\nmultiplies the coil sensitivities senseMaps, an array of size (x, y, z, coils) with numChan coils, by L_inv' from the right along the coil dimension, reshape(senseMaps, :, numChan) * L_inv', which decorrelates them in the same way as the data. L_inv is the inverse of the lower Cholesky factor of the matrix that covariance returns. If L_inv is nothing, senseMaps is returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIReco.decorrelateSenseMaps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/encoding_operators/#encoding-operators","page":"Use Encoding Operators Directly","text":"reconstruction builds the encoding operator of an acquisition and solves the reconstruction problem with it. To change one of these steps, for example to add an operator or to follow the iterations of the solver, you can build the operator with MRIOperators yourself and solve the problem with RegularizedLeastSquares.jl, which MRIReco re-exports. Signal Model and Encoding Operators explains the operators.\n\nThe example is the CG-SENSE reconstruction of Multi-Coil Reconstruction: the knee data of the documentation, undersampled by a factor of 4, with the coil sensitivities estimated by ESPIRiT.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling, MRICoilSensitivities, CairoMakie\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nacqData = AcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")))\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30, seed = 1234)\nsensitivities = espirit(acqDataSub, (6, 6), 30, eigThresh_2 = 0)","title":"Use Encoding Operators Directly"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/encoding_operators/#Building-the-Encoding-Operator","page":"Use Encoding Operators Directly","text":"encodingOps_parallel returns the encoding operators for data with coil sensitivities, one for each contrast of a slice, which the keyword slice selects, by default the first one. The knee data has one contrast and one slice:\n\nshape = (320, 320)\nE = encodingOps_parallel(acqDataSub, shape, sensitivities)[1]\nsize(E)\n(204800, 102400)\n\nThe operator maps an image with 320 × 320 voxels, stored as a vector, to the measured samples of all 8 coils. The data needs the same layout, a vector with the samples of the first coil, then those of the second coil, and so on. multiCoilData returns it for one contrast and slice:\n\nb = multiCoilData(acqDataSub, 1, 1)\nlength(b) == size(E, 1)\ntrue\n\nLike a matrix, the operator is applied with *, and adjoint(E) or E' applies its adjoint. Applied to the data, the adjoint sums the zero-filled images of the coils, weighted with the conjugate sensitivities. Since profiles are missing, the result, on the left in the figure below, shows the artifacts of the undersampling:\n\nimageAdjoint = reshape(adjoint(E) * b, shape)","title":"Building the Encoding Operator"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/encoding_operators/#Solving-the-Reconstruction-Problem","page":"Use Encoding Operators Directly","text":"createLinearSolver creates a solver of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl for an operator, and solve! runs it on the data. reconstruction with \"multiCoil\" does two more things. It weights the operator and the data with a diagonal operator, WeightingOp from LinearOperatorCollection.jl, which MRIOperators re-exports. Its diagonal is what samplingDensity returns, the square roots of the density compensation factors, see Density Compensation. For Cartesian data, they are constant, but they set the scale of the problem and with it the effect of the regularization. And it gives the solver the normal operator from normalOperator, which can be faster than applying the operator and then its adjoint, see The Normal Operator and the Toeplitz Approach. With both, CGNR with a small ell_2 regularization, scaled with the magnitude of the data, and 10 iterations gives the CG-SENSE reconstruction of the tutorial. A callback stores, in each iteration, the norm of the residual of the normal equations that CGNR solves:\n\nnumChan = numChannels(acqDataSub)\nweights = samplingDensity(acqDataSub, shape)[1]\nW = WeightingOp(eltype(weights); weights, rep = numChan)\nWE = W ∘ E\nsolver = createLinearSolver(CGNR, WE; AHA = normalOperator(WE), reg = L2Regularization(1e-3),\n                            normalizeReg = MeasurementBasedNormalization(), iterations = 10)\nconvergence = StoreConvergenceCallback()\nimageCGSENSE = reshape(solve!(solver, W * b; callbacks = convergence), shape)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (620, 330))\nfor (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip((imageAdjoint, imageCGSENSE), (\"Adjoint\", \"CG-SENSE\")))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; title, aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(image)'; colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe callback holds this norm before the first and after each of the 10 iterations:\n\nround.(convergence.convMeas[:residual], sigdigits = 2)\n11-element Vector{Float32}:\n 390000.0\n  14000.0\n   4500.0\n   1700.0\n    800.0\n    330.0\n    160.0\n     78.0\n     31.0\n     13.0\n      5.7\n\nThe result equals that of reconstruction with the same parameters:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}(:reco => \"multiCoil\", :reconSize => shape, :senseMaps => sensitivities,\n                           :solver => CGNR, :reg => L2Regularization(1e-3),\n                           :normalizeReg => MeasurementBasedNormalization(), :iterations => 10)\nvec(imageCGSENSE) ≈ vec(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params))\ntrue\n\nWith noise samples in :noiseData, reconstruction also decorrelates the coils, see Noise Decorrelation, and for trajectories with circular k-space coverage (isCircular), such as radial and spiral ones, it sets the image outside a circle to zero with circularShutter!.","title":"Solving the Reconstruction Problem"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/encoding_operators/#Building-the-Operator-from-Its-Parts","page":"Use Encoding Operators Directly","text":"The operator of encodingOps_parallel is composed of simpler operators. fourierEncodingOp creates the Fourier operator of one coil. With \"fast\", it is an FFT for Cartesian trajectories, here followed by a sampling operator that keeps the samples in subsampleIdx, and an NFFT for non-Cartesian ones; \"explicit\" evaluates the Fourier sum directly. SensitivityOp multiplies the image by the sensitivities of all coils, here those of the first slice, and DiagOp applies a copy of the Fourier operator to the image of each coil:\n\nF = fourierEncodingOp(shape, trajectory(acqDataSub, 1), \"fast\";\n                      subsampleIdx = acqDataSub.subsampleIndices[1])\nS = SensitivityOp(sensitivities[:, :, 1:1, :])\nE2 = DiagOp(F, numChan) ∘ S\nx = randn(ComplexF32, prod(shape))\nE2 * x ≈ E * x\ntrue\n\nBuilt in this way, each part can be replaced or extended. The Fourier operator has to be repeated for each coil, and the data has to be one vector (discussion #225): with only one block, DiagOp(F) ∘ S is still created, but applying it fails with LinearOperatorException(\"shape mismatch\"), and solve! takes the columns of a matrix of samples × coils as separate data vectors, which don't fit the operator.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Building the Operator from Its Parts"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#prepare-profiles","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"Raw data from a scanner often contains readouts that are not part of the image, such as noise measurements, and readouts that are oversampled, that is, sampled densely enough for a larger field of view than the image needs. This guide shows how to find the special readouts through their flags and counters and separate them, and how to remove the readout oversampling before the conversion to AcquisitionData. Data Model explains the two data types.\n\nThe data is the first repetition of simple_gre.h5, the gradient-echo example dataset of the ISMRMRD project, a phantom measured with 32 coils on a 1.5 T scanner. It belongs to the documentation data, which is downloaded as in Direct Reconstruction. Besides MRIReco, the guide needs MRIFiles to read the file and CairoMakie for the plots.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, CairoMakie, LinearAlgebra\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"simple_gre_rep0.h5\")))\nlength(rawData.profiles)\n129","title":"Select and Prepare Profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#Looking-at-Flags-and-Counters","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"The header of each profile holds flags, which describe the readout, for example as a noise measurement or as the first readout of a slice, and counters, which place it in the acquisition. flags_of lists the flags that are set. For the first two profiles of the file:\n\nflags_of(rawData.profiles[1]), flags_of(rawData.profiles[2])\n([\"ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT\"], [\"ACQ_FIRST_IN_SLICE\"])\n\nThe first profile is a noise measurement, and the second one is the first k-space line of the image. Both have the same counters, for example the phase encoding step and the repetition, but the noise was sampled with a different dwell time in µs:\n\nh1, h2 = rawData.profiles[1].head, rawData.profiles[2].head\n(Int(h1.idx.kspace_encode_step_1), Int(h1.idx.repetition), h1.sample_time_us),\n(Int(h2.idx.kspace_encode_step_1), Int(h2.idx.repetition), h2.sample_time_us)\n((0, 0, 30.0f0), (0, 0, 7.8f0))\n\nThe flags have the names of the ISMRMRD format without its prefix ISMRMRD_, and FLAGS holds all names that MRIBase knows. flag_is_set checks one of them, for example to count the noise measurements:\n\ncount(p -> flag_is_set(p, \"ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT\"), rawData.profiles)\n1\n\nWhen a converter did not mark a special readout or marked it wrongly, flag_set! and flag_remove! change the flags of a profile. The flags are also exported as UInt64 bit masks with the same names, for example ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT, for working with head.flags directly.","title":"Looking at Flags and Counters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#Separating-the-Noise-Measurement","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"filter_raw_by_flags keeps the profiles that have a flag, here the one noise readout. Stacking the samples of all noise readouts gives the noise data, a matrix of samples × coils:\n\nnoise = filter_raw_by_flags(rawData, \"ACQ_IS_NOISE_MEASUREMENT\")\nnoiseData = reduce(vcat, [p.data for p in noise.profiles])\nsize(noiseData)\n(256, 32)\n\nWith a list of flags, filter_raw_by_flags keeps the profiles that have all of them. Profiles with any of the flags in a list flags can be selected with filter(p -> any(f -> flag_is_set(p, f), flags), rawData.profiles) and put into RawAcquisitionData(rawData.params, profiles).\n\nThe noise of different coils can be correlated. covariance estimates the covariance matrix from the samples, and the magnitudes of the correlation coefficients between different coils show how strongly. Here, some pairs of coils reach 0.2 to 0.3; with only 256 samples, the smaller values are largely estimation noise:\n\nC = covariance(noiseData)\ns = sqrt.(real.(diag(C)))\nR = abs.(C) ./ (s * s')\nR[diagind(R)] .= NaN\nfig = Figure(size = (420, 340))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true,\n          xlabel = \"coil\", ylabel = \"coil\", title = \"Correlation of the coil noise\")\nhm = heatmap!(ax, R; colorrange = (0, 0.3), nan_color = :white)\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe longer dwell time of the noise readout lowers its noise variance per sample compared with the image data, but does not change the correlation between the coils. The reconstruction types \"multiCoil\", \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and \"multiCoilMultiEchoSubspace\" use these samples to decorrelate the coils when they are passed as params[:noiseData], see Noise Decorrelation.","title":"Separating the Noise Measurement"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#Removing-Profiles-That-Are-Not-Image-Data","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"The conversion to AcquisitionData does not skip special readouts. It keeps the first readout of each phase encoding step, which here is the noise instead of the first k-space line of the image. The noise is also placed by its center sample, 0 instead of 128, so it fills half of each of the first two k-space lines:\n\nn = size(noiseData, 1)\nAcquisitionData(rawData).kdata[1][1:n, :] == noiseData\ntrue\n\nremove_raw_by_flags removes the profiles that have any of the given flags. By default, these are noise measurements, parallel calibration, navigator, phase correction and phase stabilization readouts, and dummy scans:\n\nrawImage = remove_raw_by_flags(rawData)\nlength(rawImage.profiles)\n128\n\nProfiles without any of these flags are kept, for example readouts marked only as ACQ_IS_HPFEEDBACK_DATA. A list of flags passed as second argument replaces the default list.\n\nThe readouts marked ACQ_IS_PARALLEL_CALIBRATION hold calibration data for parallel imaging, for example to estimate coil sensitivities, and are removed by default. Readouts that are calibration and image data at once carry ACQ_IS_PARALLEL_CALIBRATION_AND_IMAGING instead, at least from the converter siemens_to_ismrmrd, and are kept. To get all calibration readouts, select the profiles with either flag before the removal, as shown above, see #237.","title":"Removing Profiles That Are Not Image Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#Removing-the-Readout-Oversampling","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"The header gives a field of view of 600 mm in the readout direction for the encoding, twice the 300 mm of the image:\n\nrawImage.params[\"encodedFOV\"], rawImage.params[\"reconFOV\"]\n([600.0, 300.0, 5.0], [300.0, 300.0, 5.0])\n\nremove_oversampling transforms each readout into image space, keeps the central part that covers the field of view of the image and transforms it back. This saves memory and computation time, and here it halves the number of samples:\n\nrawCropped = remove_oversampling(rawImage)\nsize(rawImage.profiles[1].data, 1), size(rawCropped.profiles[1].data, 1)\n(256, 128)\n\nIt takes the number of samples from the first profile and applies the same window to all profiles, so profiles of another length have to be removed before. Header values that are not a multiple of the ratio, for example an odd center_sample with an oversampling factor of 2, make it fail, see #381. Oversampling in the phase encoding directions, which this data does not have, stays and has to be cropped from the reconstructed image.\n\nremove_oversampling adapts encodedSize but not encodedFOV, which AcquisitionData uses as the field of view and from which the reconstruction computes the pixel spacing. Set its readout entry to the field of view of the image (see #239):\n\nrawCropped.params[\"encodedFOV\"][1] = rawCropped.params[\"reconFOV\"][1]","title":"Removing the Readout Oversampling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#Converting-and-Reconstructing","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"Both versions can now be converted and reconstructed. The direct reconstruction gives one image per coil, which we combine by the root sum of squares:\n\nacqData = AcquisitionData(rawImage)\nacqDataCropped = AcquisitionData(rawCropped)\nrss(img) = sqrt.(sum(abs2, img[:, :, 1, 1, :, 1]; dims = 3)[:, :, 1])\nparamsFull = Dict{Symbol, Any}(:reco => \"direct\", :reconSize => (256, 128))\nparamsCropped = Dict{Symbol, Any}(:reco => \"direct\", :reconSize => (128, 128))\nimageFull = rss(reconstruction(acqData, paramsFull))\nimageCropped = rss(reconstruction(acqDataCropped, paramsCropped))\nacqData.fov, acqDataCropped.fov\n((600.0, 300.0, 5.0), (300.0, 300.0, 5.0))\n\nThe readout direction is vertical in the figure, and both images are drawn with the same pixel size. With oversampling, the image covers twice the field of view in this direction, and the cropped image is its central half:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 600))\naxFull = Axis(fig[1, 1]; aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true,\n              title = \"With oversampling\")\nheatmap!(axFull, imageFull'; colormap = :grays)\ncanvas = fill(NaN, 256, 128)\ncanvas[65:192, :] = imageCropped\naxCropped = Axis(fig[1, 2]; aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true, title = \"Cropped\")\nheatmap!(axCropped, canvas'; colormap = :grays)\nhidedecorations!.((axFull, axCropped))\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Converting and Reconstructing"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/prepare_profiles/#Selecting-Profiles-When-Reading","page":"Select and Prepare Profiles","text":"When MRIFiles reads an ISMRMRD file, it can also select profiles by their counters: the keywords slice, repetition and contrast of RawAcquisitionData(::ISMRMRDFile) keep the profiles whose counters have the given value or one of the given values, counted from 0. The file here was made in this way, with repetition = 0, from simple_gre.h5, which holds ten repetitions. Data without the first slice or repetition is not converted correctly yet, see #382.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Selecting Profiles When Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"#MRIReco.jl","page":"Home","text":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging Reconstruction in Julia\n\nMRIReco.jl is a framework for MRI image reconstruction written in Julia. It provides data types for raw data and for k-space data prepared for reconstruction, encoding operators, and direct as well as iterative reconstruction methods, including parallel imaging and compressed sensing. Further packages read and write raw data files, generate sampling patterns, estimate coil sensitivities and simulate data.","title":"MRIReco.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Packages","page":"Home","text":"MRIReco.jl consists of several packages that are developed in the same repository and registered individually. The last column lists the packages of this repository that each package depends on. MRIReco re-exports its dependencies MRIBase and MRIOperators, so their names are available after using MRIReco; the other packages are independent of MRIReco and are installed and loaded separately.\n\nPackage Purpose Depends on\nMRIReco Image reconstruction MRIBase, MRIOperators\nMRIBase Data types for raw data, acquisition data and trajectories —\nMRIOperators Encoding operators MRIBase\nMRIFiles Reading and writing raw data files MRIBase\nMRISampling Undersampling patterns MRIBase\nMRICoilSensitivities Coil sensitivity estimation and coil compression MRIBase\nMRISimulation Simulation of MRI data MRIBase, MRIOperators","title":"Packages"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-the-Documentation-Is-Organized","page":"Home","text":"Getting Started shows how to install the packages and run a first reconstruction.\nThe tutorials walk through complete reconstructions step by step.\nThe how-to guides show how to solve specific tasks, grouped by package.\nThe explanations describe the design of the packages and the concepts behind them.\nThe reference lists the API of each package.\n\nThe pages under \"Legacy Pages\" come from the previous version of this documentation and are being rewritten.","title":"How the Documentation Is Organized"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing-MRIReco.jl","page":"Home","text":"If you use MRIReco.jl in your work, please cite\n\nT. Knopp and M. Grosser, \"MRIReco.jl: An MRI reconstruction framework written in Julia\", Magnetic Resonance in Medicine 86, 1633–1646 (2021), doi:10.1002/mrm.28792.\n\nA BibTeX entry is in MRIReco.bib.","title":"Citing MRIReco.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-Help","page":"Home","text":"Questions are welcome in GitHub Discussions, and bugs can be reported as issues. To contribute changes, see the contributing guide.","title":"Getting Help"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#simulate-kspace","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"MRISimulation computes the k-space data of an image along a trajectory, optionally with coil sensitivities, off-resonance, relaxation, several echoes and noise. Since the image is known, such data are useful to test and compare reconstructions. MRIReco.jl does not depend on MRISimulation, so it is loaded with using MRISimulation.","title":"Simulate k-Space Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Simulating-from-a-Parameter-Dictionary","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"simulation takes the image and a dictionary of parameters. params[:trajName] names the trajectory, for example \"Cartesian\", the default, \"Radial\", \"Spiral\" or the 3D trajectory \"Kooshball\", and params[:numProfiles] and params[:numSamplingPerProfile] are required. Options of the trajectory, such as the number of :windings of a spiral, the readout duration :AQ and :TE, the time of the first sample of each profile, both in seconds, are passed on to trajectory. The example simulates a spiral with four interleaves and eight coils:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nN = 128\nimage = shepp_logan(N)\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:simulation] = \"fast\"\nparams[:trajName] = \"Spiral\"\nparams[:numProfiles] = 4\nparams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = 4096\nparams[:windings] = 16\nparams[:AQ] = 10e-3\nparams[:senseMaps] = birdcageSensitivity(N, 8, 1.5)\nacqData = simulation(image, params)\nsize(acqData.kdata), size(acqData.kdata[1])\n((1, 1, 1), (16384, 8))\n\nThe result is an AcquisitionData object with one block of data per contrast, slice and repetition, here a single one, which holds one column of samples per coil. A matrix is simulated as one slice, and a 3D array holds the slices of a 2D trajectory or the volume of a 3D trajectory. The element type of the image sets the precision of the data, so a Float32 image gives ComplexF32 samples.\n\nparams[:simulation] chooses how the Fourier integrals are computed: \"fast\", the default, uses FFTs or NFFTs, and \"explicit\" evaluates the sums directly. It needs no approximations but is much slower, which matters for larger images, while \"fast\", with its default options, differs from it only by the accuracy of its approximations, apart from the limits at the end.","title":"Simulating from a Parameter Dictionary"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Coil-Sensitivities","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"params[:senseMaps] holds the coil sensitivities as an array with the dimensions x, y, z and coils. Give it the element type of the data, as the simulation and the reconstruction require, so ComplexF32 for a Float32 image. birdcageSensitivity computes them for coils on a circle around the center, with the radius relative to half the image width, and measured2DSensitivity returns up to eight measured 2D coil profiles from BART as ComplexF32 arrays without normalization. The reconstruction of the data takes the same maps.","title":"Coil Sensitivities"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Noise","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"addNoise returns a copy of the data with complex Gaussian noise. The signal-to-noise ratio, given as a Float64, is the mean magnitude of the samples divided by the root mean square of the noise, computed for each block of data, with all coils together:\n\nusing Random\nRandom.seed!(1)    # the same noise in every run\nnoisyData = addNoise(acqData, 1.0)\n\nThe figure shows the trajectory and the reconstructions of both data sets, with their normalized root mean squared deviation from the phantom. Since the center of k-space dominates the mean magnitude, the image is much less noisy than the ratio suggests:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nrecoParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nrecoParams[:reco] = \"multiCoil\"\nrecoParams[:reconSize] = (N, N)\nrecoParams[:senseMaps] = params[:senseMaps]\nrecoParams[:solver] = CGNR\nrecoParams[:reg] = L2Regularization(1e-3)\nrecoParams[:iterations] = 10\nimages = [reconstruction(acqData, recoParams) => \"Without noise\",\n          reconstruction(noisyData, recoParams) => \"SNR 1\"]\n\ndeviation(x) = round(nrmsd(image, abs.(x[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])), sigdigits = 2)\nnodes = kspaceNodes(trajectory(acqData, 1))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (940, 320))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Spiral with four interleaves\", aspect = DataAspect())\nscatter!(ax, nodes[1, :], nodes[2, :], markersize = 1)\nfor (i, (x, title)) in enumerate(images)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i + 1], title = \"$title, NRMSD $(deviation(x))\", aspect = DataAspect(),\n              yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(x[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Noise"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Off-Resonance-and-Relaxation","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"params[:correctionMap] adds off-resonance and relaxation in the form of the correction map of the reconstruction, explained in Signal Model and Encoding Operators. Its imaginary part is the off-resonance frequency in radians per second, for example 1im .* quadraticFieldmap(N, N, 125 * 2pi) for frequencies between -125 Hz and 125 Hz, and the simulation applies it with the sampling times of the trajectory, which start at :TE. Its real part is the relaxation rate in 1/s, which only sets the amplitudes of the echoes described below; the dictionary interface does not apply it during the readout (#365). params[:fmap], in radians per second, and params[:r2map] set the two parts separately.","title":"Off-Resonance and Relaxation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Multi-Echo-Data","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"params[:T_echo], a Vector{Float64} of echo times in seconds, simulates a multi-echo spin-echo sequence with one contrast per echo. The extended phase graph method computes the amplitudes of the echoes from the relaxation rates params[:r2map], or the real part of the correction map, and params[:r1map], and from the flip angles params[:excitationAngle], by default π/2, and params[:refocusingAngles], a vector with one angle per echo, by default π. All contrasts share the trajectory of the parameters, so their echo time does not follow :T_echo.","title":"Multi-Echo Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Simulating-along-a-Given-Trajectory","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"simulation(tr, image, correctionMap; senseMaps) simulates along any Trajectory, for example one from trajectory or from measured data. The image and the correction map are complex arrays with the element type of the trajectory and the slices in their third dimension. Unlike the dictionary interface, this function also applies the real part of the correction map during the readout, as R_2^*. opName = \"explicit\" selects the direct evaluation.","title":"Simulating along a Given Trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/simulation/#Limits","page":"Simulate k-Space Data","text":"A Float32 image needs ComplexF32 coil sensitivities, for a single coil ones(ComplexF32, size(image, 1), size(image, 2), size(image, 3), 1), and, if given, a ComplexF32 correction map and a Float32 :fmap. For a Float32 image, a correction map or :fmap in double precision makes the simulation fail with a MethodError. Missing or double-precision coil sensitivities do so too, except with \"fast\" along a non-Cartesian trajectory without a correction map applied during the readout (#366).\nIf the part of the correction map that the simulation applies during the readout is not zero, \"fast\" with the default :method returns NaN in every slice in which this part is spatially constant, zero included, and in all slices if all samples have the same time; \"leastsquare\", \"hist\" and \"svd\" fail in some of these cases, and \"hist\" also if this part contains both relaxation and off-resonance (#360). If this part is not zero, \"fast\" with any :method also fails for 3D trajectories (#361) and, for trajectories with circular = true, such as the built-in radial and spiral ones and the non-Cartesian ones built from RawAcquisitionData, sets the object to zero outside a circle whose diameter is the longer side of the image (#372). \"explicit\" works in these cases.\nIf the simulation applies no correction map during the readout, \"fast\" places the samples of Cartesian trajectories on the k-space grid of the image by counting along each axis, not by their nodes; the reconstruction does the same without a correction map. Unless the counted positions are the nodes, the samples end up in the wrong place, or the simulation fails (#373). For \"Cartesian\" with the default :kmin and :kmax and for \"Cartesian3D\", they are the nodes only when, along each axis, the image has as many points as the trajectory or, when the trajectory has an even number of them, a whole multiple of it, and for \"EPI\", they never are (#370). \"explicit\" places the samples at their nodes.\nAlong non-Cartesian trajectories, and along Cartesian ones when a correction map is applied during the readout, \"fast\" stops with an error for nodes beyond ±0.5, for example those of the spiral above with :kmax = 0.6 (#270); \"explicit\" takes any nodes.\nIf the image has an odd number of pixels along x or y, or along z for a 3D trajectory, \"explicit\" puts its center half a pixel away from where \"fast\" and the reconstruction put it along that axis, so that the reconstruction of its data is shifted by half a pixel (#375).\nWith several echoes and several slices, the data of the echoes and slices end up in the wrong blocks (#367).\nsimulation(image, params, filename), which should store the data in a file, fails (#295).\nAfter using MRIReco, MRISimulation, the name numContrasts is ambiguous (#298); MRIBase.numContrasts(acqData) returns the number of contrasts.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/trajectory/#create-trajectory","page":"Create a Trajectory","text":"A Trajectory holds the k-space positions of the samples of one contrast, in the units explained in Data Model, together with their sampling times and their division into profiles and, in stacked 3D trajectories, partitions. Simulations and AcquisitionData need one trajectory per contrast. This guide shows how to create the built-in trajectories, how to build one from your own nodes and how to set the sampling times.","title":"Create a Trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/trajectory/#Built-in-Trajectories","page":"Create a Trajectory","text":"trajectory creates a built-in trajectory from the floating-point type of its nodes and times, the name of the trajectory, the number of profiles and the number of samples per profile. The type has to match that of the image and the k-space data, and a call without the type fails (#371). The 2D trajectories are \"Cartesian\", with one line of the grid per profile, \"EPI\", with EPI_factor lines per profile, \"Radial\", with one spoke per profile, the spirals \"Spiral\", \"SpiralVarDens\", \"SpiralDualDens\" and \"SpiralPerturbed\", with one interleave per profile, and \"OneLine\", whose profiles all lie on one line through the center. In 3D, \"Cartesian3D\" stacks numSlices partitions of Cartesian lines, \"StackOfStars\" is meant to stack radial ones, and \"Kooshball\" has spokes in all directions. \"EPI\" and \"StackOfStars\" don't work as intended yet; see the limits at the end. Keyword arguments set further options:\n\nwindings is the number of turns of each spiral interleave, and angleOffset spaces the interleaves equally, \"equispaced\", by the golden angle of 137.5°, \"golden\", or randomly, \"random\". For \"Radial\", angleOffset takes the symbols :equispaced and :random.\nIn \"SpiralVarDens\", the radius grows with the angle to the power alpha, and in \"SpiralDualDens\", the first half of the turns lies densityFactor times closer together than in \"Spiral\"; in both cases, 1.0 gives the plain spiral, and larger values sample the center more densely and the edge of k-space more sparsely.\nangle is the angle of \"OneLine\" in radians, and TE and AQ set the sampling times described below.\n\ndensityFactor and angle have to be Float64 numbers, such as 2.0 and pi / 4, and TE and AQ floating-point numbers. Keyword arguments that a trajectory doesn't use are ignored without a warning, so a misspelled winding = 8 gives the default number of turns. The example creates a spiral with four interleaves at golden-angle offsets and a variable-density spiral:\n\nusing MRIReco\n\nspiral = trajectory(Float64, \"Spiral\", 4, 512; windings = 8, angleOffset = \"golden\")\nvarDens = trajectory(Float64, \"SpiralVarDens\", 4, 512; windings = 8, alpha = 2.0)\nsize(kspaceNodes(spiral)), numProfiles(spiral), numSamplingPerProfile(spiral)\n((2, 2048), 4, 512)","title":"Built-in Trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/trajectory/#Custom-Trajectories","page":"Create a Trajectory","text":"Trajectory(nodes, numProfiles, numSamplingPerProfile) builds a trajectory named \"Custom\" from a matrix with one row per dimension and one column per sample, ordered profile by profile and, for a stack of partitions, partition by partition; the keyword numSlices gives the number of partitions, without which the default sampling times only cover the first one and the reconstruction of a Cartesian stack fails. The nodes have to lie between -0.5 and 0.5; otherwise, the NFFT stops with an error about the range of the nodes (#270). scale(tr, factor) returns a copy with the nodes divided by factor, for example to convert them from other units.\n\nWith cartesian = true, the samples should lie where \"Cartesian\" or, in 3D, \"Cartesian3D\" with the same numbers puts them, in the same order: unless a correction map is used, the reconstruction and the default simulation, \"fast\", apply FFTs, which don't use the nodes. Set circular = true for a trajectory with a circular field of view, as the built-in radial and spiral ones do; the reconstructions \"direct\", \"standard\" and \"multiCoil\" then set the image to zero outside a circle, or a sphere in 3D, whose diameter is the largest side of the image, and with a correction map, the encoding operator ignores the image outside it, so that the default simulation drops that part of the object (#372). The example builds a radial trajectory whose spokes follow each other at 111.25°, the golden angle for spokes, which repeat after 180° rather than 360°:\n\nprofiles, samples = 64, 128\ngoldenAngle = pi * (sqrt(5) - 1) / 2  # 180° divided by the golden ratio, about 111.25°\npositions = range(-0.5, step = 1 / samples, length = samples)\nnodes = zeros(2, samples, profiles)\nfor l in 1:profiles\n    θ = goldenAngle * (l - 1)\n    nodes[1, :, l] .= positions .* cos(θ)\n    nodes[2, :, l] .= positions .* sin(θ)\nend\nradial = Trajectory(reshape(nodes, 2, :), profiles, samples; circular = true)\nstring(radial), extrema(kspaceNodes(radial))\n(\"Custom\", (-0.5, 0.4995730270036413))\n\nThe figure draws each profile of the three trajectories as a line:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 320))\ntitles = [\"Spiral, golden angle\", \"SpiralVarDens\", \"Golden-angle radial\"]\nfor (i, tr) in enumerate([spiral, varDens, radial])\n    k = reshape(kspaceNodes(tr), 2, numSamplingPerProfile(tr), numProfiles(tr))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = titles[i], aspect = DataAspect(),\n              limits = (-0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5))\n    for l in 1:numProfiles(tr)\n        lines!(ax, k[1, :, l], k[2, :, l])\n    end\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Custom Trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/trajectory/#Sampling-Times","page":"Create a Trajectory","text":"Each trajectory stores the sampling times of its nodes, which readoutTimes returns, and the time TE, which echoTime returns, both in seconds. The built-in trajectories and Trajectory without times start every profile at TE and spread its samples over the readout duration AQ, so TE is the time of the first sample of each profile, and it equals the echo time only for readouts that start in the center of k-space, such as spirals. For other timings, for example to set TE to the echo time of a full Cartesian line or for an echo train whose times continue from one profile to the next, pass TE and the keyword times to Trajectory: a Vector with one time per node and the element type of the nodes; trajectory ignores times. The times only matter where off-resonance or relaxation is simulated or corrected; Signal Model and Encoding Operators explains how the reconstruction counts them from TE.","title":"Sampling Times"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/trajectory/#Limits","page":"Create a Trajectory","text":"\"StackOfStars\" returns the nodes of \"Cartesian3D\" instead of radial stacks (#369).\n\"EPI\" marks its nodes as Cartesian, although they don't lie on the grid, so that the FFTs put them at the wrong positions (#370).\nWithout a correction map, the reconstruction and the default simulation place Cartesian samples on the k-space grid of the image by counting along each axis, not by their nodes, and fail when the trajectory has more samples, profiles or partitions than the image has points along that axis. Unless the counted positions are the nodes, the samples end up in the wrong place. For \"Cartesian\" with the default kmin and kmax and for \"Cartesian3D\", they are the nodes only when, along each axis, the image has as many points as the trajectory or, when the trajectory has an even number of them, a whole multiple of it (#373).\n\"Radial\" with angleOffset = :golden, \"SpiralVarDens\" with angleOffset = \"random\" and unknown names fail with an UndefVarError (#371). The custom example above builds a golden-angle radial trajectory.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#multiecho-reconstruction","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"A multi-echo spin-echo acquisition measures the same slice at several echo times. The signal decays from echo to echo at a rate that depends on the tissue, so that the series of images reveals the relaxation time T_2 of each voxel. The acquisition takes long because it repeats the echo train for every profile, and undersampling shortens it. Since all echo images show the same anatomy, they share much of their information. This tutorial undersamples a measured multi-echo series, reconstructs it first each echo on its own and then all echoes jointly with the reconstruction type \"multiEcho\", and computes T_2 maps from the results.\n\nBesides MRIReco, the tutorial needs MRIFiles to read the data, MRISampling to undersample it and CairoMakie for the plots. The plotting function is the one from Direct Reconstruction, but it also passes keywords on to the heatmap.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, MRISampling, CairoMakie\n\nfunction showimages(images, titles; columns = length(images), kwargs...)\n    fig = Figure(size = (300 * columns, 310 * cld(length(images), columns)))\n    for (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip(images, titles))\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(i, columns)...], title = title,\n                  aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n        heatmap!(ax, image'; colormap = :grays, kwargs...)\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n    end\n    return fig\nend","title":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#The-Data","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"The data is one slice of a multi-echo spin-echo series of the kidneys, measured at 3 T and published by A. J. Daniel under the CC BY 4.0 license. It has ten echoes and 96 times 96 voxels of 3 mm in the image plane, with a slice thickness of 5 mm. The published series consists of magnitude images, not raw data. The file used here therefore holds the Fourier transform of these images as the k-space data of one coil, scaled so that the maximum of the first echo is 1. Its header contains the echo times in ms:\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(joinpath(datadir, \"kidney_MESE_slice3.h5\")))\nechoTimes = rawData.params[\"TE\"]\n10-element Vector{Float64}:\n  12.9\n  25.8\n  38.7\n  51.6\n  64.5\n  77.4\n  90.3\n 103.2\n 116.1\n 129.0\n\nEach echo is one contrast of the acquisition data:\n\nacqData = AcquisitionData(rawData)\nnumContrasts(acqData), numChannels(acqData)\n(10, 1)\n\nThe echoes are the fourth of the six dimensions of the reconstructed image, so we keep this dimension. The direct reconstruction of the fully sampled data returns the measured images, which serve as the reference for everything below. We look at the echoes 1, 5 and 10 throughout this tutorial. In a common gray scale, the tissue with a short T_2 fades first:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (96, 96)\n\nechoStack(images) = abs.(images[:, :, 1, :, 1, 1])\nreferenceImages = echoStack(reconstruction(acqData, params))\n\nshownEchoes = [1, 5, 10]\nshowechoes(images, label) = showimages([images[:, :, e] for e in shownEchoes],\n                                       [\"$label, echo $e\" for e in shownEchoes],\n                                       colorrange = (0, 0.7))\nshowechoes(referenceImages, \"Fully sampled\")\nFigure()","title":"The Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#Undersampling","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"We undersample the series retrospectively, that is, we remove samples from the fully sampled data, as Zhang et al. do. We keep a third of the profiles of each echo, chosen by Poisson-disk sampling, with the 8 profiles in the center of k-space always included. With its default rand = true, sample_kspace uses a different pattern for each contrast, so the masks of the first two echoes differ outside of the center:\n\nacqDataSub = sample_kspace(acqData, 3.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 8, seed = 1234)\n\nfunction samplingMask(data, echo)\n    mask = zeros(96, 96)\n    mask[data.subsampleIndices[echo]] .= 1\n    return mask\nend\nshowimages([samplingMask(acqDataSub, 1), samplingMask(acqDataSub, 2)],\n           [\"Mask of echo 1\", \"Mask of echo 2\"])\nFigure()\n\nWe compare all results with the fully sampled images by nrmsd. The direct reconstruction of the undersampled data is blurred and shows vertical stripes, because profiles are missing along the horizontal direction:\n\ndirectImages = echoStack(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params))\n\ndeviation(images) = round(nrmsd(referenceImages, images), sigdigits = 2)\nshowechoes(directImages, \"Direct\")\nFigure()","title":"Undersampling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#Reconstructing-Each-Echo-on-Its-Own","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction type \"standard\" handles the contrasts independently of each other. We use it with total variation regularization, whose weight λ is the first argument of TVRegularization, and the solver POGM, an accelerated proximal gradient method. For POGM, :rho is the step size. Values close to 1 would also work here, but the joint reconstruction below gives more stable results with small steps, so we set it to 0.05 and use more iterations. As in Iterative Reconstruction, we also scale λ with MeasurementBasedNormalization, which with POGM uses the back-projected data, here of each echo separately. The regularization reduces the stripes, but also smooths fine structures:\n\ntotalVariation = TVRegularization(0.01, shape = (96, 96))\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"standard\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (96, 96)\nparams[:solver] = POGM\nparams[:rho] = 0.05\nparams[:reg] = totalVariation\nparams[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparams[:iterations] = 200\nseparateImages = echoStack(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params))\nshowechoes(separateImages, \"Separate\")\nFigure()","title":"Reconstructing Each Echo on Its Own"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#Joint-Reconstruction","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction type \"multiEcho\" treats the images mathbfx_e of all echoes e as one unknown and solves\n\nundersetmathbfx_1 ldots mathbfx_N_eoperatornameargmin \nfrac12sum_e lVert mathbfF_emathbfx_e - mathbfb_e rVert_2^2\n+ mathrmR(mathbfx_1 ldots mathbfx_N_e)\n\nwhere mathbfF_e applies the Fourier transform, scaled to be unitary, and keeps the samples measured for echo e, and mathbfb_e holds these samples with the same scaling. Only a regularization mathrmR that couples the echoes makes this problem different from reconstructing them one by one with the same weight.\n\nA locally low-rank regularization, as Zhang et al. use it for parameter mapping in a similar form, is such a term. It divides the images into small blocks and forms for each block a matrix with one row per voxel and one column per echo. The decay curves of nearby voxels are similar, so that a few singular values describe this matrix well. Noise and artifacts that differ from echo to echo raise the smaller singular values. The regularization therefore penalizes the sum of the singular values in every block, with a weight set by λ. We use blocks of 8 times 8 voxels, like the final blocks of Zhang et al. As with their changing partition of the images, the random shift of the blocks in every iteration, which LLRRegularization applies by default, avoids a block pattern in the result. It also lets the result vary slightly from one reconstruction to the next, less so with small steps:\n\nparams[:reco] = \"multiEcho\"\nparams[:reg] = LLRRegularization(0.1, shape = (96, 96), blockSize = (8, 8))\njointImages = echoStack(reconstruction(acqDataSub, params))\nshowechoes(jointImages, \"Joint\")\nFigure()\n\nThe stripes are almost gone and fine structures stay sharp, and the deviation from the fully sampled images drops clearly:\n\n(direct = deviation(directImages), separate = deviation(separateImages),\n joint = deviation(jointImages))\n(direct = 0.048, separate = 0.034, joint = 0.012)","title":"Joint Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#The-Role-of-the-Sampling-Masks","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"The gain depends strongly on the sampling masks. With different masks, nearly every profile is measured in some echo. With rand = false, all echoes share one sampling mask, so that two thirds of the profiles are measured in no echo. The artifacts then have nearly the same structure in all echoes and stay in the largest singular values, so that the regularization removes much less of them:\n\nacqDataSameMask = sample_kspace(acqData, 3.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 8, seed = 1234,\n                                rand = false)\njointSameMask = echoStack(reconstruction(acqDataSameMask, params))\nshowechoes(jointSameMask, \"Joint, same mask\")\nFigure()\n\nThe separate reconstructions, in contrast, do not use differences between the masks: total variation reduces the artifacts within each image. With one mask for all echoes, they deviate less than the joint reconstruction:\n\nparams[:reco] = \"standard\"\nparams[:reg] = totalVariation\nseparateSameMask = echoStack(reconstruction(acqDataSameMask, params))\n(joint = deviation(jointSameMask), separate = deviation(separateSameMask))\n(joint = 0.042, separate = 0.035)\n\nA conventional multi-echo spin-echo sequence uses the same phase encoding for all echoes of a train, which corresponds to rand = false. Different masks require a sequence that changes the phase encoding from echo to echo, as Liu et al. do. Here, we only simulate this by removing samples.","title":"The Role of the Sampling Masks"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#Computing-T-Maps","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"Often, the echo images are only a means to obtain the T_2 map. We assume that the signal of each voxel decays exponentially with the echo time T_E from a value S_0,\n\nS(T_E) = S_0  e^-T_E  T_2\n\nReal echo trains deviate from this model, for example through stimulated echoes, but it is enough to compare reconstructions. The logarithm of the model is linear, log S(T_E) = log S_0 - T_E  T_2, and we fit it for all voxels at once by least squares. The fit can return negative values and values far above the last echo time, which are unreliable, so we limit T_2 to the range from 0 to 300 ms. The fit is also unreliable at low signal, so we keep only the voxels with more than 20 % of the maximum of the first echo and show the others as 0 ms. The titles show the mean absolute deviation from the map of the fully sampled images:\n\nfunction fitT2(images)\n    A = [ones(length(echoTimes)) -echoTimes]\n    coefficients = A \\ reshape(log.(max.(images, eps(Float32))), :, length(echoTimes))'\n    return reshape(clamp.(1 ./ coefficients[2, :], 0, 300), 96, 96)\nend\n\nreliable = referenceImages[:, :, 1] .> 0.2\nreferenceT2 = fitT2(referenceImages)\nmeanDeviation(t2) = round(sum(abs, t2[reliable] .- referenceT2[reliable]) / count(reliable),\n                          digits = 1)\n\nmaps = fitT2.([separateImages, jointImages, separateSameMask, jointSameMask])\nlabels = [\"Separate\", \"Joint\", \"Separate, same mask\", \"Joint, same mask\"]\ntitles = [\"$label, $(meanDeviation(t2)) ms\" for (label, t2) in zip(labels, maps)]\n\nfig = showimages([t2 .* reliable for t2 in [referenceT2, maps...]],\n                 [\"Fully sampled\", titles...],\n                 columns = 3, colormap = :viridis, colorrange = (0, 150))\nColorbar(fig[:, 4], colormap = :viridis, limits = (0, 150), label = \"T₂ in ms\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFrom a third of the samples, the joint reconstruction comes closest to the map of the fully sampled images, as long as the masks differ. With one mask, the separate reconstructions come closer.\n\nThese results have limits. For both iterative reconstructions, we chose λ by comparing a few values with the fully sampled images, which is not possible when the data is undersampled during the measurement. And evaluating reconstructions on magnitude images tends to give overly optimistic results (Shimron et al.), so the gain on raw data may be smaller.","title":"Computing T₂ Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/multiecho/#Next-Steps","page":"Multi-Echo Reconstruction","text":"With several coils, \"multiEcho\" reconstructs the echo images of each coil separately. Multi-Coil Multi-Echo and Subspace Reconstruction reconstructs one image per echo from all coils with \"multiCoilMultiEcho\" and exploits the same redundancy with a fixed temporal basis.\nThe RegularizedLeastSquares.jl documentation describes the available solvers and regularization terms.\nThe API reference describes reconstruction and sample_kspace.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/direct/#direct-reconstruction","page":"Direct Reconstruction","text":"This tutorial reconstructs an image from fully sampled Cartesian k-space data that was acquired with eight receive coils. It reads the data from a file, reconstructs one image per coil and combines the coil images into a single image.\n\nThe data is one slice of a knee dataset from mridata.org, stored in the ISMRMRD format. It belongs to the documentation data of MRIReco.jl, which ensure_artifact_installed downloads from Zenodo on first use and keeps as a Julia artifact. Besides MRIReco, the tutorial needs MRIFiles to read the file and CairoMakie for the plots.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles, CairoMakie\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfilename = joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")","title":"Direct Reconstruction"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/direct/#Raw-Data-and-Acquisition-Data","page":"Direct Reconstruction","text":"MRIReco uses two data types for measured data, both defined in MRIBase. A RawAcquisitionData object holds the data as it is stored in the file: the parameters from the file header and a list of profiles, each with the samples of one readout from all coils.\n\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(filename))\nlength(rawData.profiles), size(rawData.profiles[1].data)\n(320, (320, 8))\n\nThe file contains 320 profiles, each with 320 samples from 8 coils. For the reconstruction, the profiles are converted into an AcquisitionData object. It sorts the k-space data by contrast, slice and repetition and stores the sampling trajectory:\n\nacqData = AcquisitionData(rawData)\nencodingSize(acqData), numChannels(acqData)\n((320, 320), 8)","title":"Raw Data and Acquisition Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/direct/#Reconstructing-the-Coil-Images","page":"Direct Reconstruction","text":"The reconstruction parameters are passed in a dictionary. params[:reco] = \"direct\" selects a direct reconstruction, which for Cartesian data is an inverse fast Fourier transform. For non-Cartesian data, it applies density compensation and the adjoint of a nonuniform fast Fourier transform instead, as in Getting Started.\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\ncoilImages = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nsize(coilImages)\n(320, 320, 1, 1, 8, 1)\n\nThe reconstruction returns one image per coil. The coils are the fifth of the six dimensions: x, y, z, echoes, coils and repetitions. We plot the images with the following function:\n\nfunction showimages(images, titles; columns = length(images))\n    fig = Figure(size = (300 * columns, 310 * cld(length(images), columns)))\n    for (i, (image, title)) in enumerate(zip(images, titles))\n        ax = Axis(fig[fldmod1(i, columns)...], title = title,\n                  aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n        heatmap!(ax, image', colormap = :grays)\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n    end\n    return fig\nend\n\nshowimages([abs.(coilImages[:, :, 1, 1, c, 1]) for c in 1:8],\n           [\"Coil $c\" for c in 1:8], columns = 4)\nFigure()\n\nEach coil is most sensitive to the tissue close to it, so the coil images differ in brightness. In this slice, coil 6 contributes mostly noise.","title":"Reconstructing the Coil Images"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/direct/#Combining-the-Coil-Images","page":"Direct Reconstruction","text":"A common way to combine the coil images is the root sum of squares\n\nI(mathbfr) = sqrtsum_c I_c(mathbfr)^2\n\nwhere I_c is the image of coil c. It does not need the coil sensitivities.\n\ncombinedImage = sqrt.(sum(abs2, coilImages, dims = 5))\nshowimages([combinedImage[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1]], [\"Root sum of squares\"])\nFigure()","title":"Combining the Coil Images"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/tutorials/direct/#Next-Steps","page":"Direct Reconstruction","text":"Iterative Reconstruction shows how to reconstruct undersampled data.\nMulti-Coil Reconstruction uses the coil sensitivities to reconstruct a single image from the undersampled data of all coils.\nThe API reference describes reconstruction and the functions for reading files in MRIFiles.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Next Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/from_arrays/#acqdata-from-arrays","page":"Create AcquisitionData from Arrays","text":"Data that does not come from a file that MRIFiles reads, such as k-space data exported from other software or the samples of a sequence whose trajectory is stored separately, can be passed to MRIReco.jl as arrays. This guide shows how to create AcquisitionData from a Cartesian k-space array and from the k-space positions and samples of a non-Cartesian acquisition. Data Model explains the fields of this type.","title":"Create AcquisitionData from Arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/from_arrays/#Cartesian-k-Space-Arrays","page":"Create AcquisitionData from Arrays","text":"AcquisitionData accepts a six-dimensional Array of complex floating-point numbers with the dimensions x, y, z or slice, coil, contrast and repetition, where x is the readout direction. This is the layout that kDataCart returns, as shown in Extract Cartesian k-Space, and permutedims brings an array with another order of dimensions into it. The array has to follow the convention of the centered Fourier transform, fftshift(fft(ifftshift(image))) for a single 2D image, which places the center of k-space at index N ÷ 2 + 1 of a dimension of length N. The transform runs along x and y and, for 3D data, along z. The example computes the k-space of a phantom in this way and adds the four dimensions that a single 2D image lacks:\n\nusing MRIReco, FFTW\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nN = 128\nphantom = shepp_logan(N)\nkspace = fftshift(fft(ifftshift(ComplexF32.(phantom))))\n\nacqData = AcquisitionData(reshape(kspace, N, N, 1, 1, 1, 1))\ntypeof(acqData)\nAcquisitionData{Float32, 2}\n\nThe data has the precision of the array, here single precision. A direct reconstruction returns the phantom:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nimage = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nimage[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1] ≈ phantom\ntrue\n\nThe constructor interprets the array as follows:\n\nBy default, the third dimension is the second phase encoding direction of a 3D acquisition. If it holds the slices of a 2D acquisition, pass enc2D = true. An array whose third dimension has the length 1 is always taken as 2D data.\nUndersampled data is passed with zeros at the positions that were not measured. The constructor looks for them in the first coil and the first repetition of each contrast and, for 2D data, in the first slice. Where this part of the array is exactly zero, it leaves out the samples of all coils, slices and repetitions. The sampling pattern can therefore differ between contrasts, but not between slices or repetitions, and a measured sample that happens to be exactly zero there is left out as well (#348).\nThe array does not contain the field of view, so acqData.fov is zero, and so are the coordinates of the spatial axes of the image. If they are needed, set the field of view in millimeters, for example acqData.fov = (220.0, 220.0, 5.0). The image axes use the third entry as the distance between the slices of 2D data and as the extent of a 3D volume.\nThe array does not contain the sampling times either, so every sample gets the time zero. A reconstruction with a correction map needs the times of the acquisition, which can be set as described in Correct Off-Resonance.","title":"Cartesian k-Space Arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/from_arrays/#Non-Cartesian-Nodes-and-Samples","page":"Create AcquisitionData from Arrays","text":"For a non-Cartesian acquisition, AcquisitionData is created from the k-space positions of the samples, called nodes, and from the samples:\n\nnodes is a Matrix with one row per dimension and one column per sample; permutedims converts a matrix with one row per sample. 2D data needs exactly two rows, so remove a third row of zeros. The positions are given in units of the inverse pixel size of the image grid and have to lie in the interval [-0.5, 0.5]; values outside make the reconstruction fail. To get these units, divide positions in cycles per field of view by the matrix size along that dimension, or multiply positions in cycles per meter by the pixel size in meters. Positions in radians per pixel are divided by 2π. The first row belongs to the first dimension of the image, and the sign of the positions follows the signal model in Signal Model and Encoding Operators.\nThe samples of one contrast, slice and repetition form a matrix with one row per sample, in the order of the columns of nodes, and one column per coil. An array with the dimensions sample, profile and coil becomes such a matrix with reshape(data, :, size(data, 3)), and a vector with the samples of a single coil with reshape(samples, :, 1).\nThe nodes and the samples need the same precision, for example Float32 nodes for ComplexF32 samples. Otherwise the constructor fails with a MethodError.\n\nThe example takes both arrays from a simulated radial acquisition with 200 profiles of 128 samples:\n\nusing MRISimulation\n\nsimParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nsimParams[:simulation] = \"fast\"\nsimParams[:trajName] = \"Radial\"\nsimParams[:numProfiles] = 200\nsimParams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = N\nsimData = simulation(phantom, simParams)\n\nnodes = kspaceNodes(trajectory(simData, 1))\nsamples = simData.kdata[1]\nsize(nodes), size(samples)\n((2, 25600), (25600, 1))\n\nA Trajectory holds the nodes together with the number of profiles and the number of samples per profile. Their product has to be the number of nodes, and nodes that are not organized in profiles can be passed as a single profile. circular = true marks a trajectory that covers a circular region of k-space, like the predefined non-Cartesian trajectories of MRIBase. Several reconstruction types, among them \"direct\", \"standard\" and \"multiCoil\", then set the corners of the image to zero. The default is false. The k-space data is passed as a three-dimensional array of matrices with the dimensions contrast, slice and repetition, which has a single entry here:\n\ntr = Trajectory(nodes, 200, N; circular = true)\nkdata = fill(samples, 1, 1, 1)\nacqData = AcquisitionData(tr, kdata; encodingSize = (N, N))\n\nencodingSize is the size of the image grid as a tuple. It cannot be derived from the nodes, so always pass it; the reconstruction uses it as the default image size. The field of view can be passed in the same way, in millimeters and as a tuple of three Float64 numbers, for example fov = (220.0, 220.0, 5.0). For a 3D trajectory, encodingSize has three entries, and kdata holds the whole volume as a single slice. A direct reconstruction shows whether the arrays were passed correctly:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nimage = reconstruction(acqData, params)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (350, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true,\n          title = \"Direct reconstruction\")\nheatmap!(ax, abs.(image[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])', colormap = :grays)\nhidedecorations!(ax)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nIf the image is mirrored or transposed, the nodes follow another convention: the opposite sign in a row of nodes mirrors the image along the dimension of that row, and swapped rows transpose it.\n\nThe constructors do not check that the sizes of the arrays fit together. A number of samples that differs from the number of nodes makes the reconstruction fail, and a wrong number of profiles goes unnoticed until the profiles or the sampling times are used, for example when the data is converted into raw data. This AcquisitionData constructor also ignores keywords that it does not know, so a misspelled keyword has no effect.","title":"Non-Cartesian Nodes and Samples"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/from_arrays/#Several-Contrasts,-Slices-and-Repetitions","page":"Create AcquisitionData from Arrays","text":"For data with several contrasts, slices or repetitions, kdata holds one matrix for each combination. A trajectory belongs to one contrast and is shared by all slices and repetitions. Pass a vector with one trajectory per contrast, even if all contrasts were acquired with the same trajectory: with a single trajectory for several contrasts, the constructor succeeds, but the reconstruction fails, by default with a BoundsError (#349).\n\nCreate a separate Trajectory for each contrast, because some functions, such as changeEncodingSize2D, change the trajectory of each contrast in turn and would change a shared one once per contrast. Multi-contrast data from a Cartesian array is affected by this, because its contrasts share one trajectory (#350).\n\nThe following lines create kdata from an array with the dimensions sample, coil, contrast and slice, which here repeats the samples from above for two contrasts and three slices:\n\ndata = repeat(samples, 1, 1, 2, 3)\nkdata = [data[:, :, c, s] for c in 1:2, s in 1:3, r in 1:1]\ntrs = [Trajectory(nodes, 200, N; circular = true) for c in 1:2]\nacqData = AcquisitionData(trs, kdata; encodingSize = (N, N))\nsize(reconstruction(acqData, params))\n(128, 128, 3, 2, 1, 1)\n\nThe image has three slices in its third and two contrasts in its fourth dimension.","title":"Several Contrasts, Slices and Repetitions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/from_arrays/#Sampling-Times","page":"Create AcquisitionData from Arrays","text":"A reconstruction with a correction map, for example for an off-resonance correction, uses the sampling times, which the Trajectory constructor sets. By default, each profile starts at time zero and takes 1 ms. With its keywords TE and AQ, as in Trajectory(nodes, 200, N; TE = 0.01, AQ = 0.004), each profile starts at the time TE and takes the time AQ, both in seconds. Alternatively, its keyword times takes a Vector with the time of every sample in seconds, in the precision of the nodes. The correction counts the times from TE, which stays zero unless it is passed as well.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Sampling Times"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/custom_transform/#custom-transform","page":"Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform","text":"The iterative reconstructions can apply the regularization params[:reg] to a transform of the image instead of the image itself, as described in Reconstruction as an Inverse Problem. params[:sparseTrafo] sets this transform: the name \"Wavelet\", an operator of LinearOperatorCollection.jl or an operator of your own, which LinearOperators.jl helps to build. using MRIReco loads both packages. The example reconstructs a phantom from 48 simulated radial profiles, too few for a good reconstruction without regularization, with ADMM and an ell_1 regularization of the transformed image:\n\nusing MRIReco, MRISimulation, Wavelets, FFTW, CairoMakie\nusing ImageUtils: shepp_logan\n\nN = 128\nimage = shepp_logan(N)\nsimParams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nsimParams[:trajName] = \"Radial\"\nsimParams[:numProfiles] = 48\nsimParams[:numSamplingPerProfile] = 2N\nacqData = simulation(image, simParams)\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"standard\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (N, N)\nparams[:solver] = ADMM\nparams[:vary_rho] = :balance\nparams[:iterations] = 50\nparams[:normalizeReg] = MeasurementBasedNormalization()\nparams[:reg] = L1Regularization(0.01)\nparams[:sparseTrafo] = \"Wavelet\"\nimageWavelet = reconstruction(acqData, params)","title":"Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/custom_transform/#An-Operator-of-LinearOperatorCollection.jl","page":"Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform","text":"The operators of LinearOperatorCollection.jl take the complex element type of the data, ComplexF64 here, and the image size. \"Wavelet\" is WaveletOp with its default wavelet, Daubechies 2 of Wavelets.jl. The Haar wavelet suits the piecewise constant phantom better:\n\nparams[:sparseTrafo] = WaveletOp(ComplexF64; shape = (N, N), wt = wavelet(WT.haar))\nimageHaar = reconstruction(acqData, params)","title":"An Operator of LinearOperatorCollection.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/custom_transform/#An-Operator-of-Your-Own","page":"Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform","text":"The operator takes the image as a vector of length prod(params[:reconSize]). LinearOperator(T, nrows, ncols, symmetric, hermitian, prod!, tprod!, ctprod!) builds one from functions that compute the product and the product with the adjoint in place. As an example, the 2D discrete cosine transform of FFTW.jl gives the same result as DCTOp of LinearOperatorCollection.jl:\n\nfunction dctOperator(N)\n    prod!(res, x) = (res .= vec(dct(reshape(x, N, N))))\n    ctprod!(res, y) = (res .= vec(idct(reshape(y, N, N))))\n    return LinearOperator(ComplexF64, N * N, N * N, false, false, prod!, nothing, ctprod!)\nend\n\nparams[:sparseTrafo] = dctOperator(N)\nimageDCT = reconstruction(acqData, params)\nparams[:sparseTrafo] = DCTOp(ComplexF64; shape = (N, N))\nreconstruction(acqData, params) ≈ imageDCT\ntrue","title":"An Operator of Your Own"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/howto/custom_transform/#Transforms-That-Are-Not-Orthonormal","page":"Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform","text":"MRIReco.jl combines the regularization term with the transform in a TransformedRegularization of RegularizedLeastSquares.jl. Its proximal map applies the one of the term to the coefficients and transforms the result back with the adjoint. This is correct for orthonormal transforms, whose adjoint is their inverse, such as the wavelets and the DCT above, but not for others. With the finite differences of GradientOp as params[:sparseTrafo], the reconstruction above deviates more from the phantom than without regularization, and with FISTA, or with ADMM without vary_rho = :balance, it diverges. Total variation, which penalizes these differences, is available as the regularization term TVRegularization and needs no transform. As in Iterative Reconstruction, it gets more inner iterations than by default:\n\ndelete!(params, :sparseTrafo)\nparams[:reg] = TVRegularization(0.01, shape = (N, N), iterationsTV = 50)\nimageTV = reconstruction(acqData, params)\n\nThe figure compares the four regularizations by the normalized root mean squared deviation from the phantom. Since the phantom is piecewise constant, the Haar wavelet and the finite differences of total variation represent it with few coefficients, and the cosines of the DCT with many, so that the DCT does not improve this reconstruction:\n\ndeviation(x) = round(nrmsd(image, abs.(x[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])), sigdigits = 2)\nimages = [imageWavelet => \"Daubechies 2\", imageHaar => \"Haar\", imageDCT => \"DCT\",\n          imageTV => \"Total variation\"]\nfig = Figure(size = (940, 270))\nfor (i, (x, title)) in enumerate(images)\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = \"$title, NRMSD $(deviation(x))\", aspect = DataAspect(), yreversed = true)\n    heatmap!(ax, abs.(x[:, :, 1, 1, 1, 1])', colormap = :grays)\n    hidedecorations!(ax)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Transforms That Are Not Orthonormal"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators","page":"MRIOperators","text":"MRIOperators implements the encoding operators used in MRI reconstruction: Fourier encoding with and without field inhomogeneity correction, coil sensitivities and sparsifying transforms. It builds on LinearOperatorCollection.jl and re-exports its operators, such as FFTOp, NFFTOp, WaveletOp, DiagOp and normalOperator, which are documented there. MRIOperators depends on MRIBase. MRIReco re-exports MRIOperators, so using MRIReco makes all names on this page available.","title":"MRIOperators"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIOperators/#Encoding-Operators","page":"MRIOperators","text":"","title":"Encoding Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIOperators/#Operator-Types","page":"MRIOperators","text":"","title":"Operator Types"},{"category":"section","location":"API/MRIOperators/#Sparsifying-Transforms","page":"MRIOperators","text":"Use a Custom Sparsifying Transform shows how to use these and other transforms in a reconstruction.","title":"Sparsifying Transforms"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.encodingOp_multiEcho-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"encodingOp_multiEcho(acqData::AcquisitionData{T,D}, shape::NTuple{D,Int64}; kargs...) where {T,D}\n\ngenerates a LinearOperator which describe combined signal encoding of all the contrasts in an MRI acquisition (for a given slice).\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData            - AcquisitionData object\nshape::NTuple{D,Int64}              - size of image to be encoded/reconstructed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.encodingOp_multiEcho"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.encodingOp_multiEcho_parallel-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}, AbstractArray{Complex{T}}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"encodingOp_multiEcho_parallel(acqData::AcquisitionData{T,D}, shape::NTuple{D,Int64}\n                                      , senseMaps::Array{Complex{T}}\n                                      ; kargs...) where {T,D}\n\ngenerates a LinearOperator which describe combined signal encoding of all the contrasts in an MRI acquisition (for a given slice). The different coils are taken into account in terms of their sensitivities\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData{T,D}       - AcquisitionData object\nshape::NTuple{2,Int64}              - size of image to be encoded/reconstructed\nsenseMaps::Array{Complex{T}}        - coil sensitivities\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.encodingOp_multiEcho_parallel"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.encodingOps_parallel-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}, AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"encodingOps_parallel(acqData::AcquisitionData{T,D}, shape::NTuple{D,Int64}\n                          , senseMaps::Array{Complex{T}}\n                          ; kargs...) where {T,D}\n\ngenerates an Array of LinearOperators which describe signal encoding of the individual contrasts in an MRI acquisition. The different coils are taken into account in terms of their sensitivities\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData{T,D}       - AcquisitionData object\nshape::NTuple{D,Int64}              - size of image to be encoded/reconstructed\nsenseMaps::Array{Complex{T}}        - coil sensitivities\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.encodingOps_parallel"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.encodingOps_simple-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AcquisitionData{T, D}, NTuple{D, Int64}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"encodingOps_simple(acqData::AcquisitionData, shape::NTuple{D,Int64}\n                          ; kargs...) where D\n\ngenerates an Array of LinearOperators which describe signal encoding of the individual contrasts in an MRI acquisition (for a given slice).\n\nArguments\n\nacqData::AcquisitionData            - AcquisitionData object\nshape::NTuple{D,Int64}              - size of image to be encoded/reconstructed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.encodingOps_simple"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.fourierEncodingOp-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Int64}, Trajectory{T}, String}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"return Fourier encoding operator (either Explicit or NFFT)\n\nopname : \"explicit\" or \"fast\"\nslice : slice to which the operator will be applied\n\nechoImage : calculate signal evolution relative to the echo time\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.fourierEncodingOp"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.ExplicitOp-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{Tc}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Int64}, Trajectory{T}, AbstractArray{Tc, D}}} where {T, Tc<:Union{Complex{T}, T}, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"ExplicitOp(shape::NTuple{D,Int64}, tr::Trajectory, correctionmap::Array{ComplexF64,D}\n        ; echoImage::Bool=false, kargs...) where D\n\ngenerates a ExplicitOp which explicitely evaluates the MRI Fourier signal encoding operator.\n\nArguments:\n\nshape::NTuple{D,Int64}             - size of image to encode/reconstruct\ntr::Trajectory                     - Trajectory with the kspace nodes to sample\ncorrectionmap::Array{ComplexF64,D} - fieldmap for the correction of off-resonance effects\nechoImage::Bool=false              - if true sampling times will only be considered relative to the echo time                                        this results in complex valued image even for real-valued input.\nkargs                              - additional keyword arguments\n\nMoving to another array backend\n\nAdapt.adapt(CuArray, op::ExplicitOp)\n\nreturns a new ExplicitOp built from Adapt.adapt(CuArray, ...) of the k-space nodes, readout times and correction map of op, with the same shape and echo time offset; the trajectory is not used again. adjoint(op) is an AdjointLinearOperator with parent op, and adapting it adapts that parent. On the same backend, the arrays may be shared with op and are only read. op itself is not modified. SensitivityOp, SubspaceOp, MapSliceOp, ExplicitOp and FieldmapNFFTOp are the MRI operators that support Adapt.adapt so far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.ExplicitOp"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.FieldmapNFFTOp-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Int64}, Trajectory, Array{Complex{T}, D}}} where {T, D}","page":"MRIOperators","text":"FieldmapNFFTOp(shape::NTuple{D,Int64}, tr::Trajectory,\n                    correctionmap::Array{Complex{T},D};\n                    method::String=\"nfft\",\n                    echoImage::Bool=true,\n                    alpha::Float64=1.25,\n                    m::Float64=2.0,\n                    K=20,\n                    K_tol::Float64=1.e-3,\n                    step::Int64=10,\n                    S = Vector{Complex{T}},\n                    scheduler = DynamicScheduler(),\n                    kargs...) where {T,D}\n\ngenerates a FieldmapNFFTOp for two-dimensional images, which evaluates the MRI Fourier signal encoding operator, including off-resonance and relaxation, as a weighted sum of NFFTs.\n\nArguments:\n\nshape::NTuple{D,Int64}             - size of image to encode/reconstruct\ntr::Trajectory                     - Trajectory with the kspace nodes to sample\ncorrectionmap::Array{Complex{T},D} - complex correction map z with the relaxation rate R₂* in 1/s as its real                                        part and the off-resonance frequency in rad/s as its imaginary part\n(method::String=\"nfft\")            - approximation of exp(-z t), where t is the sampling time, for a map                                        that is not spatially constant and sampling times that are not all equal:                                        \"nfft\", \"leastsquare\", \"hist\", which works for a map with only an                                        off-resonance part, or \"svd\", which works for a double-precision map with                                        a spatially varying off-resonance part; see                                        Correct Off-Resonance\n(echoImage::Bool=true)             - if true, the sampling times are counted from the echo time TE of the                                        trajectory, so the image is the magnetization at that time, which with                                        off-resonance is complex-valued even for a real-valued object\n(alpha::Float64=1.25)              - oversampling factor of the \"nfft\" approximation\n(m::Float64=2.0)                   - kernel size of the \"nfft\" approximation\n(K=20)                             - number of terms of \"leastsquare\" and \"hist\" and maximum number of terms                                        of \"svd\"; \"nfft\" determines the number itself\n(K_tol::Float64=1.e-3)             - tolerance with which \"svd\" chooses the number of terms\n(step::Int64=10)                   - \"svd\" takes its functions of time from the first profile, fits its                                        coefficients to every step-th sample of it and uses the functions for                                        all profiles, so it works for profiles with the same sampling times and                                        about step or more samples per function\n(S = Vector{Complex{T}})           - storage vector type, for example CuVector{ComplexF32} on a GPU\n(scheduler = DynamicScheduler())   - scheduler of the parallel loop over the terms\n(kargs)                            - further keyword arguments, which are ignored, so NFFT options such as                                        oversamplingFactor have no effect\n\nMoving to another array backend\n\nAdapt.adapt(CuArray, op::FieldmapNFFTOp)\n\nreturns a new FieldmapNFFTOp with the approximation coefficients of op moved by Adapt.adapt(CuArray, ...), so they are not computed again. Its NFFT plans and work arrays are new: the plans are built from k-space nodes kept on the CPU, with the NFFT backend that was active when op was constructed. op itself is not modified. The backend may choose other plan defaults on the new device (NonuniformFFTs uses another window on CUDA), so the results can differ from those of op within the NFFT accuracy. With JLArrays the operator was tested with the NFFT.jl backend only; NonuniformFFTs does not create plans for them. SensitivityOp, SubspaceOp, MapSliceOp, ExplicitOp and FieldmapNFFTOp are the MRI operators that support Adapt.adapt so far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.FieldmapNFFTOp"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.SensitivityOp","page":"MRIOperators","text":"SensitivityOp(sensMaps::AbstractMatrix{T}, numContr=1)\n\nbuilds a LinearOperator which performs multiplication of a given image with the coil sensitivities specified in sensMaps\n\nArguments\n\nsensMaps    - sensitivity maps ( 1. dim -> voxels, 2. dim-> coils)\nnumContr    - number of contrasts to which the operator will be applied\n\nThe forward operation uses sensMaps itself, while the adjoint uses conj.(sensMaps) computed at construction.\n\nMoving to another array backend\n\nAdapt.adapt(CuArray, op::SensitivityOp)\n\nreturns a new SensitivityOp with a new parent operator, built from Adapt.adapt(CuArray, ...) of the sensitivity maps and of their conjugates, so it applies the same forward and adjoint operation as op at the time of the call. In particular, the adjoint keeps using the conjugated maps computed when op was built. On the same backend, the maps may be shared with op and are only read. op itself is not modified. SensitivityOp, SubspaceOp, MapSliceOp, ExplicitOp and FieldmapNFFTOp are the MRI operators that support Adapt.adapt so far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.SensitivityOp"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.SensitivityOp-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 4}}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 4}, Any}} where T","page":"MRIOperators","text":"SensitivityOp(sensMaps::AbstractArray{T,4}, numContr=1)\n\nbuilds a LinearOperator which performs multiplication of a given image with the coil sensitivities specified in sensMaps\n\nArguments\n\nsensMaps  - sensitivity maps ( 1.-3. dim -> voxels, 4. dim-> coils)\nnumContr  - number of contrasts to which the operator will be applied\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.SensitivityOp"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.SubspaceOp","page":"MRIOperators","text":"SubspaceOp(basis::AbstractMatrix{T},shape::NTuple{D,Int64},numContr) where {T,D}\n\nbasis correspond to svd_object.V cropped to a certain level\n\nbasis type should correspond to the type of the rawdata\n\nMoving to another array backend\n\nAdapt.adapt(CuArray, op::SubspaceOp)\n\nreturns a new SubspaceOp with a new parent operator, built from Adapt.adapt(CuArray, basis). Its storage type S is derived from the adapted basis. On the same backend, the basis may be shared with op and is only read. op itself is not modified. SensitivityOp, SubspaceOp, MapSliceOp, ExplicitOp and FieldmapNFFTOp are the MRI operators that support Adapt.adapt so far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.SubspaceOp"},{"category":"type","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.MapSliceOp","page":"MRIOperators","text":"MapSliceOp(trafo, dim::Int64, size1::Tuple, size2::Tuple; T=ComplexF64)\n\ngenerates an operator that applies trafo to each slice of dimension dim of an array\n\nArguments\n\ntrafo           - transformation to apply\nsize1           - size of the Array to be transformed\nsize2           - size of the resulting Array of applying trafo to all slices\n(T=ComplexF64)  - type of the transformation\n\nMoving to another array backend\n\nAdapt.adapt(CuArray, op::MapSliceOp)\n\nreturns a new MapSliceOp with a new parent operator, built from Adapt.adapt(CuArray, trafo) and the same dim, size1, size2 and T. An operator trafo is adapted by its own Adapt rule, such as those of LinearOperatorCollection; an operator without one is kept unchanged. On the same backend, an array trafo may be shared with op and is only read; an operator trafo kept unchanged is shared with op, including any internal state it updates when applied. op itself is not modified. SensitivityOp, SubspaceOp, MapSliceOp, ExplicitOp and FieldmapNFFTOp are the MRI operators that support Adapt.adapt so far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.MapSliceOp"},{"category":"method","location":"API/MRIOperators/#MRIOperators.SparseOp-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{Type, AbstractString, NTuple{N, Int64}}} where N","page":"MRIOperators","text":"SparseOp(type::T,name::AbstractString, shape::NTuple{N,Int64}; kargs...) where{N,T}\n\ngenerates the sparsifying transform (<: AbstractLinearOperator) given its name.\n\nArguments\n\nname::AbstractString    - name of the sparsifying transform\nshape::NTuple{D,Int64}  - size of the Array to be transformed\n(kargs)                 - additional keyword arguments\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MRIOperators.SparseOp"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/explanations/data_model/#data-model","page":"Data Model","text":"MRIReco.jl represents measured MRI data with a few data types, which are defined in MRIBase and shared by all packages of the framework. This page explains what these types contain, how data flows between them and how k-space data and images are laid out.\n\n(Image: Data flow: MRIFiles reads a raw data file into RawAcquisitionData and writes it back. RawAcquisitionData is converted into AcquisitionData and back. AcquisitionData can also be created by simulation with MRISimulation. The function reconstruction from MRIReco turns AcquisitionData and a dictionary of parameters into an image stored as an AxisArray.)\n\nRaw data is read from a file into a RawAcquisitionData object and converted into an AcquisitionData object, which the reconstruction function turns into an image. AcquisitionData can also be created by simulation. In the diagram, functions are drawn with round ends, and arrows with two heads mark conversions that work in both directions.\n\nThe examples on this page use one slice of a knee dataset from mridata.org, which belongs to the documentation data of MRIReco.jl and is downloaded from Zenodo on first use.\n\nusing MRIReco, MRIFiles\n\nusing Pkg.Artifacts\nartifacts = joinpath(pkgdir(MRIReco), \"docs\", \"Artifacts.toml\")\ndatadir = ensure_artifact_installed(\"MRIRecoDocsData\", artifacts; io = devnull)\nfilename = joinpath(datadir, \"knee_3dFSE_slice170.h5\")","title":"Data Model"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/explanations/data_model/#Raw-Data","page":"Data Model","text":"RawAcquisitionData closely follows the ISMRMRD format, an open standard for MRI raw data. It has two fields: params, a dictionary with the information from the XML header of an ISMRMRD file, and profiles, a vector with one Profile for each readout. MRIFiles reads ISMRMRD files and data from Bruker scanners into this type and writes it to ISMRMRD files, as shown in Load and Save Raw Data. Data from other vendors can be converted into ISMRMRD files with the converters of the ISMRMRD project, such as siemens_to_ismrmrd.\n\nrawData = RawAcquisitionData(ISMRMRDFile(filename))\n\nThe XML header is nested, but params stores it flattened, so that each parameter can be accessed directly. The matrix size of the encoded k-space, for example, is stored in the header under encoding/encodedSpace/matrixSize and is available as\n\nrawData.params[\"encodedSize\"]\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 320\n 320\n   1\n\nEach profile holds the samples of one readout. For non-Cartesian acquisitions, its field traj contains their k-space positions. The samples of all coils are stored in its field data, with one row per sample and one column per coil:\n\nprofile = rawData.profiles[1]\nsize(profile.data)\n(320, 8)\n\nThe header of a profile, an AcquisitionHeader, describes the readout. Its encoding counters idx give the position of the readout in the acquisition, such as the phase encoding step, the slice, the contrast and the repetition. Like in ISMRMRD, the counters start at zero. The 320 profiles of the knee data cover the phase encoding steps 0 to 319:\n\nextrema(Int(p.head.idx.kspace_encode_step_1) for p in rawData.profiles)\n(0, 319)\n\nThe header also contains flags that mark special readouts, for example noise measurements or calibration data. The counters and flags are described in the ISMRMRD documentation.","title":"Raw Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/explanations/data_model/#Preprocessed-Data","page":"Data Model","text":"The profiles in a raw data file are not necessarily stored in the order of the k-space positions, and their layout depends on the sequence. For reconstruction, the data is therefore converted into an AcquisitionData object:\n\nacqData = AcquisitionData(rawData)\n\nThe conversion groups the profiles by contrast, slice and repetition, averages the profiles that differ only in their average counter, removes samples that the header marks to be discarded and reverses readouts that were acquired in reverse direction. It also creates the sampling trajectory and places each readout in it by its phase encoding counters, so the readouts of one contrast, slice, repetition and average need different counters. All slices, repetitions and averages have to be stored in the same order, and non-Cartesian readouts also in the order of their phase encoding counters. The other counters, such as phase and set, are not used (#246).\n\nFor non-Cartesian acquisitions that do not meet these conditions, for example because their counters do not give the position of a readout in k-space, params[\"trajectory\"] has to be set to \"custom\". The conversion then takes the k-space positions from the field traj of the profiles, in the order in which they are stored, which currently works only for data with one slice, repetition and average (#331).\n\nSpecial readouts, such as noise measurements, are not filtered out and have to be removed before the conversion with remove_raw_by_flags, as shown in Select and Prepare Profiles. The conversion works in the other direction as well: RawAcquisitionData(::AcquisitionData) creates raw data, for example from simulated data.\n\nAcquisitionData is defined as\n\nmutable struct AcquisitionData{T <: AbstractFloat, D}\n  sequenceInfo::Dict{Symbol,Any}\n  traj::Vector{Trajectory{T}}\n  kdata::Array{Matrix{Complex{T}},3}\n  subsampleIndices::Vector{Vector{Int64}}\n  encodingSize::NTuple{D, Int64}\n  fov::NTuple{3,Float64}\nend\n\nwhere T is the floating point precision and D the number of encoding dimensions, 2 or 3:\n\ntypeof(acqData)\nAcquisitionData{Float32, 2}\n\nThe k-space data kdata is an array of matrices. Its three dimensions are the contrasts (or echoes), the slices and the repetitions. Each matrix holds the data of one contrast, slice and repetition, with one row per k-space sample and one column per coil:\n\nsize(acqData.kdata), size(acqData.kdata[1])\n((1, 1, 1), (102400, 8))\n\nThe knee data has one contrast, slice and repetition, and 320 × 320 = 102400 samples from 8 coils. In 3D data, the samples of all partitions are stored in one matrix, as a single slice. The functions numContrasts, numSlices, numRepetitions and numChannels return these sizes.\n\nsequenceInfo can store additional information on the pulse sequence and is empty for the knee data. encodingSize is the size of the image grid that the data encodes, and the reconstruction uses it as the default image size. It is needed in particular for non-Cartesian trajectories, where the grid size cannot be derived from the trajectory. fov is the size of the encoded field of view in millimeters. It always has three entries, which are zero for data without this information, such as simulated data. For this knee slice, which was taken from a 3D acquisition, the third entry is the extent of the 3D volume in the third dimension:\n\nacqData.encodingSize, acqData.fov\n((320, 320), (160.0, 160.0, 153.600006))","title":"Preprocessed Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/explanations/data_model/#Trajectories-and-Undersampling","page":"Data Model","text":"The field traj of AcquisitionData contains one Trajectory for each contrast. Its most important field is nodes, a matrix with the k-space positions of the samples, with one row per dimension and one column per node. The positions are measured in units of the inverse pixel size of the encoding grid, so that a fully sampled grid covers the interval [-0.5, 0.5) in each dimension. The positions in the field traj of raw data are used as they are, so they have to be in these units as well. kspaceNodes returns nodes:\n\ntr = trajectory(acqData, 1)\nsize(kspaceNodes(tr)), extrema(kspaceNodes(tr))\n((2, 102400), (-0.5f0, 0.496875f0))\n\nThe knee data was acquired on a Cartesian grid. MRIBase also provides constructors for non-Cartesian trajectories, such as radial and spiral trajectories and, in 3D, kooshball trajectories; Create a Trajectory describes them. The following figure compares a Cartesian, a radial and a spiral trajectory:\n\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 320))\nfor (i, (name, numProfiles, numSamples)) in\n        enumerate([(\"Cartesian\", 16, 16), (\"Radial\", 16, 32), (\"Spiral\", 4, 256)])\n    nodes = kspaceNodes(trajectory(Float64, name, numProfiles, numSamples))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = name, aspect = DataAspect(),\n              limits = (-0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5), xlabel = \"kx\", ylabel = \"ky\")\n    scatter!(ax, nodes[1, :], nodes[2, :], markersize = 4)\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFor Cartesian data, subsampleIndices contains, for each contrast, the indices of the trajectory nodes at which data was measured, in the order of the rows of kdata. For fully sampled data, these are all nodes. Undersampling, for example with sample_kspace from MRISampling, keeps the trajectory and removes the samples that are not measured from kdata and subsampleIndices. The indices then form a mask on the Cartesian grid. The following figure shows this mask for regular undersampling, as in SENSE, and for Poisson-disk sampling along the phase encoding direction with a fully sampled calibration region, each with a quarter of the profiles:\n\nusing MRISampling\n\nacqDataRegular = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"regular\")\nacqDataPoisson = sample_kspace(acqData, 4.0, \"poisson\", calsize = 30)\n\nfig = Figure()\nfor (i, (name, data)) in\n        enumerate([\"Regular\" => acqDataRegular, \"Poisson-disk\" => acqDataPoisson])\n    mask = zeros(320, 320)\n    mask[data.subsampleIndices[1]] .= 1\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, i], title = name, width = 320, height = 320,\n              xlabel = \"Sample in the readout\", ylabel = \"Phase encoding step\")\n    heatmap!(ax, mask, colormap = :grays)\nend\nresize_to_layout!(fig)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe reconstruction uses this mask in the encoding operator: after the Fourier transform, a SamplingOp built from subsampleIndices keeps only the measured samples, in the order of the rows of kdata. The operator therefore has one row per measured sample and one column per pixel:\n\nE = encodingOps_simple(acqDataRegular, (320, 320))[1]\nlength(acqDataRegular.subsampleIndices[1]), size(E)\n(25600, (25600, 102400))\n\nNon-Cartesian reconstructions do not use these indices: the NFFT computes the Fourier transform directly at all nodes of the trajectory. Undersampling such data therefore means using a trajectory with fewer nodes. For data from sample_kspace, convertUndersampledData creates this trajectory.\n\nSince traj and subsampleIndices have one entry per contrast, the trajectory and the undersampling pattern can differ between contrasts, but they are shared by all slices and repetitions.","title":"Trajectories and Undersampling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/explanations/data_model/#Images","page":"Data Model","text":"The reconstruction returns the image as an AxisArray from AxisArrays.jl, an array whose dimensions have names and coordinates. It always has six dimensions. Their names and coordinates can be read with the functions of AxisArrays.jl, which has to be added to the environment to be loaded:\n\nparams = Dict{Symbol, Any}()\nparams[:reco] = \"direct\"\nparams[:reconSize] = (320, 320)\nimage = reconstruction(acqData, params)\n\nusing AxisArrays\naxisnames(image)\n(:x, :y, :z, :echos, :coils, :repetitions)\n\nThe first three dimensions are spatial, and for 2D data the slices are stored along z. The fourth dimension, echos, holds the contrasts, or the basis coefficients of a subspace reconstruction. The fifth dimension holds the coils and the sixth the repetitions. The direct reconstruction used here reconstructs each coil separately, so the coil dimension has length 8. Reconstructions that combine the coils with coil sensitivities return a single image in this dimension. The spatial axes have coordinates in millimeters, computed from fov and encodingSize:\n\naxisvalues(image)[1]\n(0.0:0.5:159.5) mm\n\nparent(image) returns the underlying array. MRIReco does not write image files itself; images can be stored with other packages, for example in the NIfTI format with saveImage from ImageUtils.jl.","title":"Images"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/explanations/data_model/#Array-Layouts","page":"Data Model","text":"The order of the dimensions differs between the data types, which matters when data is passed between them:\n\nData Dimensions\nkdata of AcquisitionData contrast, slice, repetition; each matrix: sample, coil\nk-space array of kDataCart x, y, z or slice, coil, contrast, repetition\nreconstructed image x, y, z or slice, contrast, coil, repetition\ncoil sensitivities of espirit x, y, z or slice, coil\n\nA k-space array in the layout of kDataCart can also be converted into AcquisitionData; if its third dimension holds the slices of a 2D acquisition, the keyword enc2D = true is needed. Create AcquisitionData from Arrays shows how to do this and how to create AcquisitionData from the nodes and samples of a non-Cartesian acquisition. Note that the coil and contrast dimensions are in a different order in k-space arrays and in images:\n\nsize(kDataCart(acqData)), size(image)\n((320, 320, 1, 8, 1, 1), (320, 320, 1, 1, 8, 1))\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Array Layouts"}]
}
